window.MAPLE_SEARCH=[{"p":"Licence","h":"Licence","u":"license.html","x":"Licence Copyright (c) 2026 Kinn Coelho Juliao <kinncj@gmail.com>. All rights reserved. MAPLE HARNESS — BINARY LICENCE (FREE TO USE, CLOSED SOURCE) 1. Use of the binaries. Permission is granted, free of charge, to any person or organisation to download, install and run the compiled Maple Harness programs (maple-harness,","m":"both"},{"p":"Alerts and forecasts","h":"Alerts and forecasts","u":"alerts-and-forecasts.html","x":"The proxy can warn you before a budget runs out, and estimate when it will at the rate you have been using it. Both read the usage log on your own disk. Nothing here changes a request: an alert only warns, and a forecast is an estimate. If you have not set a budget, there is nothing to do and nothing changes: no alerts","m":"both"},{"p":"Alerts and forecasts","h":"In plain words","u":"alerts-and-forecasts.html#in-plain-words","x":"An alert is a warning: \"this budget is 80% used\", \"a quarter of the last hour's requests failed\". A forecast is the proxy's estimate of when a budget runs out at the rate you have been using it. Neither stops anything; a budget is what stops spending. On the setup page they live in two places: Costs & budgets (in the W","m":"both"},{"p":"Alerts and forecasts","h":"What fires by default","u":"alerts-and-forecasts.html#what-fires-by-default","x":"For each budget you set (`budgets:` in `config.yaml`, or on the setup page), the proxy warns twice a day at most: once when that provider has used 80% of the day's budget, and once at 100%. Days are UTC days, the same days the budget counts. A monthly dollar budget warns the same way at 80% and 100% of its month, with ","m":"both"},{"p":"Alerts and forecasts","h":"Set the rules on the setup page","u":"alerts-and-forecasts.html#set-the-rules-on-the-setup-page","x":"Monitoring , step 7 Alerts , then Alert rules . Each field is one key; an empty field turns it off: Field Key Budget warnings (%) `budget_fractions`, as percentages: `80, 100`. Empty turns budget alerts off Quota warnings (%) `quota_fractions` Daily AI units `daily_spend_ai_units` Daily cost (USD, est.) `daily_spend_co","m":"both"},{"p":"Alerts and forecasts","h":"Other rules","u":"alerts-and-forecasts.html#other-rules","x":"All off unless you set them, on the page or in `config.yaml`: A provider's quota is what the provider says about your account (for example a monthly allowance of premium requests). Each level fires once per reset period. With `quota_fractions` set, the proxy asks the provider about the account once a minute, the same q","m":"both"},{"p":"Alerts and forecasts","h":"Where you see them","u":"alerts-and-forecasts.html#where-you-see-them","x":"In Maple Harness: one warning line in the conversation, the first time an alert comes back from the proxy. It is shown once, never added to what the model is sent, and only appears when an alert has fired. On your phone or desktop: if you have notifications set up, an alert is sent once, with the project's name. Leave ","m":"both"},{"p":"Alerts and forecasts","h":"Who gets which alert","u":"alerts-and-forecasts.html#who-gets-which-alert","x":"On your own machine, without a token, you get every alert, as before. With people set up, a person using their own token gets only the alerts about their own budget (at the same levels, `budget_fractions`, for their daily and monthly limits), with their own figures and nobody else's; never a provider's budget, quota, s","m":"both"},{"p":"Alerts and forecasts","h":"Send alerts to Slack, Teams or a webhook","u":"alerts-and-forecasts.html#send-alerts-to-slack-teams-or-a-webhook","x":"Off unless you add one. A webhook is told of each alert as it fires (not when it ends), once. It is sent the alert's text, level, rule, id and time, and nothing else: never your requests, prompts or usage rows. Who gets what follows the same rules as the header: a webhook for the admin (the default) gets every alert; a","m":"both"},{"p":"Alerts and forecasts","h":"Slack","u":"alerts-and-forecasts.html#slack","x":"Create an incoming webhook for the channel (an address starting `https://hooks.slack.com/services/`) and add it with `--format slack`. The message is one line, for example: Maple warning: openai has used 80% of today's budget (80 of 100 requests); it resets at 00:00 UTC (budget.80)","m":"both"},{"p":"Alerts and forecasts","h":"Microsoft Teams","u":"alerts-and-forecasts.html#microsoft-teams","x":"In the channel, add a Workflows webhook (\"Post to a channel when a webhook request is received\") and add the address it gives you with `--format teams`. Maple sends an Adaptive Card with the same line, and the alert's id and time beneath it.","m":"both"},{"p":"Alerts and forecasts","h":"Any other endpoint (JSON)","u":"alerts-and-forecasts.html#any-other-endpoint-json","x":"With `--format json` (the default) the body is: A test message has `\"rule\": \"test\"` and `\"test\": true`.","m":"both"},{"p":"Alerts and forecasts","h":"In config.yaml","u":"alerts-and-forecasts.html#in-configyaml","x":"The page and the CLI write this for you; the address and the secret stay in the secret store. `url` is always a reference: an address written here is refused.","m":"both"},{"p":"Alerts and forecasts","h":"Check the signature","u":"alerts-and-forecasts.html#check-the-signature","x":"With a signing secret, each request carries two headers: `X-Maple-Timestamp` (Unix seconds) and `X-Maple-Signature`, which is `v1=` and the hex HMAC-SHA256, keyed with your secret, of the timestamp, a dot, and the raw body. Check it on the raw body before parsing, and refuse an old timestamp so a request cannot be repl","m":"both"},{"p":"Alerts and forecasts","h":"Where it may be sent","u":"alerts-and-forecasts.html#where-it-may-be-sent","x":"https only. Plain http only to this machine, and only with `allow_http_loopback: true` (with `allow_private: true`), for a local relay. Not to your own network (this machine, private ranges) unless the webhook says `allow_private: true`. Link-local and cloud-metadata addresses are refused whatever it says. The check is","m":"both"},{"p":"Alerts and forecasts","h":"Forecasts","u":"alerts-and-forecasts.html#forecasts","x":"A forecast takes the average of what you spent on each of the last seven complete UTC days, with the 25th and 75th percentile days as the range, and sets it against what is left. Today does not count until it is over. With fewer than three days of usage there is no forecast at all. It answers in words such as: At this ","m":"both"},{"p":"Alerts and forecasts","h":"When you stop using it","u":"alerts-and-forecasts.html#when-you-stop-using-it","x":"Going back to a release before alerts: the `alerts:` block is ignored, and the alert lines already in the events log stay readable.","m":"both"},{"p":"Alerts and forecasts","h":"Related","u":"alerts-and-forecasts.html#related","x":"Proxy settings · Monthly dollar budgets · See and filter your usage · Your usage data","m":"both"},{"p":"Amazon Bedrock","h":"Amazon Bedrock","u":"bedrock.html","x":"Use the models in your company's AWS account through Amazon Bedrock: Claude, Amazon Nova, Llama, Mistral and the others Bedrock offers. Requests are signed with your own AWS credentials and billed to your AWS account under your AWS agreement. Maple stores no AWS key: on a laptop it uses your AWS profile or SSO sign-in,","m":"both"},{"p":"Amazon Bedrock","h":"In plain words","u":"bedrock.html#in-plain-words","x":"What it is. Claude, Amazon Nova, Llama and the other models in your company's AWS account, through Amazon Bedrock, billed to that account. Why you want it. No new key: Maple signs each request with the AWS sign-in you already have (a profile, SSO, or the pod's role on Kubernetes). What to do. Sign in to AWS as usual, t","m":"both"},{"p":"Amazon Bedrock","h":"On a laptop","u":"bedrock.html#on-a-laptop","x":"Sign in to AWS the way you already do, then add Bedrock to the proxy: `bedrock models` lists the text models and inference profiles the region offers your account, and which Bedrock API the proxy uses for each. `bedrock add` checks the models you name against that list and writes the provider into `config.yaml` (a runn","m":"both"},{"p":"Amazon Bedrock","h":"Where the credentials come from","u":"bedrock.html#where-the-credentials-come-from","x":"The proxy uses the AWS SDK's own credential chain, in its usual order, and the SDK refreshes temporary credentials before they expire: Environment variables: `AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID`, `AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY`, `AWS_SESSION_TOKEN`, or `AWS_PROFILE`. The shared files `~/.aws/config` and `~/.aws/credentials`: a profile (`bedroc","m":"both"},{"p":"Amazon Bedrock","h":"Permissions","u":"bedrock.html#permissions","x":"The identity needs `bedrock:InvokeModel` and `bedrock:InvokeModelWithResponseStream` on the models (and on the inference profiles you use), plus `bedrock:ListFoundationModels` and `bedrock:ListInferenceProfiles` for `bedrock models` and `bedrock add`.","m":"both"},{"p":"Amazon Bedrock","h":"Model access","u":"bedrock.html#model-access","x":"A model must be available to your AWS account in the region before it answers. Some model providers ask for a one-time use-case form or an AWS Marketplace subscription first; open the Amazon Bedrock console in that region to check. Until then, requests fail as not authorised , and the message names the model, the regio","m":"both"},{"p":"Amazon Bedrock","h":"Regions and inference profiles","u":"bedrock.html#regions-and-inference-profiles","x":"One provider is one region with one set of credentials: an account in Maple's sense. Several regions are several providers: This writes `bedrock-us-east-1` and `bedrock-us-west-2` and chains them with \u00002\u0000 : a throttled region (HTTP 429), a spent service quota or an outage moves the request to the next one. The provider","m":"both"},{"p":"Amazon Bedrock","h":"Which Bedrock API","u":"bedrock.html#which-bedrock-api","x":"Claude Opus 4.7 and later go through Bedrock's Messages API endpoint (`bedrock-mantle`), which speaks Anthropic's Messages format; every other model, earlier Claude models included, goes through Bedrock's Converse API. Both stream, take tools and images, and use prompt caching where the model supports it (cache points ","m":"both"},{"p":"Amazon Bedrock","h":"On Kubernetes (EKS)","u":"bedrock.html#on-kubernetes-eks","x":"The Kubernetes samples include a component for IAM roles for service accounts in `config/cloud-identity/eks/`. Add it to your overlay: Create an IAM role whose trust policy allows the cluster's OIDC provider for `system:serviceaccount:maple:maple-proxy`, with the Bedrock permissions above. Put the role's ARN in `config","m":"both"},{"p":"Amazon Bedrock","h":"Cost and usage","u":"bedrock.html#cost-and-usage","x":"Bedrock does not state a price on its replies, so the proxy records the tokens each call used (input, cached input and output) and no price; your AWS bill is the authority. For Claude models on the Messages API, `estimate_cost: true` on the provider adds an estimate from Anthropic's list prices, labelled as an estimate","m":"both"},{"p":"Measure with bench","h":"Measure with bench","u":"bench.html","x":"`maple-harness bench` answers one question on your own work: does a setting change what a task costs, and does the result stay as good? It replays the same tasks under different settings (\"arms\"), several times each, in fresh directories, and compares the arms task by task with the uncertainty shown. It is an advanced ","m":"single"},{"p":"Measure with bench","h":"In plain words","u":"bench.html#in-plain-words","x":"What it is. A command that runs the same tasks several times with and without a setting, and compares what they cost and whether the result stayed as good. What to do. Try it with `--dry-run` first (it spends nothing), then set a budget and run it for real. There is no setup page step.","m":"single"},{"p":"Measure with bench","h":"Try it without spending","u":"bench.html#try-it-without-spending","x":"With no `--yes` the command prints the plan (how many runs, in what arms) and an estimate of the cost from earlier runs of the same suite, or says the cost is unknown. Nothing is run and nothing is spent.","m":"single"},{"p":"Measure with bench","h":"Run it for real","u":"bench.html#run-it-for-real","x":"`--k 3` runs every task three times under every arm. Three is the least that says anything about variation. `--budget-ai-units` (or `--budget-tokens`) is required with `--yes`. The bench stops before the run that would pass it, judged by the average cost so far. One run that costs far more than the average can still pa","m":"single"},{"p":"Measure with bench","h":"Write your own tasks","u":"bench.html#write-your-own-tasks","x":"A suite is a folder: `task.yaml`: Instead of `files/` a task can name a repository and a commit to check out (`repo:` and `commit:`). Source files that a build would otherwise pick up as part of its own project can carry a `.tmpl` suffix, which is removed on copy. A task without `verify` has no check, and its quality i","m":"single"},{"p":"Measure with bench","h":"Compare another tool on the same model","u":"bench.html#compare-another-tool-on-the-same-model","x":"An arm can be another program instead of a change to this one. It gets the same tasks, in the same fresh directories, with the same check, so the comparison is like for like. The example below runs GitHub Copilot CLI against a model you host yourself, next to Maple Harness on the same model. No shell. The command is a ","m":"single"},{"p":"Measure with bench","h":"Counting on the model server","u":"bench.html#counting-on-the-model-server","x":"Measured in AI units or the tool's own tokens, two programs cannot be compared, because one may report nothing. If your model server is vLLM, point the bench at its metrics and it reads the server's own counters around every run: Before each run it checks that the server is idle (nothing running or waiting). If it is n","m":"single"},{"p":"Measure with bench","h":"A check for a person, too","u":"bench.html#a-check-for-a-person-too","x":"Tests can pass on something that does not work. A task may add `verify_browser: check.py` (a script in the task folder; `.js` runs with node, `.py` with python, `.sh` with sh). It runs after the tests, gets the working directory and the machine's numeric network address in `MAPLE_BENCH_LAN_IP`, and is typically a Playw","m":"single"},{"p":"Measure with bench","h":"Read the results","u":"bench.html#read-the-results","x":"The report shows, for each arm, the median with a 95% interval; then each arm against the baseline, task by task: the change (negative means it used less), its interval, a p-value, and how many tasks it won, lost and tied; then the pass rate, so that a cheaper arm that fails more is visible; then the total each arm cos","m":"single"},{"p":"Measure with bench","h":"What is recorded","u":"bench.html#what-is-recorded","x":"Each run records tokens, cache reads and writes, AI units, requests, retries, tool calls, wall time, files changed, and whether the check passed. No prompt text, file contents or credentials are recorded, and paths are removed from error messages unless you pass `--anonymize=false`. Related: how to read the cost of a s","m":"single"},{"p":"Chain proxies into hubs","h":"Chain proxies into hubs","u":"chaining.html","x":"One Maple proxy can use another as a provider. The one it uses is its hub . A hub can serve several proxies, and it can use a hub of its own, so a laptop can go to a team hub, and the team hub can go to a company hub. Nothing about this is on by default. A proxy only takes part once you connect it to a hub (by signing ","m":"teams"},{"p":"Chain proxies into hubs","h":"In plain words","u":"chaining.html#in-plain-words","x":"A hub is just another Maple proxy that other proxies use, usually one on a team machine. There are two ways to connect a proxy to a hub, and which one you use depends on who the proxy serves: Your own proxy, on your laptop: sign in with your company account, or paste your personal token. Sign in yourself, in a browser,","m":"teams"},{"p":"Chain proxies into hubs","h":"Join your laptop to a team hub","u":"chaining.html#join-your-laptop-to-a-team-hub","x":"You need the hub's sign-in address (the address you open to sign in to the hub, such as `https://maple-admin.example.com`; ask the hub's admin) and your company account. Then, on your own proxy: Open your setup page, Hubs & chaining , Connect this proxy to a parent hub (until your proxy uses a hub, the tab is under Set","m":"teams"},{"p":"Chain proxies into hubs","h":"Join with a personal token","u":"chaining.html#join-with-a-personal-token","x":"Without single sign-on, the hub's admin makes you a token on the hub ( People & teams , your Access and tokens , Make a token ) and sends it to you with the hub's address; How they connect lists this first. On your own proxy: Open your setup page, Hubs & chaining , Connect this proxy to a parent hub , under Or paste a ","m":"teams"},{"p":"Chain proxies into hubs","h":"Connect a team proxy with a chain token","u":"chaining.html#connect-a-team-proxy-with-a-chain-token","x":"A proxy that serves a team connects with a chain token. It takes two people and two minutes: The hub's admin makes a chain token for your proxy, on the hub's setup page: Hubs & chaining , Let a proxy connect to this hub , type a name for your proxy, Make a chain token . The token is shown once. You connect your proxy ,","m":"teams"},{"p":"Chain proxies into hubs","h":"On the hub: let a proxy connect","u":"chaining.html#on-the-hub-let-a-proxy-connect","x":"Making a chain token for design-laptop on the team hub. In this demo every proxy runs on one machine, so the page also says this hub listens only on this machine: on a real hub, open it to the network first ( Share Maple with a team , step 1). After Make a chain token the page shows the token once, with a ready-made sn","m":"teams"},{"p":"Chain proxies into hubs","h":"On your proxy: connect to the hub","u":"chaining.html#on-your-proxy-connect-to-the-hub","x":"The wizard after Test the connection . The token field never shows the token, and the page never reads it back. Test the connection asks the hub, without saving or binding anything: whether it answers, whether it takes the token, whether the token is a chain token (one that may say who asked) or a person's own token (a","m":"teams"},{"p":"Chain proxies into hubs","h":"The hub map","u":"chaining.html#the-hub-map","x":"The team hub's map: its parent above, the two proxies below it, and each one's last seven days. A demo setup with stand-in model services. Above: the hubs this proxy uses, and whether they answer ( Check the hubs above asks them; nothing leaves your machine otherwise). In the middle: this proxy, with its own and inheri","m":"teams"},{"p":"Chain proxies into hubs","h":"Day to day on the hub","u":"chaining.html#day-to-day-on-the-hub","x":"Each chain token, the proxy it speaks for, and when that proxy last asked. Unbind when the proxy below was replaced: the next proxy to use the token is the one it speaks for. Revoke to cut a proxy off: it can no longer use the hub, within seconds. A proxy below is marked not heard lately after five minutes without aski","m":"teams"},{"p":"Chain proxies into hubs","h":"The technical view","u":"chaining.html#the-technical-view","x":"Everything above can also be done in `config.yaml` and with `maple-proxy` commands, as below, and the page and the commands can be mixed.","m":"teams"},{"p":"Chain proxies into hubs","h":"Personal token or chain token: which do I need?","u":"chaining.html#personal-token-or-chain-token-which-do-i-need","x":"A proxy below a hub holds one of two kinds of credential for it. A personal token is one person's own token. Your proxy gets one when you sign it in with your company account; an admin can also make one for you (People & teams), or you can make one on My access. The hub counts everything the proxy sends as that person.","m":"teams"},{"p":"Chain proxies into hubs","h":"Sign in to a hub with your company account","u":"chaining.html#sign-in-to-a-hub-with-your-company-account","x":"What the plain-words steps do, in detail. It needs a hub that runs Maple 2.2 or later with single sign-on and developer sign-in on, and its sign-in address, which must be `https://` (only the address: no path, query or user).","m":"teams"},{"p":"Chain proxies into hubs","h":"Sign in: on the page or in a terminal","u":"chaining.html#sign-in-on-the-page-or-in-a-terminal","x":"There are three ways in, and they are one and the same sign-in: a sign-in started in one shows in the others, and any of them can end it. Step On your proxy's setup page In a terminal on your proxy's machine Start Hubs & chaining , Connect this proxy to a parent hub , Sign in with your company account : type The hub's ","m":"teams"},{"p":"Chain proxies into hubs","h":"What your proxy keeps, and what the hub sees","u":"chaining.html#what-your-proxy-keeps-and-what-the-hub-sees","x":"On your proxy: a token for you with the `proxy` scope only (it uses the hub's models; it is never a chain token), kept in your proxy's secret store. `config.yaml` gets a `maple` provider for the hub (the name you chose, `hub` by default) at the model address the hub gives, with only a reference to the token. The hub's ","m":"teams"},{"p":"Chain proxies into hubs","h":"Where your requests go","u":"chaining.html#where-your-requests-go","x":"The hub tells your proxy where its models are. When that address is on the hub's sign-in address's own domain over `https://` (`https://maple.acme.example:11535` beside `https://sso.acme.example`, say), your proxy uses it. When it is anywhere else (another domain, plain `http://`, an IP address, this machine), nothing ","m":"teams"},{"p":"Chain proxies into hubs","h":"Status and \"Sign in again\"","u":"chaining.html#status-and-sign-in-again","x":"Your running proxy renews its token quietly before it expires (a token lasts 12 hours), for up to 7 days after you signed in in the browser. A laptop that was off for a night or a weekend renews when it is back, as long as the seven days are not over. Then it stops, and you sign in again: Sign in again on the page, or ","m":"teams"},{"p":"Chain proxies into hubs","h":"Disconnect","u":"chaining.html#disconnect","x":"On the page, Disconnect next to the sign-in. In a terminal: Disconnecting ends the sign-in at the hub (its token is revoked there), forgets it on your proxy, and removes the hub's provider (unless `--keep-provider`). If the hub cannot be reached, it says so: the sign-in is forgotten here, its renewal ends by itself aft","m":"teams"},{"p":"Chain proxies into hubs","h":"When the hub or this proxy cannot do it","u":"chaining.html#when-the-hub-or-this-proxy-cannot-do-it","x":"What you see Why What to do `<host> does not offer sign-in from a proxy (a Maple release before 2.2, or not a Maple hub): paste a token from your admin instead.` The hub runs an older release, or the address is not a Maple hub's sign-in address Ask the hub's admin for a token `<host> has sign-in from a proxy turned off","m":"teams"},{"p":"Chain proxies into hubs","h":"Limits","u":"chaining.html#limits","x":"Not a parent hub. A personal token uses the hub's models as you. It cannot bring the hub's people and teams down or send usage up: that needs a chain token. A hub you signed in to is never this proxy's `chain.parent`, and the page refuses to make it one (paste a chain token to replace the sign-in instead). One proxy, o","m":"teams"},{"p":"Chain proxies into hubs","h":"The two common setups","u":"chaining.html#the-two-common-setups","x":"","m":"teams"},{"p":"Chain proxies into hubs","h":"Your own account first, the hub when it runs out","u":"chaining.html#your-own-account-first-the-hub-when-it-runs-out","x":"The laptop uses its own Copilot account. When that account is out of quota or rate limited, requests go to the hub, and come back to the laptop's account once it answers again. The hub's models are reached as `hub/<model>`, whether or not they are listed under the provider. A hub in the middle of a chain (one with `cha","m":"teams"},{"p":"Chain proxies into hubs","h":"The hub first, your own model when the hub says no","u":"chaining.html#the-hub-first-your-own-model-when-the-hub-says-no","x":"The laptop sends everything to the hub. When the hub refuses (a budget there is spent, the hub is down, or the hub's own accounts are out of quota), a local model answers instead. Each refusal from the hub is named the same way as a refusal from any provider, so the usage log says why the fallback ran: `daily budget sp","m":"teams"},{"p":"Chain proxies into hubs","h":"Set up a hub","u":"chaining.html#set-up-a-hub","x":"On the hub, make a token for each proxy that will use it: The token carries two scopes: `proxy`, to use the hub's models, and `proxy-chain`, which lets the proxy holding it say who asked. The token is shown once. The hub's model surface must be reachable from the other proxy: on another machine that means a listen addr","m":"teams"},{"p":"Chain proxies into hubs","h":"What the wizard writes","u":"chaining.html#what-the-wizard-writes","x":"On the proxy below, Save writes what the hand-written setup above writes: a provider of `type: maple` with the address (`local: true` when the private-network box is ticked), `api_key: ${secret:provider/<name>/api_key}` with the token in the secret store, the models you ticked, `chain.parent` (unless you untick it), `c","m":"teams"},{"p":"Chain proxies into hubs","h":"What the hub map shows","u":"chaining.html#what-the-hub-map-shows","x":"On the map Where it comes from A hub above: answers, latency, checked Check the hubs above asks each `maple` provider's hub, at most once every ten seconds whoever asks; the answers are shown for ten minutes How many hubs further up What the parent's own answer says This proxy Its name and id, its own and inherited peo","m":"teams"},{"p":"Chain proxies into hubs","h":"The proxy-chain scope","u":"chaining.html#the-proxy-chain-scope","x":"Only a token with the `proxy-chain` scope (and the `proxy` scope) is trusted to say who asked. A request with any other token, or none, is recorded as that token's own: headers claiming another person are ignored and removed. A token for everything (all scopes) does not include `proxy-chain`, and a proxy from before 2.","m":"teams"},{"p":"Chain proxies into hubs","h":"Who a hub takes a forwarded request to be from","u":"chaining.html#who-a-hub-takes-a-forwarded-request-to-be-from","x":"One of the hub's own people (defined on the hub, or inherited by it from further up) only when that person signed in below with their own token. The proxy where they signed in sends a proof made from that token with the request, and each hub on the way checks it; the token itself never leaves that proxy. A proxy below ","m":"teams"},{"p":"Chain proxies into hubs","h":"People and teams cascade down","u":"chaining.html#people-and-teams-cascade-down","x":"With `chain.parent` set, a proxy fetches its parent's people, teams and their tokens at start, keeps a copy in its configuration folder, and asks again at once whenever the parent says something changed (the parent holds each request for up to 30 seconds until it has news). `chain.directory_interval` (a minute by defau","m":"teams"},{"p":"Chain proxies into hubs","h":"What each proxy records","u":"chaining.html#what-each-proxy-records","x":"Every proxy a request passes through writes its own line in its usage log. On each line: Column On a laptop's own request On the hub, for a request from the laptop `person`, `team` who asked who asked, at the laptop `proxy_id`, `proxy_name` the laptop the hub `origin_proxy` the laptop the laptop `hops` 0 1 (2 one hub f","m":"teams"},{"p":"Chain proxies into hubs","h":"Usage from below","u":"chaining.html#usage-from-below","x":"With `chain.parent` set, a proxy sends its usage records, and those its own children sent it, to the parent, every `chain.directory_interval`. Only the usage columns are sent, never a prompt or an answer. If the parent is away, the records are sent when it is back, from where the proxy got to; a record already received","m":"teams"},{"p":"Chain proxies into hubs","h":"Limits","u":"chaining.html#limits-1","x":"A request that has passed through more than `chain.max_hops` proxies (4 by default) is refused, and so is one that comes back to a proxy it already passed through. Both are answered with status 508. `chain.max_hops` follows `config.yaml` without a restart. A person's budget is enforced on the proxy where they sign in, ","m":"teams"},{"p":"Chain proxies into hubs","h":"Settings","u":"chaining.html#settings","x":"Setting Default What it does `server.proxy_name` none A readable name for this proxy in usage records and in other proxies' records. `chain.max_hops` 4 The most proxies a request may have passed through before this one (1 to 16). `chain.parent` none The `maple` provider whose hub this proxy inherits people from and sen","m":"teams"},{"p":"Azure and Google Cloud","h":"Azure and Google Cloud","u":"clouds.html","x":"Use the models your company already pays for in its own cloud: Azure OpenAI and Microsoft Foundry (including Claude in Foundry), and Google's Vertex AI, which Google now also calls Agent Platform (Gemini, and Claude on Vertex). Requests are billed to your cloud account under your cloud agreement. Maple stores no cloud ","m":"both"},{"p":"Azure and Google Cloud","h":"In plain words","u":"clouds.html#in-plain-words","x":"What it is. Use the models your company already pays for in Azure (Azure OpenAI, Microsoft Foundry) or Google Cloud (Vertex AI), billed to your cloud account. Why you want it. Your company's agreement, region and data rules apply, and you usually need no new key: on a laptop Maple uses your `az login` or `gcloud` sign-","m":"both"},{"p":"Azure and Google Cloud","h":"Azure OpenAI and Microsoft Foundry","u":"clouds.html#azure-openai-and-microsoft-foundry","x":"One provider is one Azure resource. Its deployments are its models : the model id is the deployment name you chose in the Azure portal or Foundry. On the setup page: Add provider , type Azure OpenAI / Foundry , the resource's endpoint as the base URL, then add the deployment names ( Detect models lists them when the re","m":"both"},{"p":"Azure and Google Cloud","h":"Signing in","u":"clouds.html#signing-in","x":"An API key : put it in the provider's API key field on the setup page (it goes to the secret store, and the configuration keeps a `${secret:...}` reference). Keys are under Keys and Endpoint on the resource in the portal. Microsoft Entra ID : leave the key empty. The proxy asks Entra ID for a token when it needs one, k","m":"both"},{"p":"Azure and Google Cloud","h":"All the settings","u":"clouds.html#all-the-settings","x":"`api_version: v1` uses Azure's versionless `/openai/v1` API. A dated version uses the older `/openai/deployments/<name>/chat/completions?api-version=...` route, for a resource or gateway that needs it. `client_id` works with `managed-identity` and `workload-identity`; with `auto`, set `AZURE_CLIENT_ID` instead.","m":"both"},{"p":"Azure and Google Cloud","h":"Google Vertex AI (Agent Platform)","u":"clouds.html#google-vertex-ai-agent-platform","x":"One provider is one Google Cloud project in one location : a region (`us-east5`, `europe-west4`), a multi-region (`us`, `eu`) or `global`. Models whose id starts with `claude` go to Anthropic's models on Vertex; the rest to Google's Gemini models. Use the model ids from the Model Garden page of each model. On the setup","m":"both"},{"p":"Azure and Google Cloud","h":"Signing in","u":"clouds.html#signing-in-1","x":"The proxy uses Google's application default credentials , found in this order: The file named by `GOOGLE_APPLICATION_CREDENTIALS`: a service account key, or a workload identity federation configuration (for a pod or runner outside Google Cloud). Your own sign-in, on a laptop: The metadata server of the machine or pod i","m":"both"},{"p":"Azure and Google Cloud","h":"Regions and accounts as fallbacks","u":"clouds.html#regions-and-accounts-as-fallbacks","x":"Each provider is an account in Maple's sense, so several regions, projects or resources work like several Copilot accounts: list them in \u00000\u0000 , assign them to teams , and share them through a hub . `vertex-us/gemini-2.5-pro` asks for one region's copy. A plain `gemini-2.5-pro` goes to one of them, and the proxy's log sa","m":"both"},{"p":"Azure and Google Cloud","h":"On Kubernetes","u":"clouds.html#on-kubernetes","x":"The Kubernetes samples include a component for each cloud's workload identity in `config/cloud-identity/`. Add it to your overlay:","m":"both"},{"p":"Azure and Google Cloud","h":"AKS (workload identity)","u":"clouds.html#aks-workload-identity","x":"Enable the OIDC issuer and workload identity on the cluster, and create a user-assigned managed identity with the Cognitive Services OpenAI User role on the resource. Federate it with the proxy's service account, `system:serviceaccount:maple:maple-proxy` (`az identity federated-credential create --issuer <the cluster's","m":"both"},{"p":"Azure and Google Cloud","h":"GKE (workload identity federation)","u":"clouds.html#gke-workload-identity-federation","x":"Enable workload identity federation for GKE on the cluster and its node pools. Grant the proxy's Kubernetes service account the Vertex AI User role, either directly (`principal://iam.googleapis.com/projects/<number>/locations/global/workloadIdentityPools/<project>.svc.id.goog/subject/ns/maple/sa/maple-proxy`) or throug","m":"both"},{"p":"Azure and Google Cloud","h":"Cost and usage","u":"clouds.html#cost-and-usage","x":"Neither cloud states a price on its replies, so the proxy records the tokens each call used (input, cached input and output) and no price. For Claude models, `estimate_cost: true` on the provider adds an estimate from Anthropic's list prices, labelled as an estimate; your cloud bill can differ (commitments, regional pr","m":"both"},{"p":"Commands and keys","h":"Commands and keys","u":"commands.html","x":"Everything you can type in a session, the keys, the modes, and the commands you run from a shell. For the settings in `config.yaml`, see Harness settings ; for the proxy, see Proxy settings . Each item has a Helps tag, because each exists to serve one or more of three goals: Efficient : fewer tokens or AI units for the","m":"both"},{"p":"Commands and keys","h":"In plain words","u":"commands.html#in-plain-words","x":"What it is. The list of everything you can type: slash commands in a session (`/model`, `/cost`, `/remote`), the keys, the modes, and the shell commands of both programs. What to do. `/help` in a session lists the commands; this page says what each does and its trade-off. For the setup page instead, see The proxy and i","m":"both"},{"p":"Commands and keys","h":"Modes and keys","u":"commands.html#modes-and-keys","x":"Permission modes decide what happens when the model wants to write a file or run a command. Mode What happens Helps `ask` (default) You are asked before each write or command. `deny` rules are absolute Efficacious `auto` Writes and commands run without asking; `deny` rules still apply. Unlike `yolo`, it can be chosen w","m":"both"},{"p":"Commands and keys","h":"Slash commands","u":"commands.html#slash-commands","x":"","m":"both"},{"p":"Commands and keys","h":"Session","u":"commands.html#session","x":"Command What it does Helps `/help` Shows the commands and keys `/clear` Starts over: clears the screen and the conversation. Memory notes are kept Efficient `/quit`, `/exit` Exit `/sessions` Lists saved sessions. Resume one with `--session <name>` Effective `/fork [n]` Copies the first `n` messages of this conversation","m":"both"},{"p":"Commands and keys","h":"Models and effort","u":"commands.html#models-and-effort","x":"Command What it does Helps `/model` Opens a list of the models the proxy serves. Bare `/model` also shows what automatic choice would use for each kind of work Effective `/model <name>` Switches straight to that model or alias Effective `/model auto` Chooses a model per message from what your provider serves, within yo","m":"both"},{"p":"Commands and keys","h":"Context and cost","u":"commands.html#context-and-cost","x":"Command What it does Helps `/context` What the model can see: memory notes, the project index, the tools it is offered, the window used, and what the session has spent Efficient `/cost` What the session has spent: totals, the last requests with their cache hit rate (and where a model change or a low hit rate lost the c","m":"both"},{"p":"Commands and keys","h":"Memory, project and tools","u":"commands.html#memory-project-and-tools","x":"Command What it does Helps `/memory [list|show <name>|rm <name>|path]` The agent's notes about the project and you Effective `/graph [stats|reindex|connections <id>|path]` Queries and rebuilds the project index of notes and code symbols the harness looks things up in Efficient `/jobs` Lists background commands started ","m":"both"},{"p":"Commands and keys","h":"Safety","u":"commands.html#safety","x":"Command What it does Helps `/sandbox` How the `bash` tool is contained: the mode, the backend, what is writable and hidden. See The shell sandbox Efficacious `/sandbox auto`, `/sandbox strict`, `/sandbox off` Contains commands when a backend is available; refuses to run them without one; or no containment for this sess","m":"both"},{"p":"Commands and keys","h":"Agents","u":"commands.html#agents","x":"Command What it does Helps `/agents` Manage subagents: list, view, create, edit, delete Effective `/agent <name> <task>`, `@name <task>` Runs a subagent on a task Efficient, Effective","m":"both"},{"p":"Commands and keys","h":"Remote and notifications","u":"commands.html#remote-and-notifications","x":"Command What it does Helps `/remote` Shares this session with a phone or another Maple Harness. See Control it from your phone Effective `/remote pair` Opens a list of addresses, then makes a short pairing code and one QR for the address you pick. `--via`, `--token` and `--fixed` change what it makes; `--watch-only` ma","m":"both"},{"p":"Commands and keys","h":"Agents and the agents tray","u":"commands.html#agents-and-the-agents-tray","x":"Subagents are helper agents you define in `.claude/agents/<name>.md` (or `~/.claude/agents`), or use from the built-in set. The model can hand them jobs, and you can run one yourself with `@name <task>` (`Tab` completes the name). While any run, a tray sits just above the message box: a `main` row and one row per subag","m":"both"},{"p":"Commands and keys","h":"Your own commands and skills","u":"commands.html#your-own-commands-and-skills","x":"`.claude/commands/<name>.md` becomes `/name` (with `$ARGUMENTS`, or `$1` to `$9`, substituted). `.claude/skills/<name>/SKILL.md` becomes a skill, callable with `/name` or chosen by the model. Built-in commands always win over a command or skill with the same name. Effective : your team's routines are one keystroke away","m":"both"},{"p":"Commands and keys","h":"Commands you run from a shell","u":"commands.html#commands-you-run-from-a-shell","x":"`maple-harness` with no command starts the full-screen interface. The short name is `mpl`. On a terminal, commands that print lists or status use tables and your theme's colours, fitted to the window. Piped into another program or a file, the output is plain text with no colour codes and no cut-off cells. `--no-color` ","m":"both"},{"p":"Commands and keys","h":"Command-line flags","u":"commands.html#command-line-flags","x":"Flag What it does `--prompt \"text\"` Runs one turn and exits, printing the answer. Everything else goes to the error stream, so `> out.txt` captures just the answer `--json-summary FILE` With `--prompt`: writes a machine-readable summary of the run (tokens, cache figures, AI units, tool calls, why it stopped) `--continu","m":"both"},{"p":"Harness settings","h":"Harness settings","u":"configuration.html","x":"The harness reads the settings under `harness:` in `config.yaml` (usually `~/.config/maple-harness/config.yaml`). The proxy's settings are in the same file and are covered in the Proxy settings . Changes under `harness:` apply the next time you start the harness; the proxy picks up its own changes without a restart. Ev","m":"both"},{"p":"Harness settings","h":"In plain words","u":"configuration.html#in-plain-words","x":"What it is. Every setting of the agent, the part of `config.yaml` under `harness:`. What to do. Most people never edit it: `/settings` in a session changes the common ones, and `/configuration set <key> <value>` any one. A project can override some of them in its own folder. The proxy's settings are a separate page, an","m":"both"},{"p":"Harness settings","h":"Settings at a glance","u":"configuration.html#settings-at-a-glance","x":"Key Default Helps `default_model` none Effective `compress_with_model` `off` Efficient `compress_model`, `compress_scope` none (chosen for you), `same-account` Efficient, Efficacious `digest_key_points` `on` Effective `auto_model`, `auto_model_map`, `auto_avoid`, `auto_model_resolution`, `auto_first_weight` off, none, ","m":"both"},{"p":"Harness settings","h":"Per-project overrides","u":"configuration.html#per-project-overrides","x":"A project can carry its own `.mapleharness/config.yaml` (or `.mapleharness/config.json`) that changes some of `harness:`'s settings just for that project, on top of your global `config.yaml`. It only needs the keys it changes: Anything the project's file does not mention keeps your global value — setting `loop_guard.mo","m":"both"},{"p":"Harness settings","h":"Model choice","u":"configuration.html#model-choice","x":"Key Default What it does Trade-off Helps `default_model` none The model or alias a session starts on. With none set, it is `gpt-6-sol` when the proxy serves it, else the first model that can use tools. If you name one the proxy does not serve, the harness starts on the first model it does serve that can use tools, and ","m":"both"},{"p":"Harness settings","h":"Long conversations","u":"configuration.html#long-conversations","x":"When a conversation outgrows the model's window, older messages leave the model's view. What leaves it is kept and searchable: Older parts of a long conversation stay searchable. Every message is stored, word for word, beside the saved session before it leaves the model's view, and the model can look any of it up again","m":"both"},{"p":"Harness settings","h":"Permissions and safety","u":"configuration.html#permissions-and-safety","x":"Key Default What it does Trade-off Helps `permission.mode` `ask` The starting mode: `ask`, `auto` or `yolo`. `yolo` is honoured only when you start with `--yolo` Efficacious `permission.rules[].pattern`, `permission.rules[].decision` shipped rules Rules matched in order: `decision` is `allow`, `ask` or `deny`, and `den","m":"both"},{"p":"Harness settings","h":"Runaways and long output","u":"configuration.html#runaways-and-long-output","x":"Key Default What it does Trade-off Helps `loop_guard.mode` `stop` What to do when the same problem keeps failing after different attempts: `off`, `warn` (one note to the model), or `stop` (the note, then the turn ends with a summary for you) `stop` may end a run you would have let continue; `warn` never ends one Effici","m":"both"},{"p":"Harness settings","h":"Cost preview","u":"configuration.html#cost-preview","x":"Off by default, and quiet when on: it is shown only for the messages you asked about, never sent to the model, and never kept in the conversation. Key Default What it does Trade-off Helps `cost_preview` `off` Before a message is sent, shows about what its first request is likely to cost, always labelled estimated , wit","m":"both"},{"p":"Harness settings","h":"Language servers and shell writes","u":"configuration.html#language-servers-and-shell-writes","x":"Key Default What it does Trade-off Helps `lsp` `auto` With no `lsp_servers`, finds language servers from the project's marker files and `PATH`, and adds their errors to the result of each file write or edit, so the model fixes its own mistakes. `off` turns detection, the diagnostics and the lookup tools off The first e","m":"both"},{"p":"Harness settings","h":"Tools, skills and hooks","u":"configuration.html#tools-skills-and-hooks","x":"Key Default What it does Helps `mcp_servers[].name` required The server's name. Its tools are named `mcp__<name>__<tool>`, and a permission rule names one tool, such as `mcp__github__create_issue` (the bare `create_issue` also matches it, on every server) Effective `mcp_servers[].type` inferred `http` or `sse` for a re","m":"both"},{"p":"Harness settings","h":"Subagents and remote control","u":"configuration.html#subagents-and-remote-control","x":"Key Default What it does Trade-off Helps `subagents.max_parallel` `3` How many subagents run at once (1 to 16). More wait for a free slot A local model may not serve several at once Efficient, Effective `subagents.max_turns` `50` The most turns any one subagent may take, whatever its own limit asks for (1 to 200) Effic","m":"both"},{"p":"Harness settings","h":"Notifications","u":"configuration.html#notifications","x":"Messages to your phone or desktop when a session needs approval, stops with an error, or needs your guidance. They say which project and what happened, and nothing else: never a command, a file's content or an error's text. Off until you list a destination. Try it with `maple-harness notify test`. Separately from these","m":"both"},{"p":"Harness settings","h":"Housekeeping and appearance","u":"configuration.html#housekeeping-and-appearance","x":"Key Default What it does Helps `retention.sessions_days` `30` How old a saved session must be before `cleanup` treats it as old Efficient `retention.keep_per_dir` `5` How many recent sessions per directory `cleanup` always keeps Efficient `retention.auto_prune` off Run what `cleanup --yes` would, once a day, when a ses","m":"both"},{"p":"Harness settings","h":"Where the harness finds the proxy","u":"configuration.html#where-the-harness-finds-the-proxy","x":"Key Default What it does `version` `1` The configuration file's format version `server.openai_compat` `127.0.0.1:11535` The proxy's OpenAI-compatible address, which the harness talks to `server.anthropic_compat` `127.0.0.1:11537` The proxy's Anthropic-compatible address `server.webui` `127.0.0.1:11539` The proxy's setu","m":"both"},{"p":"Harness settings","h":"An annotated example","u":"configuration.html#an-annotated-example","x":"Read next: Commands and keys , Proxy settings , and Settings by goal .","m":"both"},{"p":"What /cost tells you","h":"What /cost tells you","u":"cost.html","x":"`/cost` is the session's own bill, read back to you in plain numbers: what has been spent, request by request, and which parts of the conversation are costing the most by being sent again and again. This page walks through each part of its output and, for each thing it can flag, what to actually do about it. It makes n","m":"single"},{"p":"What /cost tells you","h":"In plain words","u":"cost.html#in-plain-words","x":"What it is. `/cost`, typed in a session, is the session's own bill so far: tokens, AI units or an estimated dollar figure, request by request. Why you want it. It points at what costs the most (often something re-sent on every turn) and says what to do about it. What to do. Type `/cost` whenever you wonder. The whole p","m":"single"},{"p":"What /cost tells you","h":"The totals line","u":"cost.html#the-totals-line","x":"The first line is the session so far: input and output tokens, how many requests were billed, and — where the provider states one — AI units (Copilot) or an estimated dollar figure (see Anthropic and estimated cost below; for Grok , the price xAI's reply stated). Under it: the model in use, how many requests were bille","m":"single"},{"p":"What /cost tells you","h":"Subagents","u":"cost.html#subagents","x":"If this session delegated any work with `@agent` or the `delegate` tool, `/cost` ends with a \"Subagents\" line — how many ran, and what they cost on their own — and a \"Session + subagents\" line, which is the true total: the main conversation's own spend plus every subagent's. This is the number that matches what your ac","m":"single"},{"p":"What /cost tells you","h":"The last requests table","u":"cost.html#the-last-requests-table","x":"A row per recent request: input, cached, written and output tokens, AI units, the cache hit rate for that one request, and the model. Two things get flagged automatically: \"model changed\" — a different model answered this request than the one before it. A new model starts with nothing cached, so the whole conversation ","m":"single"},{"p":"What /cost tells you","h":"Where the next request's input goes","u":"cost.html#where-the-next-requests-input-goes","x":"An estimated breakdown of what the next request would send, by kind: the system prompt, the tools on offer, your own messages, anything looked up on your behalf, the model's own replies and tool calls, and tool results. It is a token-count estimate (about four characters to a token), useful for proportions rather than ","m":"single"},{"p":"What /cost tells you","h":"Most expensive tool results","u":"cost.html#most-expensive-tool-results","x":"A tool's result is not paid for once. It stays in the conversation and is sent again on every request after it, until it is trimmed or the session ends — so a single large result from early in a long session can end up costing far more than its own size ever suggested. This section ranks the tool results still in the c","m":"single"},{"p":"What /cost tells you","h":"Anthropic and estimated cost","u":"cost.html#anthropic-and-estimated-cost","x":"Copilot states a price for every call it serves. Claude through Anthropic's own API does not — its responses carry token counts only, never a price. `/cost` reflects that honestly: an Anthropic request shows its tokens like any other, and no price, unless the provider is set up to estimate one. An operator can turn on ","m":"single"},{"p":"What /cost tells you","h":"When Maple can't tell which Claude model a name is","u":"cost.html#when-maple-cant-tell-which-claude-model-a-name-is","x":"The estimate needs to know which Claude model a call went to. Maple reads it from the model's name. A name such as `claude-sonnet-5-5` or `claude-opus-4-6-20260101` is clear, and so is a deployment name that includes the model and its version (`my-sonnet-5-prod`). Some names are not clear: a name that looks like a mode","m":"single"},{"p":"What /cost tells you","h":"Before a large message: the cost preview","u":"cost.html#before-a-large-message-the-cost-preview","x":"With `cost_preview` set to `large` or `on` (see Harness settings ; it is off by default), the harness says, before a message is sent, about what its first request is likely to cost. It looks like this (an illustration of the format, not a measurement): The line goes to the plain output, and the status bar shows a short","m":"single"},{"p":"What /cost tells you","h":"When the output limit goes on reasoning","u":"cost.html#when-the-output-limit-goes-on-reasoning","x":"A reasoning model thinks before it answers, and its thinking counts against the same output limit as the answer. When it uses the whole limit thinking, the reply stops with no answer at all. The proxy says so: Maple Harness shows a warning (\"the output limit was used up by reasoning before any answer; raise max_tokens\"","m":"single"},{"p":"What /cost tells you","h":"Related","u":"cost.html#related","x":"See and reduce what a task costs · Proxy settings · Commands and keys · Settings by goal","m":"single"},{"p":"The Maple data lake (DuckLake)","h":"The Maple data lake (DuckLake)","u":"data-lake.html","x":"The lake is where your usage history goes to stay. The daily files and PostgreSQL keep a window (90 days unless you change it); the lake keeps every record it was given, in a form that is quick to query over months or years, and lets you read it as it was at an earlier point. This page sets it up on one machine and for","m":"both"},{"p":"The Maple data lake (DuckLake)","h":"In plain words","u":"data-lake.html#in-plain-words","x":"What it is. A place for your whole usage history, quick to query over months or years, that you can also read as it was on an earlier day. Why you want it. The daily files keep 90 days by default; the lake keeps everything. The clicks. On the setup page, Data & storage , Usage data , set Analytics files to keep a lake,","m":"both"},{"p":"The Maple data lake (DuckLake)","h":"What DuckLake is","u":"data-lake.html#what-ducklake-is","x":"DuckLake is an open table format from the makers of DuckDB . It has two parts: data files : your usage records as Parquet files, in a folder or in object storage (S3 and compatible); a catalog : a small database that lists those files and every change made to them. On one machine it is a DuckDB file; for a team it is a","m":"both"},{"p":"The Maple data lake (DuckLake)","h":"Before you start","u":"data-lake.html#before-you-start","x":"Install DuckDB's command line, from duckdb.org , and put `duckdb` on your `PATH` (or pass `--duckdb /path/to/duckdb`). These pages were tested with DuckDB 1.5.6. Install the extensions the lake needs, once: The proxy runs `duckdb` with automatic extension downloads turned off, so a missing extension is an error, never ","m":"both"},{"p":"The Maple data lake (DuckLake)","h":"On one machine","u":"data-lake.html#on-one-machine","x":"`maple-proxy usage lake` makes a lake from your daily files, or adds to it what they gained since the last run. By default it lives in `analytics/lake` inside your usage folder: the catalog `maple.ducklake` and a `data` folder, both private to you. See what it would do first; `--dry-run` writes nothing: The next day, o","m":"both"},{"p":"The Maple data lake (DuckLake)","h":"Let the proxy keep it current","u":"data-lake.html#let-the-proxy-keep-it-current","x":"Set Analytics files to Monthly Parquet and a DuckLake on the setup page's Usage data card, or in `config.yaml`: The proxy then refreshes the lake and runs its upkeep when it starts and once a day after the usage cleanup, with your `duckdb` program. It also packs the closed days into monthly Parquet files (`parquet` doe","m":"both"},{"p":"The Maple data lake (DuckLake)","h":"Query it","u":"data-lake.html#query-it","x":"`--emit-sql attach` prints what to run in `duckdb`: it attaches the lake read-only. Inside, `main.usage` and `main.events` hold every record, and the named views (`maple.daily_spend`, `maple.sessions`, `maple.people` and the rest) work as they do over the files. Recipes are in Query your usage .","m":"both"},{"p":"The Maple data lake (DuckLake)","h":"For a team: PostgreSQL and a shared lake","u":"data-lake.html#for-a-team-postgresql-and-a-shared-lake","x":"This section is for central mode : several copies of one proxy sharing PostgreSQL and Redis. A team on one shared proxy with daily files uses the single-machine lake on the hub. PostgreSQL is the live database: every replica writes its usage there, and it keeps `storage.retention_days` of it. The lake keeps the history","m":"both"},{"p":"The Maple data lake (DuckLake)","h":"What the move does","u":"data-lake.html#what-the-move-does","x":"It takes the rows PostgreSQL stored since the last move, in the order PostgreSQL stored them, and only those stored at least an hour before the run (`--settle 1h`), so a write still in progress is never skipped. A line that arrives late with an old time (a hub's backlog from below, a replica that could not reach Postgr","m":"both"},{"p":"The Maple data lake (DuckLake)","h":"Try it by hand first","u":"data-lake.html#try-it-by-hand-first","x":"With `MAPLE_POSTGRES_DSN` holding a connection string (it is read from the environment when the SQL runs, and never written into the SQL). Keep the password out of it, in `PGPASSWORD`: DuckDB can repeat the connection string in an error message. What a first move printed, and a second one right after it: (`--catalog-sc","m":"both"},{"p":"The Maple data lake (DuckLake)","h":"The Kubernetes CronJobs","u":"data-lake.html#the-kubernetes-cronjobs","x":"The Kubernetes samples run the move hourly and the upkeep daily as two CronJobs (`manifest/maple-lake`, and the overlay `config/ha-lake` on top of `config/ha`). They run the official DuckDB image on SQL the proxy writes for you. Generate it, put it in the sample's `sql/` folder, and apply: Then apply the `config/ha-lak","m":"both"},{"p":"The Maple data lake (DuckLake)","h":"Credentials","u":"data-lake.html#credentials","x":"PostgreSQL. The job reads its connection string from `MAPLE_POSTGRES_DSN` (a Secret in the sample); put the password in `PGPASSWORD` from the same Secret rather than in the string. Give the job its own role rather than the proxy's: it needs to read the usage tables and to own the catalog's schema, nothing else. As the ","m":"both"},{"p":"The Maple data lake (DuckLake)","h":"Read-only access for analysts","u":"data-lake.html#read-only-access-for-analysts","x":"Give analysts a role that can only read. For the lake, it needs to read the catalog's schema (and, with `ALTER DEFAULT PRIVILEGES`, the tables the lake adds to it later), and read access to the data files: To also query the live data in PostgreSQL, add `GRANT USAGE ON SCHEMA public TO analyst` and `GRANT SELECT ON mapl","m":"both"},{"p":"The Maple data lake (DuckLake)","h":"Maintenance","u":"data-lake.html#maintenance","x":"Each move adds a little: up to `--inline-rows` rows (10,000 by default) are kept in the catalog itself rather than written as a new Parquet file, so frequent small moves create no small files. Once a day the upkeep: writes the rows kept in the catalog out as Parquet; merges small files towards `--target-file-mb` (32 MB","m":"both"},{"p":"The Maple data lake (DuckLake)","h":"The lake on the setup page","u":"data-lake.html#the-lake-on-the-setup-page","x":"The setup page's Data & storage tab shows the lake as its own jobs last left it: Last move and Rows moved then : when rows last went in, and how many. Rows in the lake , Data files (Parquet files and their size; rows still kept in the catalog are in no file until the upkeep writes them out) and Snapshots . Last upkeep ","m":"both"},{"p":"The Maple data lake (DuckLake)","h":"Time travel and snapshots","u":"data-lake.html#time-travel-and-snapshots","x":"Every load, move and upkeep is a snapshot. List them, then read a table as it was at one: `AT (TIMESTAMP => TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-10-01 00:00:00+00')` reads the lake as it was at a time instead. Use it to reproduce a report exactly as it was first run, or to see what a late batch changed. Snapshots older than `--expire-day","m":"both"},{"p":"The Maple data lake (DuckLake)","h":"Hubs: every proxy's data in one lake","u":"data-lake.html#hubs-every-proxys-data-in-one-lake","x":"A hub keeps the usage the proxies below it send up (see Usage from below ), and its lake takes both: its own lines with `source` `local` and the ones sent up with `source` `child`. On a top hub with two proxies below it: The views that count each request once (`maple.client_requests`, `maple.origins`, `maple.proxies`) ","m":"both"},{"p":"The Maple data lake (DuckLake)","h":"Backups","u":"data-lake.html#backups","x":"One machine. The lake is its catalog file and its data folder (`analytics/lake`). Copy both together while no refresh is running (not during the proxy's daily run). The daily files can rebuild only the days they still hold, so the lake is the one copy of anything older: back it up if that history matters. Restore it to","m":"both"},{"p":"The Maple data lake (DuckLake)","h":"Troubleshooting","u":"data-lake.html#troubleshooting","x":"`the duckdb command line was not found` Install DuckDB's command line and put it on the `PATH`, or pass `--duckdb`. With `usage_analytics: lake` or `duckdb` and no `duckdb`, the proxy makes only the monthly Parquet files and logs this once. `... not an absolute path` / `... can be written by any user` / `found only thr","m":"both"},{"p":"How developers sign in","h":"How developers sign in","u":"developer-access.html","x":"A developer on a shared proxy needs a token and a few lines that point their tools at the proxy. There are two ways to get them: a token from an admin (works everywhere, no extra setup), or signing in with the company account through the proxy's single sign-on address (the developer makes their own tokens). Both can ru","m":"teams"},{"p":"How developers sign in","h":"In plain words","u":"developer-access.html#in-plain-words","x":"Your admin can give you a token. They make it on the setup page's People & teams tab, copy it to you once, with the lines to paste into your tools. Nothing else to set up. With Maple on your laptop, you paste it on your own proxy's Hubs & chaining tab: your proxy joins the hub as you, and Maple Harness keeps using your","m":"teams"},{"p":"How developers sign in","h":"A token from an admin","u":"developer-access.html#a-token-from-an-admin","x":"On the setup page, People & teams , open the person's Access and tokens : Make a token : what it is for (their laptop, CI), and when it expires (never, by default, or 7, 30 or 90 days, or a year). It has the `proxy` scope only and is tied to the person at once, so their budget, models and accounts apply from the first ","m":"teams"},{"p":"How developers sign in","h":"Signing in with your company account","u":"developer-access.html#signing-in-with-your-company-account","x":"When your admin turns on developer sign-in ( Single sign-on, Developer sign-in ), open the single sign-on address and sign in. Depending on how it is set up: Automatic : you are added as a person at your first sign-in (your team comes from your groups), and land on My access. Ask an admin : your first sign-in says Requ","m":"teams"},{"p":"How developers sign in","h":"Sign your own proxy in","u":"developer-access.html#sign-your-own-proxy-in","x":"On your own machine, Maple Harness talks to its own proxy, and your other tools can too. To use the team hub, that proxy signs in to the hub with your company account, and the hub gives it a token for you. There are three ways to start it, and they are the same sign-in: one started in one place shows in the others, and","m":"teams"},{"p":"How developers sign in","h":"Advanced: a token for CI and containers","u":"developer-access.html#advanced-a-token-for-ci-and-containers","x":"Where no proxy of your own can run (a CI job, a container), Maple Harness can use this proxy directly with a token, from an admin or made on My access: It asks for the token without showing it (or reads it from standard input), keeps it in the secret store and writes `harness.proxy` in its `config.yaml`. `maple-harness","m":"teams"},{"p":"How developers sign in","h":"What admins see","u":"developer-access.html#what-admins-see","x":"On People & teams , each person's Access and tokens says how they sign in (tokens from an admin, or single sign-on with their provider and email) and lists their tokens as above, with Forget their sign-in (their next sign-in is matched again; their tokens work until they expire, and their proxies' sign-ins stop renewin","m":"teams"},{"p":"How developers sign in","h":"Expiry, renewal and revoking","u":"developer-access.html#expiry-renewal-and-revoking","x":"Token Expires Renewed Made by an admin Never, unless they chose a date (through single sign-on: 90 days unless chosen) No Made on My access After `developers.token_days` (30 by default, at most 90) Rotate gives a fresh one Your proxy's, signed in with your company account 12 hours Quietly, for up to 7 days after the ap","m":"teams"},{"p":"How developers sign in","h":"Limits","u":"developer-access.html#limits","x":"A developer may hold 5 live tokens (`developers.max_tokens`) and make 10 an hour. Automatic onboarding adds at most 50 people a day; more sign-ins wait for an admin. With settings from a ConfigMap, developers cannot be added or make tokens themselves (My access says so): an admin gives them one. A proxy with replicas, ","m":"teams"},{"p":"Run the proxy in Docker","h":"Run the proxy in Docker","u":"docker.html","x":"This page runs `maple-proxy` in a container: one container with everything it keeps on one volume, and, when one is not enough, three replicas sharing PostgreSQL and Redis behind a load balancer. The files it uses are samples in the `infra/` folder of the public repository : copy them and change what you need. On your ","m":"both"},{"p":"Run the proxy in Docker","h":"In plain words","u":"docker.html#in-plain-words","x":"What it is. The proxy in a container, built from a release you can check, for a server or a team. Why you want it. It runs next to your other services, keeps everything on one volume, and can grow to several copies behind a load balancer. Do you need it? Not on your own computer: there the proxy is one program. If you ","m":"both"},{"p":"Run the proxy in Docker","h":"What you get","u":"docker.html#what-you-get","x":"One container, its state on one volume. The released binary, verified. No image is published: you build one from a release. The build downloads `maple-proxy` for your architecture from the release, checks it against the release's `SHA256SUMS` (and, for a signed release, that file's signature against the project's relea","m":"both"},{"p":"Run the proxy in Docker","h":"Step 1: build the image","u":"docker.html#step-1-build-the-image","x":"You need Docker with Buildx. Get the samples and build, naming a published release: For several architectures, pushed to your own registry: `MAPLE_VERSION` is required, so an image is always one known release. If a checksum or the signature does not match, the build stops with the reason and no image is made. For build","m":"both"},{"p":"Run the proxy in Docker","h":"Step 2: run one container","u":"docker.html#step-2-run-one-container","x":"Make a token for your tools, then start it with the sample `compose.yaml`: Each line of `secrets/proxy_tokens` is `label=value`. The proxy adds each one as an access token with the `proxy` scope at start (once: a token already there is left as it is). Your tools then use it with `mh_` in front: The ports are published ","m":"both"},{"p":"Run the proxy in Docker","h":"What a start looks like","u":"docker.html#what-a-start-looks-like","x":"These lines are from a real start of the binary in a sandbox set up like the container (on other ports; a few lines left out):","m":"both"},{"p":"Run the proxy in Docker","h":"Settings and providers","u":"docker.html#settings-and-providers","x":"The proxy keeps `config.yaml` on the volume, at `/data/config/maple-harness/config.yaml`, and makes a starter one the first time. Provider keys belong in secrets, never in the image or in `config.yaml`: mount each secret as a file in `/run/secrets/maple/`, named after the secret with `/` written as `.`: the key of the ","m":"both"},{"p":"Run the proxy in Docker","h":"Health checks and stopping","u":"docker.html#health-checks-and-stopping","x":"`GET /healthz` answers `ok` while the proxy runs; `GET /readyz` answers `ok` once providers are loaded (and, with shared storage, while PostgreSQL and Redis answer), and `not ready` otherwise. They say nothing more: no version, no reason. They answer without a token, only for `GET`, behind the same host-name checks as ","m":"both"},{"p":"Run the proxy in Docker","h":"Logs","u":"docker.html#logs","x":"In the container, logs are JSON on standard output, one object per line: Field What it is `time` When, in UTC (RFC 3339, milliseconds) `level` `DEBUG`, `INFO`, `WARN` or `ERROR` `msg` What happened `component` `proxy`, `access` (one line per request), `usage`, `events`, `health`, `chain` or `mcp-hub` `storage`, `proxy_","m":"both"},{"p":"Run the proxy in Docker","h":"Images and memory","u":"docker.html#images-and-memory","x":"Resizing an image (`image_resize_tool`, `image_resize_user`) decodes it in memory, about width × height × 4 bytes, and as much again for the result. In a container the proxy keeps this inside a share of the container's memory limit, so a burst of large screenshots cannot run it out of memory: `images.resize_memory` (or","m":"both"},{"p":"Run the proxy in Docker","h":"Search by meaning","u":"docker.html#search-by-meaning","x":"The built-in models for search by meaning are downloaded the way they always are (the setup page, or `docker exec maple-proxy maple-proxy embeddings install`), into `/data/config/maple-harness/embeddings/` on the volume, so a download survives restarts and upgrades. `MAPLE_EMBEDDINGS_DIR` puts them on a volume of their","m":"both"},{"p":"Run the proxy in Docker","h":"Several replicas","u":"docker.html#several-replicas","x":"When one proxy is not enough, or a restart must not interrupt anyone, run three replicas behind a load balancer (Caddy), sharing PostgreSQL and Redis. The Kubernetes page explains what they share and how they behave; the same applies here. There are two samples: Sample Settings Use it when `compose.ha-shared.yaml` ( re","m":"both"},{"p":"Run the proxy in Docker","h":"Run several replicas: how settings reach every replica","u":"docker.html#run-several-replicas-how-settings-reach-every-replica","x":"In plain words. The three proxies must run the same settings and accept the same tokens. Both samples keep them in PostgreSQL (`MAPLE_SETTINGS_SOURCE`): change a setting on any proxy's setup page and all three run it within seconds. Every replica runs the same configuration: one that cannot confirm it is current report","m":"both"},{"p":"Run the proxy in Docker","h":"The MCP hub in a container","u":"docker.html#the-mcp-hub-in-a-container","x":"The image has no sandbox, so the MCP hub refuses to start a stdio server (a program it would run) there. Run each MCP server as a container of its own that serves MCP over HTTP, and add it to the hub by URL; see The MCP hub on the Kubernetes page for the pattern and the `mcp_hub.allow_uncontained_stdio` setting that ov","m":"both"},{"p":"Run the proxy in Docker","h":"Upgrading and backups","u":"docker.html#upgrading-and-backups","x":"One container: build the new release's image and start it on the same volume. Back up the volume (stop the container, or copy `/data` while it runs: the files are written whole). Several replicas: see Upgrades and Backups . Back up PostgreSQL (it holds the settings too); Redis holds nothing that must survive. Read next","m":"both"},{"p":"Monthly dollar budgets","h":"Monthly dollar budgets","u":"dollar-budgets.html","x":"Give an Anthropic API account a monthly allowance in dollars, with the day it resets, and decide what Maple does when it is spent: warn you, move requests to a fallback, or refuse them. People and teams on a shared proxy can have dollar caps too. Every dollar figure Maple counts itself is an estimate. Maple prices the ","m":"both"},{"p":"Monthly dollar budgets","h":"In plain words","u":"dollar-budgets.html#in-plain-words","x":"Anthropic doesn't tell an app how much money an API key has left. So if you give Maple a key with a fixed amount behind it (the monthly API credits of a Claude Max or Team plan, or a limit you set yourself), Maple can't know when it runs out until Anthropic starts refusing requests. A dollar budget fixes that from Mapl","m":"both"},{"p":"Monthly dollar budgets","h":"On the setup page","u":"dollar-budgets.html#on-the-setup-page","x":"Costs & budgets , the Monthly dollar budgets card. The card is shown when the proxy has an account it can price in dollars: Anthropic, Amazon Bedrock, Google Cloud, Microsoft Azure (Claude), and xAI. Any other account is listed once, at the bottom, as \"Not priced in dollars here\": its requests count $0 against a dollar","m":"both"},{"p":"Monthly dollar budgets","h":"The settings","u":"dollar-budgets.html#the-settings","x":"Key Default What it does `max_usd_per_month` none The month's allowance, in estimated dollars. It counts on its own: `estimate_cost` does not have to be on (that setting only decides whether `/cost` shows a dollar figure) `month_starts_on_day` `1` The day of the month the budget resets, at 00:00 UTC. 1 to 31; a day pas","m":"both"},{"p":"Monthly dollar budgets","h":"What counts","u":"dollar-budgets.html#what-counts","x":"Every request Maple sends to the account, priced from the token counts in Anthropic's answer: input, output (thinking included, as Anthropic bills it), cache reads, and cache writes at their 5-minute or 1-hour price. The long-prompt price of Claude Haiku 5.5, US-only inference, fast mode, the batch tier and web searche","m":"both"},{"p":"Monthly dollar budgets","h":"At 80% and 100%","u":"dollar-budgets.html#at-80-and-100","x":"With budget alerts on (the default once a budget exists), the proxy warns once at 80% and once at 100% of the month, with \"estimated\" and the reset date in the words, for example: anthropic: about $80.40 of $100.00 used this month (estimated at list prices, through this proxy only); resets around 14 Nov The alerts end ","m":"both"},{"p":"Monthly dollar budgets","h":"Forecast","u":"dollar-budgets.html#forecast","x":"prints the month's estimated spend so far and, once there are at least three days of usage, either At this rate, it runs out around 22 Nov (estimated, range 20-24 Nov, from 7 days of your usage through this proxy). or, when it lasts to the reset, At this rate, it lasts until it resets around 14 Dec, with about $38 left","m":"both"},{"p":"Monthly dollar budgets","h":"When Anthropic says the credit is gone","u":"dollar-budgets.html#when-anthropic-says-the-credit-is-gone","x":"Anthropic answers \"credit balance is too low\" when the organisation has no credit left. Without a dollar budget, Maple marks the account as out of credit for an hour, tries its fallbacks meanwhile, and then asks again. With a dollar budget on that account, the mark lasts until the budget's reset day instead, since mont","m":"both"},{"p":"Monthly dollar budgets","h":"People and teams","u":"dollar-budgets.html#people-and-teams","x":"On a shared proxy, a person or a team can have a dollar cap beside their AI-unit budget. On the setup page, open People & teams , then Edit under the person (or the team, under Teams on the same tab): the Dollar cap (estimated) row has a day and a month , beside Budget (AI units) ; `0` is no cap. Press Save budget . Th","m":"both"},{"p":"Monthly dollar budgets","h":"Metrics","u":"dollar-budgets.html#metrics","x":"With metrics on, `maple_budget_used_usd_estimated` and `maple_budget_limit_usd` (label `provider`) give each account's month, and with `metrics.per_person` the people's and teams' dollars too. See Metrics and telemetry .","m":"both"},{"p":"Monthly dollar budgets","h":"Check against Anthropic's bill","u":"dollar-budgets.html#check-against-anthropics-bill","x":"Maple's dollar figures are its own estimate. For an Anthropic API account, Maple can also read what Anthropic itself billed and show the two side by side, so you can see how close the estimate is and what else is spending from the same organisation.","m":"both"},{"p":"Monthly dollar budgets","h":"In plain words","u":"dollar-budgets.html#in-plain-words-1","x":"Anthropic keeps a cost report for every organisation: what each day cost, model by model, in each workspace (the Console's Cost page shows it). Maple can read that report once a day, and whenever you press Check now , and put it next to its own estimate: Anthropic's bill , labelled measured by Anthropic : what Anthropi","m":"both"},{"p":"Monthly dollar budgets","h":"Which key reads the bill","u":"dollar-budgets.html#which-key-reads-the-bill","x":"Anthropic serves the cost report from its Usage & Cost API, which answers only some keys: Key Reads the bill? An API key limited to one workspace (the usual kind, and the one Maple recommends for model requests) No An API key that is not limited to one workspace (organization-scoped) Yes An Admin API key (`sk-ant-admin","m":"both"},{"p":"Monthly dollar budgets","h":"What the card shows","u":"dollar-budgets.html#what-the-card-shows","x":"For each Anthropic account: when the bill was last read, with which key, and up to when it is complete (the day it was read is partial); with a dollar budget : This month so far , which is Anthropic's bill up to the last report plus Maple's estimate since then, said in those words with both labels, and a bar against th","m":"both"},{"p":"Monthly dollar budgets","h":"The settings","u":"dollar-budgets.html#the-settings-1","x":"Key What it does `usage_admin_key` Optional, on a `type: anthropic` provider. A key used only to read the account's cost report. It must be exactly `${secret:provider/<id>/usage_admin_key}`: the setup page writes it. With secrets kept elsewhere (a mounted Kubernetes Secret, say), give the secret that name there and add","m":"both"},{"p":"Monthly dollar budgets","h":"What it does and does not do","u":"dollar-budgets.html#what-it-does-and-does-not-do","x":"The bill is read at most once a day by itself (after 00:30 UTC, for the day before), and when you press Check now (once a minute at most). In storage mode `central`, one replica reads it and every replica shows it. What is read and kept is the month's figures per day, workspace and model, in the configuration folder (o","m":"both"},{"p":"Monthly dollar budgets","h":"The Max and Team API credits","u":"dollar-budgets.html#the-max-and-team-api-credits","x":"These are Anthropic's terms, summarised from their page API credits for Max and Team plans , as read on 2026-10-11; check it for the current details. Plan Monthly API credits Max 5x $100 Max 20x $200 Team $20 per Standard seat and $100 per Premium seat, pooled into one balance, up to $500 You claim them by linking your","m":"both"},{"p":"Monthly dollar budgets","h":"Use your Max or Team credits with Maple","u":"dollar-budgets.html#use-your-max-or-team-credits-with-maple","x":"Link your plan to a Console organisation , as Anthropic's page describes. Note the day your plan bills: that is when the credits arrive. Make a workspace for Maple in that organisation, and give it its own spend limit at or under your credit amount (for example $100 for Max 5x). Anthropic enforces this limit exactly; M","m":"both"},{"p":"Monthly dollar budgets","h":"Related","u":"dollar-budgets.html#related","x":"Proxy settings: Budgets · Check against Anthropic's bill · Alerts and forecasts · Teams · What /cost tells you · Sign in","m":"both"},{"p":"Connect a model server","h":"Connect a model server","u":"endpoints.html","x":"Any server that speaks the OpenAI API can be a provider: Ollama, LM Studio, oMLX, Lemonade, Unsloth Studio, vLLM, llama.cpp, an MLX server, or another. You can add one from the setup page or by editing the configuration file.","m":"both"},{"p":"Connect a model server","h":"In plain words","u":"endpoints.html#in-plain-words","x":"What it is. A model server is a program on your computer or network that runs models itself: Ollama, LM Studio, oMLX, Lemonade, Unsloth, vLLM, llama.cpp and others. Why you want it. Your own models cost nothing per request, and your code never leaves your machine. The clicks. On the setup page, Providers , Find local m","m":"both"},{"p":"Connect a model server","h":"From the setup page","u":"endpoints.html#from-the-setup-page","x":"Open `http://127.0.0.1:11539`, go to Providers and choose + Add provider . Pick the type. The usual address for that server is filled in. Press Detect models to list what the server has loaded, then save. It applies immediately. No restart is needed.","m":"both"},{"p":"Connect a model server","h":"Find the servers already running","u":"endpoints.html#find-the-servers-already-running","x":"If a model server is already running on this computer, the proxy can find it and add it for you. On the setup page: open Providers and press Find local models . Each server found is listed with its models. Press Add it to add one. On a first run: the Get started box shows any server that is running and not added yet. F","m":"both"},{"p":"Connect a model server","h":"By hand","u":"endpoints.html#by-hand","x":"Add a provider to `~/.config/maple-harness/config.yaml`: Field Meaning `id` A name for this provider `type` What kind of server it is (table below) `base_url` The server's address including `/v1` (Lemonade uses `/api/v1`) `optional: true` If the server is not running, skip it instead of failing to start. Good for anyth","m":"both"},{"p":"Connect a model server","h":"Types and their usual addresses","u":"endpoints.html#types-and-their-usual-addresses","x":"`type` Server Address `ollama` Ollama `http://127.0.0.1:11434/v1` `lmstudio` LM Studio `http://127.0.0.1:1234/v1` `omlx` oMLX (Apple silicon) `http://127.0.0.1:8000/v1` `lemonade` Lemonade Server (AMD) `http://127.0.0.1:13305/api/v1` `unsloth` Unsloth Studio (expects an API key) `http://127.0.0.1:8888/v1` `openrouter` ","m":"both"},{"p":"Connect a model server","h":"API keys","u":"endpoints.html#api-keys","x":"If a server needs a key, do not paste it into the file. Refer to it: A key you type on the setup page is stored in the keyring for you.","m":"both"},{"p":"Connect a model server","h":"Use your models from other tools","u":"endpoints.html#use-your-models-from-other-tools","x":"Any tool that speaks the OpenAI or Anthropic API can use the proxy. For what a tool gains from it (every model the proxy serves, fallbacks, budgets, the usage log and prompt caching) and what stays in Maple Harness, see What the proxy adds for other tools . Point it at the proxy's addresses: API Address Path OpenAI-com","m":"both"},{"p":"Connect a model server","h":"Claude Code","u":"endpoints.html#claude-code","x":"Claude Code speaks the Anthropic API. Set these in your shell, or in the `env` block of `~/.claude/settings.json`: Without `ANTHROPIC_DEFAULT_HAIKU_MODEL`, Claude Code asks the proxy for its own small model by name for background work, which fails unless the proxy serves that name.","m":"both"},{"p":"Connect a model server","h":"OpenCode","u":"endpoints.html#opencode","x":"Add a provider to `~/.config/opencode/opencode.json`: Each key under `models` is a name the proxy lists. Pick one with `/models`, or `opencode run -m maple/local \"...\"`.","m":"both"},{"p":"Connect a model server","h":"pi","u":"endpoints.html#pi","x":"Add a provider to `~/.pi/agent/models.json`: pi needs the `apiKey` to show the models; any value works on this machine. Pick a model with `/model`, or `pi --model maple/local`.","m":"both"},{"p":"Connect a model server","h":"GitHub Copilot CLI","u":"endpoints.html#github-copilot-cli","x":"Copilot CLI's bring-your-own-model mode is set with environment variables, and needs no GitHub sign-in: Leave `COPILOT_PROVIDER_TYPE` and `COPILOT_PROVIDER_WIRE_API` at their defaults (`openai` and `completions`). For a proxy on another machine, put the access token in `COPILOT_PROVIDER_API_KEY`.","m":"both"},{"p":"Connect a model server","h":"Hermes","u":"endpoints.html#hermes","x":"Hermes Agent (Nous Research) reads its model from `~/.hermes/config.yaml` (or `$HERMES_HOME/config.yaml`). Point a custom endpoint at the proxy: `hermes model` (choose Custom endpoint ) writes the same settings for you. Hermes needs a model with at least 64,000 tokens of context. For a proxy on another machine, put the","m":"both"},{"p":"Connect a model server","h":"Check it","u":"endpoints.html#check-it","x":"If Detect models finds nothing, the server is usually not running or the address is missing its `/v1`. If Find local models finds nothing: The server is not running, or it is still loading a model and does not answer within about a second and a half. Try again. It listens on a port other than its usual one, or only on ","m":"both"},{"p":"Your first session","h":"Your first session","u":"getting-started.html","x":"A first session in three steps.","m":"single"},{"p":"Your first session","h":"In plain words","u":"getting-started.html#in-plain-words","x":"Three steps: start the proxy (or let the agent start it), sign in to an account or add a model server on the setup page, then run `maple-harness` in your project. The clicks. Open `http://127.0.0.1:11539`. On a first run the Overview shows Get started : sign in to Anthropic or GitHub Copilot there, or add a model serve","m":"single"},{"p":"Your first session","h":"1. Start the proxy","u":"getting-started.html#1-start-the-proxy","x":"The proxy is the one program that talks to models and keeps your sign-ins. The agent starts it for you if nothing is listening, so you can skip this step, but running it yourself lets you watch what it does. On first run it looks for Ollama and Apple's on-device model and writes a working starter configuration. It open","m":"single"},{"p":"Your first session","h":"2. Sign in or add a model","u":"getting-started.html#2-sign-in-or-add-a-model","x":"Open `http://127.0.0.1:11539` and either sign in to GitHub Copilot, save an Anthropic API key, or add a local model server. If a model server is already running on this computer, the page offers to add it (see Find the servers already running ). Or do it from the terminal: Details for each are in Sign in and Connect a ","m":"single"},{"p":"Your first session","h":"3. Start the agent","u":"getting-started.html#3-start-the-agent","x":"Go to a project directory and run: The start screen in a project called acme-api. Type in the box at the bottom; `/help` lists every command. Type what you want done. Useful first commands: Command What it does `/model` Choose a model from the list, or `/model auto` to let the agent choose per message `/help` List ever","m":"single"},{"p":"Your first session","h":"Where things are kept","u":"getting-started.html#where-things-are-kept","x":"Everything lives in `~/.config/maple-harness/` (`%APPDATA%\\maple-harness` on Windows): the configuration file `config.yaml`, saved sessions, memory notes, and your sign-ins. Sign-ins are kept in your system keyring where there is one, not in the configuration file.","m":"single"},{"p":"Your first session","h":"Next","u":"getting-started.html#next","x":"Every command, key and setting, with what it does and its trade-off: Commands and keys , Harness settings and the Proxy settings . For the setting that fits a goal, see Settings by goal .","m":"single"},{"p":"Glossary","h":"Glossary","u":"glossary.html","x":"The words these pages use, in alphabetical order, each with the page that covers it. Access token. A secret that lets a device or another machine use what runs on this one: a paired phone driving or watching sessions, or another machine using the proxy's model APIs. Each token carries only what it is for, and you can r","m":"both"},{"p":"Provider health","h":"Provider health","u":"health.html","x":"The proxy watches how each provider and model answers, from the requests that already go through it, and tells you when one is having a bad time. By default it only watches: nothing about where your requests go changes. You can turn on health failover to have a struggling model tried after the other fallbacks you set u","m":"both"},{"p":"Provider health","h":"In plain words","u":"health.html#in-plain-words","x":"What it is. The proxy watches how each provider and model answers your real requests and marks it healthy, degraded or demoted. Why you want it. You see at once which account or server is failing, and, if you choose, requests can try a struggling model last. The clicks. On the setup page, Health . The card lists each m","m":"both"},{"p":"Provider health","h":"What it shows","u":"health.html#what-it-shows","x":"Each provider and model the proxy has sent requests to gets one of three states: State What it means healthy Answering as it usually does. degraded Failing more than it usually does, or much slower than usual, but not enough to act on. demoted Failing far more than it usually does. With health failover on, it is tried ","m":"both"},{"p":"Provider health","h":"In a terminal","u":"health.html#in-a-terminal","x":"It asks the running proxy on its setup address, like `maple-proxy cache`.","m":"both"},{"p":"Provider health","h":"Clearing a demotion","u":"health.html#clearing-a-demotion","x":"A model's state is kept across a restart, so a model you know is fine again (you fixed its server, its quota was renewed) can stay demoted until a real request tests it. To clear it now: or press Reset beside it on the setup page's Health card ( Reset all clears every model). The model is healthy again with a fresh win","m":"both"},{"p":"Provider health","h":"On a response","u":"health.html#on-a-response","x":"When a model that health would demote answers a request, the response says so in `X-Maple-Router`: Nothing is added while a model is healthy or degraded.","m":"both"},{"p":"Provider health","h":"In the events log","u":"health.html#in-the-events-log","x":"Every change of state is written to the events log as a `health.change` line: the provider and model, the new state, the previous one and why. With health failover on, `health.demote` and `health.restore` say when a model was moved behind its fallbacks and when it went back.","m":"both"},{"p":"Provider health","h":"How a state is decided","u":"health.html#how-a-state-is-decided","x":"Against the model's own usual figures. A local model is slow by nature, and a busy hosted one fails now and then on a good day, so each model is compared with its own earlier requests, not with a fixed number. Until a model has enough history, a modest usual error rate is assumed, and speed is not judged at all. Never ","m":"both"},{"p":"Provider health","h":"Health failover","u":"health.html#health-failover","x":"Off by default. Turn it on with the Health failover switch in the setup page's Proxy settings, Fallbacks, or in `config.yaml`: It applies to the next request, with no restart. With it on, a demoted model is tried after the other models and accounts you already listed for that request in `model_fallbacks` and `provider_","m":"both"},{"p":"Provider health","h":"What it cannot tell you","u":"health.html#what-it-cannot-tell-you","x":"A proxy remembers health across a restart: it saves each model's state and recent figures in `health.json` in the config folder every few minutes and when it stops, so a demoted model is still demoted after a restart and its usual figures are still known. Figures too old to be compared with any more are dropped, as the","m":"both"},{"p":"Provider health","h":"Related","u":"health.html#related","x":"Proxy settings · See and filter your usage · Troubleshooting","m":"both"},{"p":"Use it with herdr","h":"Use it with herdr","u":"herdr.html","x":"herdr hosts terminal sessions for coding agents and shows, for each pane, whether the agent in it is working, waiting for you, or idle. Maple Harness works with it without any setup.","m":"single"},{"p":"Use it with herdr","h":"In plain words","u":"herdr.html#in-plain-words","x":"What it is. herdr shows, for each terminal pane, whether the agent in it is working, waiting for you, or idle. What to do. Nothing: start Maple inside a herdr pane and the pane shows its state.","m":"single"},{"p":"Use it with herdr","h":"What you need to do","u":"herdr.html#what-you-need-to-do","x":"Nothing. Start `maple-harness` (or `mpl`) inside a herdr pane and the pane labels itself `maple-harness` and shows its state as it changes: State What it means working a turn is running blocked it is waiting for you to approve a tool call idle it is waiting for your next message The harness tells herdr itself, over her","m":"single"},{"p":"Use it with herdr","h":"If the pane does not pick it up","u":"herdr.html#if-the-pane-does-not-pick-it-up","x":"Check that you started the harness from a shell inside the herdr pane (not a separate terminal window). If herdr uses a non-default socket, make sure `HERDR_SOCKET_PATH` points at it in the environment the harness inherits. By default it looks in `~/.config/herdr/herdr.sock`. Run `maple-harness version` to confirm you ","m":"single"},{"p":"Use it with herdr","h":"The terminal signals, with or without herdr","u":"herdr.html#the-terminal-signals-with-or-without-herdr","x":"Independently of herdr, the harness sets the window title and the terminal's progress indicator on every change of state, and sends a notification when a turn finishes or needs a decision. Terminals that do not support one of these ignore it. State Window title Progress indicator idle `● MapleHarness` cleared working `","m":"single"},{"p":"Use it with herdr","h":"The detection manifest (advanced)","u":"herdr.html#the-detection-manifest-advanced","x":"herdr can also classify a pane from a detection manifest. Most people do not need this, because the harness reports its own state. If you want the manifest anyway, `maple-harness herdr-manifest` prints it, and `maple-harness herdr-manifest --id <name>` writes a different agent id into it. Whether your herdr version loa","m":"single"},{"p":"Use it with herdr","h":"Turning the installer's herdr step off","u":"herdr.html#turning-the-installers-herdr-step-off","x":"The installer needs to install nothing for herdr. `--no-herdr` is accepted and ignored, so older install commands keep working. Related: terminal notifications and their settings are in Harness settings , and the `herdr-manifest` command is in Commands and keys .","m":"single"},{"p":"Overview","h":"Overview","u":"index.html","x":"Maple Harness is a coding agent for GitHub Copilot's models, Claude and models on your own machine. It comes in three parts: The agent , `maple-harness`: the program you work with in a terminal. It reads your project, edits files and runs commands, and asks before it changes anything. The proxy , `maple-proxy`: holds y","m":"single"},{"p":"Overview","h":"In plain words","u":"index.html#in-plain-words","x":"The agent is where you work: type what you want done in a terminal, and it reads, edits and runs, asking first. The proxy holds your accounts and models. Its setup page, at `http://127.0.0.1:11539`, is where you sign in and change settings, with nothing to edit by hand. Your phone can follow a session and approve what ","m":"single"},{"p":"Overview","h":"Start here","u":"index.html#start-here","x":"Install the programs. Run Your first session . Sign in to GitHub Copilot or Claude, or Connect a model server such as Ollama or LM Studio. Choose a model , or let the agent choose for each message.","m":"single"},{"p":"Overview","h":"Get started","u":"index.html#get-started","x":"Installing, signing in and connecting models. What's inside Maple : what it does for you behind the scenes, and why it helps. Install : the one-line installer, platforms, updating and removing it. Upgrading to 2.0 : what keeps working, what is new, and how to go back to 1.x. Which setup do I need? : one laptop, a lapto","m":"single"},{"p":"Overview","h":"Guides","u":"index.html#guides","x":"How each part works, and how to use it with other tools. The agent Subagents : hand small jobs to helper agents, or make your own. Language servers : compile errors right after every edit. Search by meaning : let the agent find things by what they mean, not only the words. The shell sandbox : contain the commands the m","m":"single"},{"p":"Overview","h":"Cost and usage","u":"index.html#cost-and-usage","x":"Where the spend goes, how to keep it down, and how to check any claim on your own tasks. See and reduce what a task costs : start here. What /cost tells you : each part of `/cost`, and what to do about it. Settings by goal : the setting for spending less, keeping quality or stopping runaways. See and filter your usage ","m":"single"},{"p":"Overview","h":"Reference","u":"index.html#reference","x":"Every command, key and setting, with what it does, its default and its trade-off. Commands and keys : slash commands, keys, modes and shell commands. Harness settings : every `harness:` setting in `config.yaml`. Proxy settings : every proxy setting, setup-page card, command and response header.","m":"single"},{"p":"Overview","h":"Help","u":"index.html#help","x":"When something does not work, and the rest. Troubleshooting : common problems and their fixes. Security : what it can touch and how to keep it safe. Glossary : the words these pages use. Licence . Release notes , with the downloads.","m":"single"},{"p":"Install","h":"Install","u":"install.html","x":"On every system Maple Harness is two programs: `maple-harness`, the agent, and `maple-proxy`, the proxy, which holds your sign-ins and talks to the models. On macOS and Linux the installer also adds the short names `mpl` and `mplp`. On a Mac with Apple silicon there is a third program, `maple-sidecar`, a small helper f","m":"both"},{"p":"Install","h":"In plain words","u":"install.html#in-plain-words","x":"What it is. One command puts the two programs on your computer: `maple-harness`, the agent you talk to, and `maple-proxy`, which holds your sign-ins and talks to the models. Why this way. It needs no account, it checks every file it downloads against the release's checksums, and it refuses anything that does not match.","m":"both"},{"p":"Install","h":"One line","u":"install.html#one-line","x":"macOS and Linux: Windows (PowerShell): No account or token is needed. The installer detects your system, downloads the release, checks every file against the release's `SHA256SUMS`, and refuses to install anything that does not match. To read it first, open it on GitHub . On macOS and Linux it installs to `/usr/local/b","m":"both"},{"p":"Install","h":"Platforms","u":"install.html#platforms","x":"System Agent and proxy Apple on-device model macOS, Apple silicon yes yes (macOS 26 or newer) macOS, Intel yes no Linux, amd64 and arm64 yes no Windows, amd64 yes (less tested) no macOS. The shell sandbox is built in: nothing to install. The on-device model needs a Mac with Apple silicon and macOS 26 or newer; the agen","m":"both"},{"p":"Install","h":"Optional: contain the shell (bubblewrap)","u":"install.html#optional-contain-the-shell-bubblewrap","x":"The agent can run the commands its shell tool executes inside a sandbox. On macOS that is built in. On Linux it needs bubblewrap, which is optional : without it the shell still works, uncontained. System Command Arch, Manjaro `sudo pacman -S bubblewrap` Debian, Ubuntu `sudo apt install bubblewrap` Fedora, RHEL `sudo dn","m":"both"},{"p":"Install","h":"Release files","u":"install.html#release-files","x":"Each release on the releases page has these files. The installer picks the right ones; you only need this list to download by hand. File What it is `maple-harness_linux_amd64`, `maple-harness_linux_arm64`, `maple-harness_darwin_arm64`, `maple-harness_darwin_amd64`, `maple-harness_windows_amd64.exe` The agent `maple-pro","m":"both"},{"p":"Install","h":"Update","u":"install.html#update","x":"","m":"both"},{"p":"Install","h":"Uninstall","u":"install.html#uninstall","x":"The second line deletes your configuration, stored sign-ins, memory notes and saved sessions. Skip it if you plan to reinstall.","m":"both"},{"p":"Install","h":"Next","u":"install.html#next","x":"Your first session .","m":"both"},{"p":"Run the proxy on Kubernetes","h":"Run the proxy on Kubernetes","u":"kubernetes.html","x":"This page runs `maple-proxy` on Kubernetes from the samples in the `infra/` folder of the public repository : plain manifests with Kustomize overlays for one replica, or three replicas sharing PostgreSQL and Redis. They are starting points: read them and adapt them to your cluster. Not sure you need a cluster, or sever","m":"teams"},{"p":"Run the proxy on Kubernetes","h":"In plain words","u":"kubernetes.html#in-plain-words","x":"What it is. Sample manifests to run the proxy on Kubernetes: one replica, or three sharing PostgreSQL and Redis. Why you want it. A team proxy that survives a node going away, configured the way the rest of your cluster is. What you see on the setup page. With several replicas, settings are kept in PostgreSQL and chang","m":"teams"},{"p":"Run the proxy on Kubernetes","h":"The samples","u":"kubernetes.html#the-samples","x":"Folder What it is `manifest/maple-proxy/` The proxy: Deployment, Service, PodDisruptionBudget, an example ConfigMap and an example Secret `manifest/postgres/`, `manifest/redis/` One PostgreSQL and one Redis, for trying it out . In production use your managed or production services, with backups `manifest/maple-mcp-hub/","m":"teams"},{"p":"Run the proxy on Kubernetes","h":"One replica","u":"kubernetes.html#one-replica","x":"1. Build and push the image (no image is published; see Docker ): 2. Set the image and the secrets. In `config/single`: Edit `manifest/maple-proxy/secret.example.yaml` (or create the Secret `maple-proxy` from your secret manager): the key `access.proxy_tokens` holds one `label=value` per line, the tokens your tools use","m":"teams"},{"p":"Run the proxy on Kubernetes","h":"Several replicas","u":"kubernetes.html#several-replicas","x":"Three replicas: no state of their own, PostgreSQL for what lasts, Redis for what is shared and short-lived.","m":"teams"},{"p":"Run the proxy on Kubernetes","h":"When you need it","u":"kubernetes.html#when-you-need-it","x":"One replica is enough for most teams: the proxy is light, and a restart takes seconds. Run several when a restart or a lost node must not interrupt anyone, or when one replica is not enough. Several replicas need PostgreSQL and Redis (`storage.mode: central`): a replica then keeps nothing that matters on its own, so an","m":"teams"},{"p":"Run the proxy on Kubernetes","h":"What lives where","u":"kubernetes.html#what-lives-where","x":"What One replica (`storage.mode: disk`, the default) Several replicas (`storage.mode: central`) Usage log Daily files on the volume PostgreSQL (each line once), and a copy on each replica's own folder Events, alerts fired Files on the volume PostgreSQL; each alert fires once, on one replica Audit log A file on the volu","m":"teams"},{"p":"Run the proxy on Kubernetes","h":"Set it up","u":"kubernetes.html#set-it-up","x":"`config/ha` adds PostgreSQL and Redis samples and three replicas, with settings from the ConfigMap; `config/ha-shared`, the recommended one, builds on it and keeps the settings in PostgreSQL (see how settings reach every replica ): Edit the Secret patch in `kustomization.yaml` (and `manifest/postgres/service.yaml`'s pa","m":"teams"},{"p":"Run the proxy on Kubernetes","h":"On the setup page","u":"kubernetes.html#on-the-setup-page","x":"The setup page's Data & storage tab has a section, Several replicas (central storage) , that explains what it is for and writes the settings for you to copy. Pick where the proxy runs ( Docker Compose , Kubernetes or systemd ), where the settings live ( in PostgreSQL , edited on the page, recommended, or from a file , ","m":"teams"},{"p":"Run the proxy on Kubernetes","h":"Run several replicas: how settings reach every replica","u":"kubernetes.html#run-several-replicas-how-settings-reach-every-replica","x":"In plain words. Every replica has to run the same settings (`config.yaml`) and accept the same access tokens. There are two ways to do that; pick one: Settings in PostgreSQL (recommended). The replicas keep one copy of the settings in the PostgreSQL they already share. Change a setting on any replica's setup page and e","m":"teams"},{"p":"Run the proxy on Kubernetes","h":"Load balancing","u":"kubernetes.html#load-balancing","x":"Round robin is right: nothing needs sticky sessions. A conversation keeps to the provider account that last answered it on every replica (so the provider's prompt cache keeps hitting), signed-in browsers are known to every replica, and rate limits count across replicas. If you want them anyway, hash on the `X-Maple-Ses","m":"teams"},{"p":"Run the proxy on Kubernetes","h":"When PostgreSQL or Redis is away","u":"kubernetes.html#when-postgresql-or-redis-is-away","x":"PostgreSQL away: requests are still served. Requests of people with a budget are refused with `503` until it is back (`storage.budgets_when_down: open` lets them through, uncounted). Usage lines wait in a small queue in the replica's memory (`storage.spool_max_records`, 2000) and are written once it is back, each once.","m":"teams"},{"p":"Run the proxy on Kubernetes","h":"Upgrades","u":"kubernetes.html#upgrades","x":"Roll replicas out one at a time (the sample sets `maxUnavailable: 0`). A new release adds to the database's tables and never removes or renames anything that the release before still uses, so old and new replicas run side by side during the rollout; upgrade one release at a time. A replica that stops turns not ready fi","m":"teams"},{"p":"Run the proxy on Kubernetes","h":"Backups","u":"kubernetes.html#backups","x":"Back up PostgreSQL (`pg_dump`, or your provider's backups): it holds the usage, events, budgets, settings and audit log. `maple-proxy storage export-config` also writes the shared settings to files. Redis holds nothing that must survive: after losing it, browsers sign in again and caches fill again (and the record of t","m":"teams"},{"p":"Run the proxy on Kubernetes","h":"Usage for analysis","u":"kubernetes.html#usage-for-analysis","x":"The shared PostgreSQL holds every replica's usage and events as two read-only views with typed columns: `maple_usage_rows` (every column of the usage record , in its order, after `_day`, `_ingested_at` and `_ingest_seq`) and `maple_events_rows` (the events columns after `_event_id`, `_day`, `_ingested_at`, `_ingest_seq","m":"teams"},{"p":"Run the proxy on Kubernetes","h":"The MCP hub","u":"kubernetes.html#the-mcp-hub","x":"The hub's servers are programs and sessions that live on one machine, so the hub runs as one replica of its own (`manifest/maple-mcp-hub`, started with `maple-proxy mcp-hub serve`), with its own ConfigMap; clients connect to its Service. The proxy replicas do not serve it. It has a Secret of its own: never mount the pr","m":"teams"},{"p":"Run the proxy on Kubernetes","h":"Reaching cloud metadata or link-local services","u":"kubernetes.html#reaching-cloud-metadata-or-link-local-services","x":"The hub never connects an MCP server to a link-local address (`169.254.0.0/16`, `fe80::/10`) or a cloud metadata address (such as `169.254.169.254` or `fd00:ec2::254`), also in the standard IPv6 forms that carry such an address, and `allow_private` does not change that: on a node, the metadata address hands out the nod","m":"teams"},{"p":"Run the proxy on Kubernetes","h":"Logs","u":"kubernetes.html#logs","x":"Logs are JSON on standard output, one object per line, with `proxy_instance` naming the pod; see Docker: Logs for the fields, levels and shipping. Probes are left out of the request log.","m":"teams"},{"p":"Run the proxy on Kubernetes","h":"Metrics","u":"kubernetes.html#metrics","x":"Each pod can serve its own Prometheus `/metrics` (off by default): set `metrics: {prometheus: \"on\"}` in the ConfigMap, add a metrics token to the Secret (`access.metrics_tokens`, one `label=token` a line), and add the `config/metrics` component to your overlay. It adds a `ServiceMonitor` (Prometheus Operator) that scra","m":"teams"},{"p":"Run the proxy on Kubernetes","h":"Images and memory","u":"kubernetes.html#images-and-memory","x":"The pod's memory limit sets how much image resizing may take at once (25% of it by default) and Go's own memory limit (90% of it); see Docker: Images and memory . Raise `limits.memory`, or `images.resize_memory`, if your tools send many large screenshots at once.","m":"teams"},{"p":"Run the proxy on Kubernetes","h":"Search by meaning","u":"kubernetes.html#search-by-meaning","x":"Each replica downloads a model on first use into its own folder (an `emptyDir` in `config/ha`). To download once for all, mount one ReadWriteMany volume at `/models` in every replica: replicas installing the same model at the same moment wait for one another, and each download is checked against its pinned checksums be","m":"teams"},{"p":"Language servers","h":"Language servers","u":"language-servers.html","x":"Language servers give the agent compile errors right after every edit. A language server is the program your editor uses to underline errors, jump to a definition and show a type on hover, and Maple Harness can use the same programs. With one installed for your project's language: Errors are shown right after each edit","m":"single"},{"p":"Language servers","h":"In plain words","u":"language-servers.html#in-plain-words","x":"What it is. The programs your editor uses to underline errors. The agent uses the same ones. Why you want it. The agent sees a compile error right after its edit and fixes it on the next step, instead of finding out when the build fails. What to do. Have the language server for your language installed (the one your edi","m":"single"},{"p":"Language servers","h":"How it finds servers","u":"language-servers.html#how-it-finds-servers","x":"By default (`harness.lsp: auto`), the agent looks at the folder you started it in. If it finds a file that marks a language, and the matching server is on your `PATH`, that server is used for that language. Nothing else is needed. If you name servers yourself in `harness.lsp_servers`, those are used exactly as you wrot","m":"single"},{"p":"Language servers","h":"Check what it found","u":"language-servers.html#check-what-it-found","x":"Type `/lsp` in a session. It lists each server as running , idle (installed, starts when first needed) or missing , and prints the install command for a missing one. `maple-harness doctor` has a \"Language servers\" line too. A missing server is only a warning: everything still works, the agent just doesn't get compile e","m":"single"},{"p":"Language servers","h":"Install a server","u":"language-servers.html#install-a-server","x":"Start the agent in the project's root folder, the one with the marker file. The first two columns say what triggers a language and which server is used. Language Marker files in the project folder Server Install Go `go.mod`, `go.work` `gopls` `go install golang.org/x/tools/gopls@latest` TypeScript, JavaScript `tsconfig","m":"single"},{"p":"Language servers","h":"Gherkin and Cucumber","u":"language-servers.html#gherkin-and-cucumber","x":"If your project describes behaviour in `.feature` files (behaviour-driven development), the Cucumber language server checks them. The most useful thing it does is report a step with no matching step definition , so when the agent writes a scenario, it learns immediately which steps still need code. A typical project: W","m":"single"},{"p":"Language servers","h":"Choose the servers yourself","u":"language-servers.html#choose-the-servers-yourself","x":"Add `lsp_servers` to `config.yaml`. Each entry names the file extensions, the command and its arguments. This replaces detection.","m":"single"},{"p":"Language servers","h":"Turn it off","u":"language-servers.html#turn-it-off","x":"With `lsp: off` no server is started, no errors are added to results and the three lookup tools are not offered. `lsp: auto` is the default.","m":"single"},{"p":"Language servers","h":"When the agent still writes files with echo and cat","u":"language-servers.html#when-the-agent-still-writes-files-with-echo-and-cat","x":"The agent is told to create and change files with its own file tools, because those show you a diff, apply the safety checks and run the language server. Some models still write files through the shell, for example with `cat > file <<EOF`. By default the agent notes it in that command's result so the model can correct ","m":"single"},{"p":"Language servers","h":"Troubleshooting","u":"language-servers.html#troubleshooting","x":"Installed, but `/lsp` says missing. The agent looks on the `PATH` it was started with. A tool installed by `npm i -g` or `go install` lives in a folder that must be on that `PATH` (for Go, `~/go/bin`). Start the agent from a shell where the command works, and check with `which gopls` (or the server's name). No server i","m":"single"},{"p":"Language servers","h":"What has been tried","u":"language-servers.html#what-has-been-tried","x":"Stated plainly, so you know what to expect: The way the agent starts servers, sends them each edit and reads back their errors, and the answers it gives to the requests servers make of an editor, has been tested against a stand-in server in the test suite. The Cucumber language server was run for real: it reported \"Und","m":"single"},{"p":"Language servers","h":"Related","u":"language-servers.html#related","x":"The settings are in Harness settings , and `/lsp` is in Commands and keys . Other problems: Troubleshooting .","m":"single"},{"p":"MCP hub (experimental)","h":"MCP hub (experimental)","u":"mcp-hub.html","x":"The proxy can run your MCP servers once and offer all of their tools at one address. Maple Harness, Claude Code, OpenCode and any other tool that speaks MCP then point at that address instead of each starting its own copy of every server. It is experimental and off by default . While it is off nothing listens, nothing ","m":"both"},{"p":"MCP hub (experimental)","h":"In plain words","u":"mcp-hub.html#in-plain-words","x":"What it is. The proxy runs your MCP servers (the add-ons that give an agent extra tools) once, and every tool you use reaches them at one address. Why you want it. One copy of each server instead of one per tool, one place to limit what each person may call. The clicks. Servers are added in a terminal (`maple-proxy mcp","m":"both"},{"p":"MCP hub (experimental)","h":"How it works","u":"mcp-hub.html#how-it-works","x":"You list the servers in `config.yaml` under `mcp_hub.servers`, in the same shape as the agent's `mcp_servers`: a program the proxy starts (stdio), or a server it reaches by URL (`http` or `sse`). The proxy serves one MCP endpoint, `http://127.0.0.1:11545/mcp` by default (Streamable HTTP). Each tool is named `<server>__","m":"both"},{"p":"MCP hub (experimental)","h":"Turn it on","u":"mcp-hub.html#turn-it-on","x":"Add a server from a terminal, then turn the hub on: `token` prints an access token once: every tool that uses the hub sends it (see below). or write it in `config.yaml`: A running proxy picks the change up within a few seconds; there is nothing to restart. The setup page's MCP hub card shows each server, its state and ","m":"both"},{"p":"MCP hub (experimental)","h":"Point your tools at it","u":"mcp-hub.html#point-your-tools-at-it","x":"Each tool sends the token from `maple-proxy mcp-hub token` as `Authorization: Bearer <token>`. Keep it in an environment variable or your tool's secret store rather than in a file you share.","m":"both"},{"p":"MCP hub (experimental)","h":"Maple Harness","u":"mcp-hub.html#maple-harness","x":"In `config.yaml`: The tools appear as `mcp__hub__<server>__<tool>` and ask for approval like any other tool. To allow one without asking, name it in a rule, for example `- {decision: allow, pattern: mcp__hub__files__read_file}`. Maple Harness sends the token to the hub's address on this machine only after the hub prove","m":"both"},{"p":"MCP hub (experimental)","h":"Claude Code","u":"mcp-hub.html#claude-code","x":"or in `.mcp.json`:","m":"both"},{"p":"MCP hub (experimental)","h":"OpenCode","u":"mcp-hub.html#opencode","x":"In `opencode.json`:","m":"both"},{"p":"MCP hub (experimental)","h":"Settings","u":"mcp-hub.html#settings","x":"Key Default What it does `mcp_hub.enabled` off Turns the hub on `mcp_hub.listen` `127.0.0.1:11545` Where the endpoint is served (path `/mcp`) `mcp_hub.loopback_without_token` off Lets tools on this machine use the hub without a token. Anything that runs on this machine, a sandboxed command included, could then call eve","m":"both"},{"p":"MCP hub (experimental)","h":"Who may use which tools","u":"mcp-hub.html#who-may-use-which-tools","x":"With people set up, each access token belongs to a person, and `mcp_hub.people` says which of the hub's tools each person may see and call: `local` is whoever calls from this machine without a token (only with `loopback_without_token`), `unattributed` a token tied to no person, and `*` anyone not listed. With no `peopl","m":"both"},{"p":"MCP hub (experimental)","h":"Security","u":"mcp-hub.html#security","x":"This machine only, by default, and a token on every request. The token has the `mcp-hub` scope, which no other scope includes, so a token made for the model surfaces or for remote control cannot reach the hub. Make one with `maple-proxy mcp-hub token <label>` in a terminal (it is shown once) and revoke it on the setup ","m":"both"},{"p":"MCP hub (experimental)","h":"Reaching cloud metadata or link-local services","u":"mcp-hub.html#reaching-cloud-metadata-or-link-local-services","x":"A server reached by URL is never connected to a link-local address (`169.254.0.0/16`, `fe80::/10`) or a cloud provider's metadata address (such as `169.254.169.254`, `fd00:ec2::254`, `fd20:ce::254` or `100.100.100.200`), whatever name points there, and also when it is written in one of the standard IPv6 forms that carr","m":"both"},{"p":"MCP hub (experimental)","h":"Running it as a service of its own","u":"mcp-hub.html#running-it-as-a-service-of-its-own","x":"`maple-proxy mcp-hub serve` runs the hub alone. Run one copy (the servers it starts are not shared between copies). To put it behind a reverse proxy or a load balancer, forward `/mcp` with the `Authorization` header, give the hub a `listen` address the forwarder can reach (with `tls_cert` and `tls_key`, or `insecure_ht","m":"both"},{"p":"MCP hub (experimental)","h":"Running it in a container","u":"mcp-hub.html#running-it-in-a-container","x":"The container image has no sandbox (one would need user namespaces, which containers rarely get), so there the hub starts a stdio server only if a working sandbox is available, and otherwise refuses it with a message in its log and in `maple-proxy mcp-hub status`. The status shows the sandbox as `container-strict`. Run","m":"both"},{"p":"MCP hub (experimental)","h":"Limits","u":"mcp-hub.html#limits","x":"Experimental: names and settings may still change before it is final. Tools only: a server's resources and prompts are not offered through the hub. A server's tool list is read when it starts; a server that changes its tools later is seen after it restarts. A call through the hub stops after 5 minutes. An older proxy i","m":"both"},{"p":"MCP hub (experimental)","h":"Commands","u":"mcp-hub.html#commands","x":"Command What it does `maple-proxy mcp-hub status` What the running hub is doing: its address, each server's state and tools. `--json` `maple-proxy mcp-hub serve` Run only the hub, without the model surfaces or the setup page, from the same `config.yaml` and tokens: for running it as a service of its own `maple-proxy mc","m":"both"},{"p":"Give other tools this project's knowledge","h":"Give other tools this project's knowledge","u":"mcp.html","x":"`maple-harness mcp serve` lets any tool that speaks MCP (Claude Code, OpenCode, another Maple Harness) read this project's notes and code map, the same ones the agent uses. Build the index once from the project directory: Then connect a tool. A client normally launches the server itself.","m":"single"},{"p":"Give other tools this project's knowledge","h":"In plain words","u":"mcp.html#in-plain-words","x":"What it is. Other tools (Claude Code, OpenCode, another Maple) can read this project's memory notes and code map, the ones Maple builds as it works. Why you want it. Each tool starts already knowing your project's structure, instead of reading it again. What to do. `maple-harness graph reindex` once in the project, the","m":"single"},{"p":"Give other tools this project's knowledge","h":"Claude Code","u":"mcp.html#claude-code","x":"or in the project's `.mcp.json`: Add `\"--read-only\"` to the arguments to withhold the tools that write notes.","m":"single"},{"p":"Give other tools this project's knowledge","h":"OpenCode","u":"mcp.html#opencode","x":"In `opencode.json`:","m":"single"},{"p":"Give other tools this project's knowledge","h":"Another Maple Harness","u":"mcp.html#another-maple-harness","x":"In `config.yaml`:","m":"single"},{"p":"Give other tools this project's knowledge","h":"Using MCP servers from the agent","u":"mcp.html#using-mcp-servers-from-the-agent","x":"The agent can use MCP servers too, from the `harness.mcp_servers` list or a project's `.mcp.json`. Their tools ask for approval like any other tool. A server that runs as a program on your machine runs with your permissions, so add only servers you trust. A server reached over the network needs `https` unless it is on ","m":"single"},{"p":"Metrics and telemetry export","h":"Metrics and telemetry export","u":"metrics-and-telemetry.html","x":"The proxy can hand what it already knows about each request (counts, timings, tokens, health, budgets) to the monitoring and security tools you run: a Prometheus `/metrics` endpoint, an OpenTelemetry (OTLP) export of traces, metrics and logs, and the audit events as JSON lines for a SIEM. All of it is off by default ; ","m":"both"},{"p":"Metrics and telemetry export","h":"In plain words","u":"metrics-and-telemetry.html#in-plain-words","x":"If you already run Prometheus, Grafana, an OpenTelemetry collector or a security tool (a SIEM), the proxy can feed them: how many requests, how fast, how many tokens and AI units, which providers are healthy, how much of each budget is used. You then watch Maple in the same place as everything else, and get its alerts ","m":"both"},{"p":"Metrics and telemetry export","h":"Headers on the page","u":"metrics-and-telemetry.html#headers-on-the-page","x":"A header's value (an API key, a `Bearer` token) is typed once and goes straight to the secret store, in the slot `otel/<name>` (the name in lower case); `config.yaml` gets the reference `${secret:otel/<name>}`, never the value. The page then shows each header by name only, with where its value comes from: The page says","m":"both"},{"p":"Metrics and telemetry export","h":"Send a test export","u":"metrics-and-telemetry.html#send-a-test-export","x":"Send a test export sends one span and one log record, both named `maple.test`, with the headers you set, to the collector as saved. Nothing in them comes from a request, prompt or answer. The page then shows where they went (the scheme and host for each signal), the protocol, the names of the headers sent (never their ","m":"both"},{"p":"Metrics and telemetry export","h":"Set it in config.yaml","u":"metrics-and-telemetry.html#set-it-in-configyaml","x":"The page writes these keys; you can also write them yourself. Every key is in Proxy settings . A header that carries a credential (`authorization`, anything with `token`, `key` or `secret` in its name) must be a `${secret:...}` or `${env:...}` reference: set it on the page, or store it with `maple-proxy secrets set ote","m":"both"},{"p":"Metrics and telemetry export","h":"Prometheus","u":"metrics-and-telemetry.html#prometheus","x":"","m":"both"},{"p":"Metrics and telemetry export","h":"Where to scrape","u":"metrics-and-telemetry.html#where-to-scrape","x":"From Address Token This machine `http://127.0.0.1:11539/metrics` (the setup page's address) none; with a setup-page password, a token with the `metrics` scope Another machine, a container, Kubernetes the OpenAI-compatible address, `/metrics` a token with the `metrics` scope Make a token on the Monitoring tab (step 2), ","m":"both"},{"p":"Metrics and telemetry export","h":"What is exported","u":"metrics-and-telemetry.html#what-is-exported","x":"Metric Type Labels `maple_requests_total` counter `provider`, `model`, `outcome` (`ok`, `error`, `canceled`, `cached`), `error_class`, `auto_weight` `maple_request_duration_seconds` histogram `provider`, `model`, `outcome` `maple_time_to_first_token_seconds` histogram `provider`, `model` (streamed answers) `maple_token","m":"both"},{"p":"Metrics and telemetry export","h":"Labels stay bounded","u":"metrics-and-telemetry.html#labels-stay-bounded","x":"Every label has a fixed set of values or a cap on how many it keeps (a model name past the cap is counted as `other`), so a client sending a new model name with every request cannot grow your Prometheus without end. There is no session, conversation, token or person label. `metrics.per_person: on` adds `person` and `te","m":"both"},{"p":"Metrics and telemetry export","h":"Several replicas","u":"metrics-and-telemetry.html#several-replicas","x":"Each pod serves its own `/metrics`, counting what it answered. Scrape every pod and add them up in Prometheus (`sum by (provider, model) (rate(maple_requests_total[5m]))`). Gauges such as `maple_budget_used` are each pod's own view. For the Prometheus Operator, the Kubernetes samples have a component, `config/metrics`,","m":"both"},{"p":"Metrics and telemetry export","h":"Grafana","u":"metrics-and-telemetry.html#grafana","x":"Some starting points: requests a second by outcome: `sum by (outcome) (rate(maple_requests_total[5m]))` error ratio by provider: `sum by (provider) (rate(maple_requests_total{outcome=\"error\"}[5m])) / sum by (provider) (rate(maple_requests_total[5m]))` 95th-percentile latency: `histogram_quantile(0.95, sum by (le, model","m":"both"},{"p":"Metrics and telemetry export","h":"OpenTelemetry","u":"metrics-and-telemetry.html#opentelemetry","x":"With `otel.endpoint` set, the proxy sends to an OpenTelemetry collector over OTLP, gRPC (`grpc`, the default, port or HTTP/protobuf (`http`, port 4318; the signal paths `/v1/traces`, `/v1/metrics` and `/v1/logs` are added to the address). `http://` is plain text and `https://` is TLS. Traces: one span per request, in o","m":"both"},{"p":"Metrics and telemetry export","h":"Environment variables","u":"metrics-and-telemetry.html#environment-variables","x":"The standard `OTEL_*` variables fill in what `otel:` leaves unset: `OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_PROTOCOL`, `OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_HEADERS`, `OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_TIMEOUT`, `OTEL_SERVICE_NAME` (default `maple-proxy`), `OTEL_RESOURCE_ATTRIBUTES`, `OTEL_BSP_SCHEDULE_DELAY`, `OTEL_BSP_MAX_QUEUE_SIZE`, `OTEL_BSP_MAX_EXPORT_BATCH_SIZE` an","m":"both"},{"p":"Metrics and telemetry export","h":"Collector","u":"metrics-and-telemetry.html#collector","x":"A minimal OpenTelemetry Collector configuration that receives from the proxy:","m":"both"},{"p":"Metrics and telemetry export","h":"Audit events for a SIEM","u":"metrics-and-telemetry.html#audit-events-for-a-siem","x":"The audit events are what was done to the proxy's access and settings: a token made or revoked, refused and rate-limited requests, setup-page sign-ins and settings written, secrets moved, and MCP hub tool calls. With `log.audit_lines: on` (or `MAPLE_LOG_AUDIT=on`) each is also written to standard output as one JSON lin","m":"both"},{"p":"Metrics and telemetry export","h":"What is never exported","u":"metrics-and-telemetry.html#what-is-never-exported","x":"prompts, answers, files, images, tool definitions, tool arguments or results; error text from a provider (only its class); keys, tokens, header values, setup-page passwords; session ids, conversation ids, request fingerprints and access-token labels in metrics and spans; people and teams, unless `metrics.per_person` is","m":"both"},{"p":"Metrics and telemetry export","h":"Related","u":"metrics-and-telemetry.html#related","x":"Proxy settings : every key. See and filter your usage : the usage log, on your own disk. Provider health and Alerts and forecasts .","m":"both"},{"p":"Choose a model","h":"Choose a model","u":"models.html","x":"Switch models at any time inside the agent: Which models appear depends on what you have configured: the ones your Copilot plan allows, the ones your Anthropic key can call, and whatever your local servers have loaded. The agent also shows how much of the model's context is used, in the status bar. `/context` shows the","m":"single"},{"p":"Choose a model","h":"In plain words","u":"models.html#in-plain-words","x":"What it is. Which model answers your messages. `/model` in the agent shows the list and switches. Let it choose. `/model auto` picks a heavier or lighter model for each message from the same provider, and says what it picked and why. On the setup page. Models & routing , Auto mode , chooses what `auto` means for every ","m":"single"},{"p":"Choose a model","h":"Automatic choice","u":"models.html#automatic-choice","x":"`/model auto` chooses a model for each message from your current provider's own ranking of its models, and tells you what it chose and why. For example, the status bar reads `gpt-4.1 (auto: heavy)` and the first message prints a line such as `model: auto → gpt-4.1 (heavy; no powerful model on this account; used versati","m":"single"},{"p":"Choose a model","h":"Auto in other tools, through the proxy","u":"models.html#auto-in-other-tools-through-the-proxy","x":"The proxy has the same idea built in, for every tool that talks to it: ask for the model `auto` and the proxy chooses a model for each request, or ask for `auto-heavy`, `auto-medium` or `auto-light` to fix the weight. With nothing configured it uses the first provider in `config.yaml` that ranks its models, and never m","m":"single"},{"p":"Choose a model","h":"Copilot Auto","u":"models.html#copilot-auto","x":"GitHub Copilot has an automatic mode of its own, in which Copilot picks the model for each request. The proxy lists it as a model of each Copilot account, named after the account, for example `github-copilot/auto`: In the `/model` list, and on the status bar once chosen, it reads `Copilot Auto (github-copilot)`, with t","m":"single"},{"p":"Choose a model","h":"A default model","u":"models.html#a-default-model","x":"Set the model the agent starts with in `config.yaml`: If the configured model is not one your proxy serves, the agent starts on the first usable model it does serve and prints a one-line note listing the real names.","m":"single"},{"p":"Choose a model","h":"Effort and thinking","u":"models.html#effort-and-thinking","x":"Related: every model setting is in Harness settings ; to fall back to another model or account when one cannot answer, see Fallbacks ; for what a model costs you, see See and reduce what a task costs .","m":"single"},{"p":"See and filter your usage","h":"See and filter your usage","u":"observability.html","x":"Every request that goes through the proxy is written to your usage log, on your own disk. This page is the guide to using it: asking questions with `maple-proxy usage query`, exporting only the requests you care about, reading it in DuckDB with ready-made views, and the events log that sits next to it. The full referen","m":"both"},{"p":"See and filter your usage","h":"In plain words","u":"observability.html#in-plain-words","x":"What it is. Every request through the proxy leaves one line in a log on your own disk: which model, how many tokens, how long, how it ended. Never the prompt or the answer. Why you want it. Answer your own questions: what did last week cost, which tool used most, how often did a provider fail. What to do. On the setup ","m":"both"},{"p":"See and filter your usage","h":"What is recorded, and what never is","u":"observability.html#what-is-recorded-and-what-never-is","x":"One line per request, with counts and labels only: what was asked and who answered: the model the client asked for (`auto`, an alias), the provider and model that answered, and whether a fallback stepped in; how big it was: input, output and cached tokens, and the AI units the provider reported; how long it took: the w","m":"both"},{"p":"See and filter your usage","h":"Ask a question: usage query","u":"observability.html#ask-a-question-usage-query","x":"`maple-proxy usage query` with no options prints the totals for everything in the log. `--group-by` splits them, and the filters narrow them down: Requests by provider and outcome. A demo setup with stand-in model services: `lab` points at an address where nothing listens, and `local` refuses more than four requests in","m":"both"},{"p":"See and filter your usage","h":"Filters","u":"observability.html#filters","x":"Every filter takes one value or several (comma-separated, or the flag repeated) and matches any of them. Different filters must all match. Ask about Flags A time range `--since`, `--until` (a UTC day such as `2026-10-01`, or a time; `--until` is exclusive), or `--last 7d`, `--last 24h`, `--last 90m` Where it went `--pr","m":"both"},{"p":"See and filter your usage","h":"Choose the figures","u":"observability.html#choose-the-figures","x":"`--metrics` picks the columns. The default is requests, errors, the four token counts that matter most, AI units, the list-price estimate and the 95th percentile of latency; `maple-proxy usage query --help` lists them all. Some useful ones: Metric What it is `error_rate`, `fallbacks`, `cached_responses` How often thing","m":"both"},{"p":"See and filter your usage","h":"Table, JSON or CSV","u":"observability.html#table-json-or-csv","x":"`--format table` (the default) is for reading. `--format json` gives the same groups and totals with a `meta` part that says what was read, for a script; `--format csv` is for a spreadsheet:","m":"both"},{"p":"See and filter your usage","h":"See what it read","u":"observability.html#see-what-it-read","x":"`--timing` adds a line after the answer: how long it took, how many daily files it read, how many came from the daily summaries (see below ), how much it read, and how many lines matched. It goes to standard error, so it never ends up in a file you redirect the answer into. Time to first token and time on the provider,","m":"both"},{"p":"See and filter your usage","h":"Notes on every answer","u":"observability.html#notes-on-every-answer","x":"Every answer ends with notes on what its figures cannot show: that percentiles are approximate, that figures named `_estimated` are estimates, that some matching lines were written before a column existed (and so have no time to first token, for example), or that a line could not be read. Read them before you compare t","m":"both"},{"p":"See and filter your usage","h":"Export only what you need","u":"observability.html#export-only-what-you-need","x":"`usage export` takes the same filters, so the file holds only the matching requests: Exports read and write a day at a time, so a long range does not have to fit in memory. Without a filter, an export is exactly what it was before this release. Formats and columns: Export to CSV or Parquet . `usage status` takes them t","m":"both"},{"p":"See and filter your usage","h":"Ready-made views in DuckDB","u":"observability.html#ready-made-views-in-duckdb","x":"For your own questions in SQL, DuckDB reads the log directly. `--views` adds a set of named views, in a schema of their own called `maple`, so they never replace a view of yours: View One row per Good for `maple.requests` request Every column, plus the UTC `day` and the `outcome` `maple.daily_spend` day, provider, mode","m":"both"},{"p":"See and filter your usage","h":"Lots of history? Use the DuckDB file or monthly Parquet","u":"observability.html#lots-of-history-use-the-duckdb-file-or-monthly-parquet","x":"The views above read one daily file per day. Over months of history, or on a hub that keeps what the proxies below it send up, that is many files. Pack them instead: Both hold exactly what the daily files hold, and both follow the cleanup. Set Analytics files on the Usage data card to have the proxy refresh them once a","m":"both"},{"p":"See and filter your usage","h":"Several pods? Query Postgres","u":"observability.html#several-pods-query-postgres","x":"In central mode the usage of every replica is in PostgreSQL. The same views over it, the connection string read from an environment variable when you query: See Several pods? .","m":"both"},{"p":"See and filter your usage","h":"Analytics with DuckLake","u":"observability.html#analytics-with-ducklake","x":"A DuckLake keeps your usage history as Parquet data files and a catalog, with snapshots you can read as they were, long after the daily files' retention has removed them. `maple-proxy usage lake` makes one and keeps it current with your own `duckdb` command line; the proxy never installs or downloads DuckDB. On one mac","m":"both"},{"p":"See and filter your usage","h":"The events log","u":"observability.html#the-events-log","x":"Things that happen that are not requests go to their own daily files, `events-YYYY-MM-DD.jsonl`, in the same folder as the usage log. They follow the usage log: on when it is on, off when it is off, and kept for the same number of days. The usage files still hold requests only, so counting their lines still counts requ","m":"both"},{"p":"See and filter your usage","h":"Why the cache missed","u":"observability.html#why-the-cache-missed","x":"When a provider's prompt cache misses, the usage log says why in `cache_reason`, and since this release one level finer in `cache_detail`. `maple-proxy usage cache-misses` sums it up: how often the cache missed, by client, client version and person, the most common reasons, and an estimated cost of the misses. It takes","m":"both"},{"p":"See and filter your usage","h":"Compare before and after","u":"observability.html#compare-before-and-after","x":"`maple-proxy usage compare` sets two windows of your usage side by side, per request, to see what changed after you changed a setting: Every answer says measured on your traffic, observational, not a controlled test , and means it. Two weeks differ in more than one setting: the work, the provider's caching and prices, ","m":"both"},{"p":"See and filter your usage","h":"How auto did","u":"observability.html#how-auto-did","x":"`maple-proxy usage auto-report` says how requests routed by `auto` turned out, per weight and model: how often they failed, needed a fallback, were sent again within a minute, or were followed by you switching model (Maple Harness tells the proxy when you switch with `/model`; other clients do not), and how long their ","m":"both"},{"p":"See and filter your usage","h":"The daily summaries","u":"observability.html#the-daily-summaries","x":"To answer quickly over months of data, `usage query` keeps a summary of each finished UTC day in your user cache folder: `~/.cache/maple-harness/usage-index/` on Linux (or under `$XDG_CACHE_HOME`), `~/Library/Caches/maple-harness/usage-index/` on macOS. They are private to you, and they are never put next to the usage ","m":"both"},{"p":"See and filter your usage","h":"What it cannot tell you yet","u":"observability.html#what-it-cannot-tell-you-yet","x":"The newer columns start with this release. Time to first token, time on the provider, the error class, the routing reason and the client's version are recorded from schema version 4 on. Lines written by earlier releases have them empty (NULL), never 0, and an answer's notes say how many matching lines are like that. Pe","m":"both"},{"p":"See and filter your usage","h":"Related","u":"observability.html#related","x":"Your usage data · Proxy settings · What /cost tells you · See and reduce what a task costs · Your data · Query your usage · The Maple data lake","m":"both"},{"p":"What the proxy adds for other tools","h":"What the proxy adds for other tools","u":"other-tools.html","x":"Claude Code, OpenCode, pi, GitHub Copilot CLI and Hermes can send their requests through the Maple proxy instead of straight to a provider. Use your models from other tools has a tested setup for each. This page says what they get from it, and what stays in Maple Harness. Other tools that speak the OpenAI or Anthropic ","m":"single"},{"p":"What the proxy adds for other tools","h":"In plain words","u":"other-tools.html#in-plain-words","x":"What it is. Claude Code, OpenCode, pi, Copilot CLI and Hermes can send their requests through the Maple proxy instead of straight to a provider. Why you want it. They get every model the proxy serves, its fallbacks when an account runs out, its budgets, and their usage on the same charts as Maple's. What to do. Point t","m":"single"},{"p":"What the proxy adds for other tools","h":"At a glance","u":"other-tools.html#at-a-glance","x":"What each tool sent was checked with Claude Code 2.1.284, OpenCode 1.18.33, pi 0.87.1, Copilot CLI 1.0.89 and Hermes Agent 0.19.0. Other versions may send different headers or cache marks. Claude Code OpenCode pi Copilot CLI Hermes Every model the proxy serves Yes Yes Yes Yes Yes Aliases, fallbacks, budgets Once you se","m":"single"},{"p":"What the proxy adds for other tools","h":"One address for many models","u":"other-tools.html#one-address-for-many-models","x":"The tool asks for a model by name, and the proxy sends the request to whichever provider serves it: your GitHub Copilot account, an Anthropic account, or a model server such as Ollama, LM Studio or oMLX. The proxy translates between the two APIs in both directions. Claude Code, which speaks the Anthropic API, can use a","m":"single"},{"p":"What the proxy adds for other tools","h":"Keeps going when an account runs out","u":"other-tools.html#keeps-going-when-an-account-runs-out","x":"Fallbacks apply to every request through the proxy, whatever tool sent it. With `model_fallbacks`, a model that cannot answer (out of chats or credit, overloaded, unreachable) hands the request to the next name on its list. `provider_fallbacks` moves one account's requests to another account. A model that has just said","m":"single"},{"p":"What the proxy adds for other tools","h":"Budgets","u":"other-tools.html#budgets","x":"A daily limit on a provider (requests, AI units, or both) counts every request to that provider through the proxy, whichever tool sent it. At the limit, requests are refused or handed to a fallback. See Daily budgets .","m":"single"},{"p":"What the proxy adds for other tools","h":"See what each tool spends","u":"other-tools.html#see-what-each-tool-spends","x":"Every request through the proxy gets one line in the usage log: the model asked for and the model that answered, tokens in and out, tokens read from and written to the cache, AI units when the provider reports them, and estimated cost and savings columns. The log keeps counts and labels, never prompts or answers. You c","m":"single"},{"p":"What the proxy adds for other tools","h":"Prompt cache","u":"other-tools.html#prompt-cache","x":"Providers charge less for the start of a request they have already seen. For Claude models, reached through an Anthropic account or through Copilot, the provider caches only the parts of a request that are marked for caching. The proxy marks them (`prompt_cache`, on by default): Claude Code marks its own system prompt.","m":"single"},{"p":"What the proxy adds for other tools","h":"Settings you can turn on","u":"other-tools.html#settings-you-can-turn-on","x":"These are off by default. When on, they apply to every tool. Image shrinking (`image_resize_tool`, `image_resize_user`, `image_superseded`) sends smaller images. Small text in a screenshot may become harder to read. Snapshot trimming (`snapshot_trim`) shortens browser page snapshots from Playwright MCP. It only acts on","m":"single"},{"p":"What the proxy adds for other tools","h":"Using it from another machine","u":"other-tools.html#using-it-from-another-machine","x":"To reach the proxy from another machine, give the tool an access token as its API key, in place of the placeholder. The token's label then names the tool in the usage log's `client` column. The setup page itself is reachable only from the proxy's own machine, and you can protect it with a password . See Using it from a","m":"single"},{"p":"What the proxy adds for other tools","h":"What stays in Maple Harness","u":"other-tools.html#what-stays-in-maple-harness","x":"These belong to the agent, not the proxy. Pointing another tool at the proxy does not give it any of them: `history_search` over earlier conversations, and the session journal of what each turn read, changed and ran. Memory notes and the project index as the agent uses them: recalled during a turn, and kept current aft","m":"single"},{"p":"What the proxy adds for other tools","h":"Measure it yourself","u":"other-tools.html#measure-it-yourself","x":"The measured comparison on this website is between Maple Harness and Copilot CLI. What the proxy changes for these tools on their own has not been measured, so this page gives no figure. To measure it on your own work, use \u00000\u0000 . It can run another tool as one arm, with the same tasks and the same check, several times e","m":"single"},{"p":"Check it yourself","h":"Check it yourself","u":"proof.html","x":"Maple Harness is built to make a task cost fewer tokens without making the work worse. This page says what that means, how to check it on your own tasks, and what we hold ourselves to. It makes no promise about your bill: that depends on your tasks and your models.","m":"single"},{"p":"Check it yourself","h":"In plain words","u":"proof.html#in-plain-words","x":"What it is. How to check, on your own tasks, whether a setting saves tokens without making the work worse. What to do. Watch `/cost` and the setup page's Cache tab, then compare runs with `maple-harness bench`. Nothing here is a promise about your bill.","m":"single"},{"p":"Check it yourself","h":"What we aim for","u":"proof.html#what-we-aim-for","x":"Efficient. Maple Harness is built to spend fewer tokens for the same work: it keeps requests in a form your provider can reuse, keeps the conversation lean, and stops waste before it compounds. Efficacious. It does not trade quality for tokens: it does not ask the model to talk in shorthand, what the model sees is cut ","m":"single"},{"p":"Check it yourself","h":"Don't take our word for it","u":"proof.html#dont-take-our-word-for-it","x":"Please be diligent. A saving that only shows up in one request, or one run, is not a saving. Here is how to check. Compare whole runs, three against three. Pick a task you run often. Run it three times with a setting on and three times with it off, for example trimming of old tool output, or letting the agent choose th","m":"single"},{"p":"Check it yourself","h":"What we hold ourselves to","u":"proof.html#what-we-hold-ourselves-to","x":"We publish results only from repeated, paired runs on real tasks, including the runs where a setting did not help, with the uncertainty shown. A technique is on by default only if quality is not worse. Nothing here is a promise about your bill.","m":"single"},{"p":"Keep it running in the background","h":"Keep it running in the background","u":"proxy-service.html","x":"By default the agent starts the proxy for you when you open it, and it stops when you are done. Run the proxy as a service when you want it to be already there: before you open a terminal, for other tools that use your models, or for your phone. `maple-harness service install` does it in one command. It writes one smal","m":"both"},{"p":"Keep it running in the background","h":"In plain words","u":"proxy-service.html#in-plain-words","x":"What it is. A way to keep the proxy (and the phone gateway) running in the background, started when you log in, instead of only while the agent is open. Why you want it. Your phone, Claude Code or another tool can use your models without a Maple session open. What to do. `maple-harness service install`, once. `maple-ha","m":"both"},{"p":"Keep it running in the background","h":"In short","u":"proxy-service.html#in-short","x":"`maple-harness service` keeps the proxy and the phone gateway running in the background and starts them when you log in. On Linux it writes systemd user units; on macOS, launchd agents . You do not need this to use the agent: it starts the proxy itself, and `/remote` starts the gateway. Install a service when you want ","m":"both"},{"p":"Keep it running in the background","h":"What you get","u":"proxy-service.html#what-you-get","x":"Linux macOS The file it writes `~/.config/systemd/user/maple-proxy.service` `~/Library/LaunchAgents/com.kinncj.maple-proxy.plist` Starts when you log in (at boot too, after one extra command below) when you log in If it stops with an error restarted 3 seconds later restarted 5 seconds later Logs `journalctl --user -u m","m":"both"},{"p":"Keep it running in the background","h":"Debian and Ubuntu","u":"proxy-service.html#debian-and-ubuntu","x":"Tested on: written from how the command works and standard systemd behaviour; not yet run on Debian or Ubuntu. Install the programs. This puts them in `/usr/local/bin` and asks for `sudo` once. To avoid `sudo`, add `-s -- --install-location ~/.local/bin` after `bash` and make sure `~/.local/bin` is on your `PATH`. Opti","m":"both"},{"p":"Keep it running in the background","h":"Arch Linux","u":"proxy-service.html#arch-linux","x":"Tested on: Arch Linux. The service files it writes pass `systemd-analyze verify`. The install and enable steps themselves have not been run on a real machine yet. Install the programs. Optional: shell sandbox. Install the service. Check it. Start at boot, not only at login. Arch has no firewall enabled by default. If y","m":"both"},{"p":"Keep it running in the background","h":"Fedora","u":"proxy-service.html#fedora","x":"Tested on: written from how the command works and standard systemd behaviour; not yet run on Fedora. Install the programs. Optional: shell sandbox. Install the service. Check it. Start at boot, not only at login. SELinux. A user service that runs a program from `/usr/local/bin` or `~/.local/bin` normally works with the","m":"both"},{"p":"Keep it running in the background","h":"macOS","u":"proxy-service.html#macos","x":"Tested on: written from how the command works and standard launchd behaviour; not yet run on a Mac. Install the programs. The programs are not notarised by Apple. The installer downloads them with `curl`, which normally does not trigger Gatekeeper. If macOS still refuses to open one, remove the quarantine mark from it:","m":"both"},{"p":"Keep it running in the background","h":"Keep the phone gateway running too","u":"proxy-service.html#keep-the-phone-gateway-running-too","x":"The gateway is the address your phone connects to ( Control it from your phone ). To keep it up as well: This also switches remote control on and starts the gateway listening on your network (port 11541) with HTTPS: a real certificate when Tailscale is signed in, otherwise a self-signed one that works after a browser w","m":"both"},{"p":"Keep it running in the background","h":"Machines with no desktop","u":"proxy-service.html#machines-with-no-desktop","x":"Your sign-ins and keys go in the system keyring when there is one, and the configuration file holds only a reference to them. A server usually has no keyring: no Secret Service is running on your user session, or it cannot be unlocked without someone at the keyboard. Then the proxy falls back to a private file in the c","m":"both"},{"p":"Keep it running in the background","h":"Reach the setup page from another machine","u":"proxy-service.html#reach-the-setup-page-from-another-machine","x":"The setup page listens on `127.0.0.1` only and refuses to listen anywhere else, so do not try to expose it. To use it on a server, forward the port over SSH and open it in your own browser: Then browse to `http://127.0.0.1:11539` on your own machine. Over Tailscale the same forward works with the machine's Tailscale na","m":"both"},{"p":"Keep it running in the background","h":"Update","u":"proxy-service.html#update","x":"Each program updates itself, then the service needs a restart to run the new copy: If `update` says permission denied for `/usr/local/bin`, run it with `sudo`, or install into `~/.local/bin` the first time. If you moved the programs, run `maple-harness service install` again so the file points at the new path.","m":"both"},{"p":"Keep it running in the background","h":"Troubleshooting","u":"proxy-service.html#troubleshooting","x":"`service install` says the systemd user manager is not reachable. There is no user session to attach to, typically over SSH or in a container. Log in through a normal session, or follow the SSH steps in the Debian and Ubuntu section. `maple-harness service print proxy` shows the file it would write so you can place it ","m":"both"},{"p":"Keep it running in the background","h":"Remove it","u":"proxy-service.html#remove-it","x":"This stops the services and removes the files it wrote. It leaves your configuration and sign-ins alone. On Linux, if you turned lingering on and no longer want it: `loginctl disable-linger \"$USER\"`.","m":"both"},{"p":"Keep it running in the background","h":"Advanced: one system-wide service","u":"proxy-service.html#advanced-one-system-wide-service","x":"Tested on: the unit text was checked with `systemd-analyze verify` on Arch Linux. Nothing else here has been run. Use the per-user service above unless you need this. A per-user service is the right default. If you want the proxy to run under its own account, for example on a server that nobody logs in to, you can writ","m":"both"},{"p":"Proxy settings","h":"Proxy settings","u":"proxy-settings.html","x":"This is the complete list of what you can change on the proxy: the cards on its setup page, the keys in `config.yaml`, the commands, and the response headers you can read. For a guided tour, read The proxy and its setup page first. The proxy and the harness share one `config.yaml`. A save on the setup page keeps the `h","m":"both"},{"p":"Proxy settings","h":"In plain words","u":"proxy-settings.html#in-plain-words","x":"What it is. The complete list of the proxy's settings, cards, commands and response headers. What to do. On the setup page you rarely need this page: each card explains itself, Advanced lists every setting with its default and when it applies, and Find a setting finds one by a word. Come here for the exact key, the def","m":"both"},{"p":"Proxy settings","h":"Settings at a glance","u":"proxy-settings.html#settings-at-a-glance","x":"On the setup page, the Proxy settings and Auto mode cards are on the Models & routing tab, and Providers is its own tab. Every key in this table is also listed on Advanced , which edits the ones that have no card of their own and says where to change the rest. Find a setting , in the page's menu, finds any of them by n","m":"both"},{"p":"Proxy settings","h":"Listeners and ports","u":"proxy-settings.html#listeners-and-ports","x":"Key Default What it is for `server.openai_compat` `127.0.0.1:11535` The OpenAI-compatible API (`/v1`). The harness, and any tool that speaks that API, use it `server.anthropic_compat` `127.0.0.1:11537` The Anthropic-compatible API. Claude Code and similar tools use it `server.webui` `127.0.0.1:11539` The setup page. It","m":"both"},{"p":"Proxy settings","h":"Providers and models","u":"proxy-settings.html#providers-and-models","x":"A provider is a backend that serves models. The setup page's Providers card builds these for you; this is what the keys mean if you edit the file. Key Default What it does `providers[].id` required The provider's name, for example `github-copilot` or `ollama-main` `providers[].type` required What it is: `github-copilot","m":"both"},{"p":"Proxy settings","h":"Caching","u":"proxy-settings.html#caching","x":"Long conversations resend the same beginning on every turn. Providers that cache charge far less for the part they have already seen. These settings help that happen; the Cache card shows whether it did. Key Setup page Default What it does Trade-off Helps `providers[].prompt_cache` Caching, a switch per provider `auto`","m":"both"},{"p":"Proxy settings","h":"Images and browser snapshots","u":"proxy-settings.html#images-and-browser-snapshots","x":"A model is charged for an image by its size in pixels, not by its file size, and a large image is scaled down by the provider before the model looks at it. Browser tools also return page snapshots (a text outline of the page) that are often the largest thing an agent resends. These settings shrink both. All of them are","m":"both"},{"p":"Proxy settings","h":"Cost visibility","u":"proxy-settings.html#cost-visibility","x":"Copilot states what a call cost, in AI units, on every response. Anthropic's own API never does — it reports token counts only. `/cost` (see What /cost tells you ) still shows a per-request figure for Anthropic when this is on, but it is a guess from Anthropic's published prices, not a bill, and is always shown labelle","m":"both"},{"p":"Proxy settings","h":"Fallbacks","u":"proxy-settings.html#fallbacks","x":"Key Setup page Default What it does Trade-off Helps `model_fallbacks` Fallbacks none For a model or alias, the names to try in order when it cannot answer: its account is out of chats or credit, the backend is overloaded, or it is unreachable A fallback may be a weaker or a different model, so results can differ. It ha","m":"both"},{"p":"Proxy settings","h":"Guards","u":"proxy-settings.html#guards","x":"Key Setup page Default What it does Trade-off Helps `context_guard` Guards, a three-way choice `off` For tools that do not know a model's window: `off` sends the request as it is, `truncate` drops the oldest whole turns until it fits, `error` refuses it with a plain message `truncate` silently forgets old turns; `error","m":"both"},{"p":"Proxy settings","h":"Budgets","u":"proxy-settings.html#budgets","x":"Key Setup page Default What it does Trade-off Helps `budgets[].provider` Budgets none Which provider the limit applies to Efficient `budgets[].max_requests_per_day` Budgets none (no limit) The most requests a day for that provider (days are UTC) When reached, requests are refused, or handed to that provider's fallback ","m":"both"},{"p":"Proxy settings","h":"Alerts","u":"proxy-settings.html#alerts","x":"Alerts only warn: they never refuse, reroute or slow a request (budgets do the refusing). Each alert fires once per rule and period, is written to the events log, and is sent to clients in `X-Maple-Alert` while it is in force; Maple Harness shows it once. With no budget and nothing set here, there are no alerts. See Al","m":"both"},{"p":"Proxy settings","h":"People and teams","u":"proxy-settings.html#people-and-teams","x":"All optional, and off until set: without them every request is served as before. See Teams . Key Setup page Default What it does Trade-off Helps `people[].id` People none A person, as the usage log's `person` column names them. `local` is a caller on this machine without a token Efficacious `people[].team` People none ","m":"both"},{"p":"Proxy settings","h":"Chained proxies","u":"proxy-settings.html#chained-proxies","x":"All optional: a proxy takes part in a chain only once it has a provider of type `maple` or a `proxy-chain` token. See Chain proxies into hubs . Key Setup page Default What it does Trade-off Helps `providers[].type: maple` Providers none Another Maple proxy used as a provider (a hub). `base_url` is its OpenAI-compatible","m":"both"},{"p":"Proxy settings","h":"MCP hub (experimental)","u":"proxy-settings.html#mcp-hub-experimental","x":"Off until `mcp_hub.enabled` is set; while off nothing listens and nothing starts. See MCP hub . Key Setup page Default What it does Trade-off Helps `mcp_hub.enabled` MCP hub, the switch off Serves the configured MCP servers' tools at one MCP endpoint A bridge from that endpoint to programs on this machine Efficient `mc","m":"both"},{"p":"Proxy settings","h":"Limits and hosts","u":"proxy-settings.html#limits-and-hosts","x":"These are read when the proxy starts. After changing them, stop and start `maple-proxy`. Key Setup page Default What it does Helps `server.rate_limit.enabled` Limits and hosts unset: off for this machine, on for other addresses Force the limit on or off Efficacious `server.rate_limit.requests_per_minute` Limits and hos","m":"both"},{"p":"Proxy settings","h":"The usage log","u":"proxy-settings.html#the-usage-log","x":"Key Setup page Default What it does Helps `server.usage_ledger` Usage data, Location (or Limits and hosts, Usage log) on: `usage/maple_harness_proxy_usage_ledger.jsonl` in your Maple Harness config folder Where the usage log goes: a file that receives one line of numbers per request (model, tokens, cache figures, AI un","m":"both"},{"p":"Proxy settings","h":"Search by meaning","u":"proxy-settings.html#search-by-meaning","x":"Which model ranks the harness's searches by meaning. See Search by meaning for what it does, both models, their licences (EmbeddingGemma is under the Gemma Terms of Use and the Gemma Prohibited Use Policy , and its download needs a Hugging Face token from an account that accepted them) and hardware guidance: EmbeddingG","m":"both"},{"p":"Proxy settings","h":"The setup page, card by card","u":"proxy-settings.html#the-setup-page-card-by-card","x":"Card What it shows and does Account (Providers tab) Sign in to GitHub Copilot (a browser flow) or save an Anthropic API key. The Copilot plan and, where the plan has one, the chats left and when they reset Providers (Providers tab) The configured backends and their models. Add a server, press Detect models , edit or de","m":"both"},{"p":"Proxy settings","h":"Single sign-on for the setup page","u":"proxy-settings.html#single-sign-on-for-the-setup-page","x":"For teams: admins sign in to the setup page with their company account, on an address of its own, and developers too when developer sign-in is on. Off unless `setup_page.sso.enabled` is true. Set on the Single sign-on tab's wizard on the proxy's own machine (never through single sign-on), or in `config.yaml`. Read at s","m":"both"},{"p":"Proxy settings","h":"Observability export","u":"proxy-settings.html#observability-export","x":"Prometheus metrics, an OpenTelemetry (OTLP) export and audit lines for a SIEM: counts, timings and labels, never content. All off by default; the setup page's Monitoring tab sets them up step by step, applied without a restart. The full guide, with what is exported and what never is: Metrics and telemetry export . Key ","m":"both"},{"p":"Proxy settings","h":"Containers and replicas","u":"proxy-settings.html#containers-and-replicas","x":"For running the proxy in a container or as several replicas; see Docker and Kubernetes . They are read when the proxy starts, and none of them is on the setup page: `storage` names where secrets come from and is never shown there. The environment variable in brackets wins over the file. Key Setup page Default What it d","m":"both"},{"p":"Proxy settings","h":"Commands","u":"proxy-settings.html#commands","x":"`maple-proxy` with no command starts the server. The others: Command What it does `maple-proxy serve` Start the proxy (the default). Flags: `-config` a config file; `-logs` where logs go (`false`, `true`, `stdout`, or a file path); `-log-bodies` also log prompt and answer text, which is off by default because a prompt ","m":"both"},{"p":"Proxy settings","h":"Response headers","u":"proxy-settings.html#response-headers","x":"The proxy adds these to responses so you can see what happened to a request. Header Meaning `X-Maple-First-Output-Timeout` On the model list (`GET /v1/models`): the longest time, in seconds, the proxy waits for any listed model's provider to start answering (`providers[].first_output_timeout_seconds`). The proxy sends ","m":"both"},{"p":"Proxy settings","h":"Files and environment","u":"proxy-settings.html#files-and-environment","x":"Where What `version` The configuration file's format version, currently `1` `~/.config/maple-harness/config.yaml` The proxy's and the harness's settings `~/.config/maple-harness/embeddings/` The installed built-in model for search by meaning, one folder per model, and the cache of computed vectors in `cache/`, private ","m":"both"},{"p":"The proxy and its setup page","h":"The proxy and its setup page","u":"proxy.html","x":"`maple-proxy` is the program that holds your sign-ins and talks to the models. The agent uses it, and so can any other tool that speaks the OpenAI or Anthropic API. Start it with: It stays in the foreground. Add `--logs=true` to watch requests, `--logs=/path/to.log` to keep a record, or `--logs=stdout` when something e","m":"both"},{"p":"The proxy and its setup page","h":"In plain words","u":"proxy.html#in-plain-words","x":"What it is. The proxy is the one program that holds your sign-ins (GitHub Copilot, Claude, your own model servers) and talks to the models. Maple Harness uses it, and so can Claude Code, OpenCode or any tool that speaks the OpenAI or Anthropic API. Why you want it. One place for your accounts and models; when one accou","m":"both"},{"p":"The proxy and its setup page","h":"What it listens on","u":"proxy.html#what-it-listens-on","x":"Address What it is Who may use it `127.0.0.1:11539` The setup page This machine only. It refuses to listen anywhere else `127.0.0.1:11535` OpenAI-compatible API (`/v1`) This machine; other machines only with an access token `127.0.0.1:11537` Anthropic-compatible API This machine; other machines only with an access toke","m":"both"},{"p":"The proxy and its setup page","h":"The setup page","u":"proxy.html#the-setup-page","x":"The setup page is the proxy's control panel, in your browser. Open `http://127.0.0.1:11539` on the machine running the proxy. Nothing to install, and it only answers on this machine. Watch tells you what is happening: how many requests, what they cost, how the cache and each provider are doing. Configure is where you c","m":"both"},{"p":"The proxy and its setup page","h":"The menu","u":"proxy.html#the-menu","x":"The tabs are in two groups. The address keeps the tab you are on (`#tab-usage`), so a tab can be bookmarked, and the arrow keys move between tabs. Group Tab What it holds Watch Overview Live tiles (requests per second, in flight, today's requests and AI units, the cache hit rate, failures in the last hour), a live char","m":"both"},{"p":"The proxy and its setup page","h":"What's configured","u":"proxy.html#whats-configured","x":"At the bottom of the Overview, What's configured lists the basics, each on , off or needs attention , with a link to where it is set: providers, auto mode, the usage log, budgets and alerts, search by meaning, remote control and the page password. Features you set up (sharing, hubs, single sign-on, monitoring, central ","m":"both"},{"p":"The proxy and its setup page","h":"Set up more","u":"proxy.html#set-up-more","x":"Set up more is where the features one person does not need begin: Share this proxy with a team , Connect to a hub, or serve other proxies , Single sign-on for admins , Monitoring and alert webhooks , Several replicas (central storage) and MCP hub (experimental) . Each card says whether it is on and opens its own tab, w","m":"both"},{"p":"The proxy and its setup page","h":"Advanced, and saved settings that wait for a restart","u":"proxy.html#advanced-and-saved-settings-that-wait-for-a-restart","x":"Advanced shows every setting the proxy has, grouped by tab, with a filter box. The ones without a card of their own (guards, caching, alert rules, where the proxy listens and its limits, trusted load balancers, chaining intervals, log format and level, image limits) can be changed there. Each says when it applies (at o","m":"both"},{"p":"The proxy and its setup page","h":"Charts and live figures","u":"proxy.html#charts-and-live-figures","x":"Every chart says whether its figures are measured or estimated , prints the notes the proxy gives with them (how many requests and daily files it read, and what the figures cannot show), and has a Table button that shows every number. Hover over a chart, or focus it and use the arrow keys, to read each point; click a l","m":"both"},{"p":"The proxy and its setup page","h":"What you can do from the Configure tabs","u":"proxy.html#what-you-can-do-from-the-configure-tabs","x":"Sign in to GitHub Copilot (a browser flow) or save an Anthropic API key, on Providers . See Sign in . Add model servers on Providers : pick the type (Ollama, LM Studio, oMLX, Lemonade, Unsloth, or any OpenAI-compatible server), press Detect models , and save. See Connect a model server . See your Copilot allowance : th","m":"both"},{"p":"The proxy and its setup page","h":"Filter the charts and cards","u":"proxy.html#filter-the-charts-and-cards","x":"Above the data tabs, pick a time range: Live (the default), 7, 30 or 90 days, a year, or Custom for a range of UTC days. With Live , the cards show the proxy's own figures since it started, exactly as before, and the charts show the last 30 days. More filters opens the rest: provider, account, model, token, agent, outc","m":"both"},{"p":"The proxy and its setup page","h":"Provider health","u":"proxy.html#provider-health","x":"On the Health tab, the charts show the time to an answer and to the first output per day (the median, with the 95th percentile as a band), failures by class, and the share of each model's requests that failed. The Health card lists each provider and model that has answered through this proxy since it started: its state","m":"both"},{"p":"The proxy and its setup page","h":"Alerts and forecasts","u":"proxy.html#alerts-and-forecasts","x":"On the Costs & budgets tab, the charts show AI units and the estimated list-price cost per day (with a line at an alert amount you set) and today's use of each budget, with a typical day once there are three days of usage. The Alerts card warns when a budget reaches 80% and 100% (the default), and, if you set them unde","m":"both"},{"p":"The proxy and its setup page","h":"Compare two ranges","u":"proxy.html#compare-two-ranges","x":"On the Costs & budgets tab, Compare two ranges sets the days before and after a change you made and compares cost and tokens per request between them, on your own traffic, narrowed by the filter bar. Requests are compared like for like (the same kind of client, model and size of work), and the answer refuses rather tha","m":"both"},{"p":"The proxy and its setup page","h":"People and teams","u":"proxy.html#people-and-teams","x":"To share one proxy with other people, open Set up more > Share this proxy with a team and add a person. The People & teams tab then stays in the menu, with charts of AI units by person and requests by team, each budget's use today and this month, and the People card: each person, their team, their tokens (by label, nev","m":"both"},{"p":"The proxy and its setup page","h":"MCP hub (experimental)","u":"proxy.html#mcp-hub-experimental","x":"Once `mcp_hub:` is set up in `config.yaml` (or with `maple-proxy mcp-hub add`), the MCP hub tab shows whether the hub is on, its address, and each server with its state and tools. You can switch the hub or a server on and off there. Servers cannot be added from the page: a server can be a program the proxy runs, so the","m":"both"},{"p":"The proxy and its setup page","h":"Protect the setup page with a password","u":"proxy.html#protect-the-setup-page-with-a-password","x":"By default the setup page has no password: any program or browser on this computer can open it and change what it manages. To require one, open Security & access > Protect this page , type a password twice (at least 8 characters) and press Turn on . The browser you did it in stays signed in for 30 days; any other brows","m":"both"},{"p":"The proxy and its setup page","h":"Settings you may want","u":"proxy.html#settings-you-may-want","x":"Everything below is a card under Proxy settings , on the setup page's Models & routing tab (the settings without a card are on Advanced ). Each control has a one-line explanation and a link back here. You do not need to edit any file. When you change something, nothing is written yet. Press Review changes to see exactl","m":"both"},{"p":"The proxy and its setup page","h":"Prompt caching","u":"proxy.html#prompt-caching","x":"Long conversations resend the same beginning on every turn, and providers that cache charge much less for the part they have already seen. The Caching card has a switch per provider, shown only where it can be controlled (Claude through the Anthropic API or through Copilot); it is on by default. Keys: `prompt_cache`, `","m":"both"},{"p":"The proxy and its setup page","h":"Model aliases","u":"proxy.html#model-aliases","x":"An alias is a short, fixed name for a model, so a tool can ask for `local` or `cloud` and you can change what that means later without touching the tool. Set them on the Model aliases card, or with `model_aliases` and a model's own `alias` in the file. See Providers and models .","m":"both"},{"p":"The proxy and its setup page","h":"Fallbacks","u":"proxy.html#fallbacks","x":"A fallback tells the proxy what to try when a model cannot answer: its account is out of chats or credit, the backend is overloaded or unreachable, or it answers that it does not serve that model (from 2.1.2). `model_fallbacks` lists other models to try for a model; `provider_fallbacks` sends one account's requests to ","m":"both"},{"p":"The proxy and its setup page","h":"Auto mode at the proxy","u":"proxy.html#auto-mode-at-the-proxy","x":"The proxy has four built-in model names that choose the model for you. Any tool that talks to the proxy can ask for them: Name What answers `auto` The proxy looks at each request and sends it to the heavy, medium or light model below `auto-heavy` The model for planning, diagnosing and deciding `auto-medium` The model f","m":"both"},{"p":"The proxy and its setup page","h":"Set up auto mode","u":"proxy.html#set-up-auto-mode","x":"Open the setup page (`http://127.0.0.1:11539`) open Models & routing and choose Auto mode in the row of links at the top of the tab. With nothing set, each weight shows Use defaults with the model it uses, the fallbacks under Then try (defaults) , and a preview of where a request goes. The note above the weights names ","m":"both"},{"p":"The proxy and its setup page","h":"Copilot Auto","u":"proxy.html#copilot-auto","x":"GitHub Copilot's own automatic mode, where Copilot picks the model for each request, is listed as a model of each Copilot account: `github-copilot/auto`, when your account offers it. It never takes the plain name `auto`, which is the proxy's own auto mode (or your alias of that name). You can choose it in two ways: As ","m":"both"},{"p":"The proxy and its setup page","h":"See what auto chose","u":"proxy.html#see-what-auto-chose","x":"Four places tell you where each request went and which model answered. The auto report. On the setup page, the Models & routing tab shows, above the Auto mode card, where auto sent requests (by weight and the model that answered) and what happened after each choice: how often the request failed, fell back to another mo","m":"both"},{"p":"The proxy and its setup page","h":"Guards","u":"proxy.html#guards","x":"The Context guard (`context_guard`) is for tools that do not know a model's window: it sends the request as is, drops the oldest turns until it fits, or refuses it. The Repeat guard (`degenerate_guard`, off by default) stops a reply that has collapsed into repeating itself. See Guards .","m":"both"},{"p":"The proxy and its setup page","h":"Keeping the cache useful","u":"proxy.html#keeping-the-cache-useful","x":"Two settings on the Guards card help a provider's prompt cache on long conversations: the Prefix stabiliser (`prefix_stabilizer`, on by default) keeps the start of each request the same from turn to turn, and Trim old tool results (`context_trim`, off by default) replaces old, large tool results with a short note. Resp","m":"both"},{"p":"The proxy and its setup page","h":"Images and browser snapshots","u":"proxy.html#images-and-browser-snapshots","x":"Four choices on the Guards card, all off by default, shrink images and browser page snapshots before they are sent: `image_resize_tool`, `image_resize_user`, `image_superseded` and `snapshot_trim`. Smaller images cost fewer tokens, and small text in a screenshot may become harder to read. See Images and browser snapsho","m":"both"},{"p":"The proxy and its setup page","h":"Seeing how the cache is doing","u":"proxy.html#seeing-how-the-cache-is-doing","x":"On the Cache tab, the charts show the share of input read from the cache per day, the tokens read from and written to it, what reading from it saved and what writing to it cost (both estimated), and how each request did. The Cache card shows today's hit rate, the tokens read from and written to the cache, a table per m","m":"both"},{"p":"The proxy and its setup page","h":"Seeing cost per session","u":"proxy.html#seeing-cost-per-session","x":"The Sessions card groups AI units, tokens and requests by session. Maple Harness sends a random session id with every request (`X-Maple-Session`), and a subagent sends its parent's id, so its spend counts toward the session that started it. Any other client can send the header too. See What /cost tells you .","m":"both"},{"p":"The proxy and its setup page","h":"Seeing what it saves","u":"proxy.html#seeing-what-it-saves","x":"The Savings card, first on the page, shows what the proxy's techniques saved and what they cost, across every session on this proxy. Every figure is tagged measured (a count, or what the provider billed) or estimated (a value in AI units, estimated from what your own requests cost). The estimate is never more than what","m":"both"},{"p":"The proxy and its setup page","h":"Daily budgets","u":"proxy.html#daily-budgets","x":"For each provider you can cap requests per day, AI units per day, or both (days are UTC). At the limit, requests are refused or handed to its fallback. Key: `budgets`. See Budgets .","m":"both"},{"p":"The proxy and its setup page","h":"Limits and hosts (restart required)","u":"proxy.html#limits-and-hosts-restart-required","x":"The rate limit (off for this machine, on for other machines by default) and allowed hosts (extra names the proxy answers to) are read when the proxy starts, so restart it after saving. The usage log , one line of counts per request, is on by default and applies as soon as you save. See Limits and hosts , The usage log ","m":"both"},{"p":"The proxy and its setup page","h":"Using it from another machine","u":"proxy.html#using-it-from-another-machine","x":"By default only this machine can reach the model APIs. To let another machine on your network use them, set the listen address and create an access token with the `proxy` scope on the setup page: The proxy refuses to start on a non-loopback address until such a token exists, and callers send it as `Authorization: Beare","m":"both"},{"p":"The proxy and its setup page","h":"Connect the harness to a proxy","u":"proxy.html#connect-the-harness-to-a-proxy","x":"In plain words. Maple Harness uses its own proxy on this machine, as it always has: it starts it when nothing is running, and you set nothing. That is the normal way, also in a team: signing in to a team hub happens at your local proxy (on its setup page, with `maple-proxy chain connect --sso`, or with the shortcut `ma","m":"both"},{"p":"The proxy and its setup page","h":"Verified and unverified proxies","u":"proxy.html#verified-and-unverified-proxies","x":"In plain words. Maple Harness checks, every time it connects, that the proxy on this machine is your own. When it is, you see nothing: that is the normal case. When it is not (it is an older release, or it runs as another user, in a container, or with a different settings folder), the harness calls it unverified . Befo","m":"both"},{"p":"The proxy and its setup page","h":"Check the cache yourself","u":"proxy.html#check-the-cache-yourself","x":"Providers cache the start of a request that they have seen recently, and reading from that cache costs a fraction of processing it again. Whether that is working for your account and your model is easy to see: It sends the same small request twice, a few seconds apart, through the running proxy, and prints what each ca","m":"both"},{"p":"The proxy and its setup page","h":"Other useful commands","u":"proxy.html#other-useful-commands","x":"","m":"both"},{"p":"Query your usage","h":"Query your usage","u":"querying.html","x":"A cookbook of questions people ask of their usage, each with the command and what it printed. Every recipe here was run as shown; the output comes from a synthetic sample log (made-up people, models and traffic), so your figures will differ. Which store to query when is on Your data . Figures are AI units as the provid","m":"both"},{"p":"Query your usage","h":"In plain words","u":"querying.html#in-plain-words","x":"What it is. Recipes: the questions people ask of their usage, each with the command and its output. What to do. Copy a recipe and run it. `maple-proxy usage query` needs nothing else; the DuckDB recipes need the `duckdb` program. The setup page's Data & storage tab has the commands to start DuckDB on your own log.","m":"both"},{"p":"Query your usage","h":"Before you start","u":"querying.html#before-you-start","x":"`maple-proxy usage query` needs nothing else. It reads the daily files on this machine, and in central mode the shared PostgreSQL, so run it on a replica there. It works whether the proxy is running or not. DuckDB recipes need DuckDB's command line ( install it ), plus `INSTALL ducklake` once for the lake and `INSTALL ","m":"both"},{"p":"Query your usage","h":"With usage query","u":"querying.html#with-usage-query","x":"The filters and `--group-by` keys are listed in Filter your data ; `--metrics` picks the columns (`maple-proxy usage query --help` lists them all).","m":"both"},{"p":"Query your usage","h":"Spend by person this week","u":"querying.html#spend-by-person-this-week","x":"On a team's proxy (here a replica in central mode, reading the shared PostgreSQL): The same question by team, on a replica in central mode: every replica's usage, from PostgreSQL. `unattributed` is requests no person was recorded for: sent before people were set up, or with an access token tied to no person. `local` (n","m":"both"},{"p":"Query your usage","h":"Top models by cost","u":"querying.html#top-models-by-cost","x":"`--limit` keeps the biggest groups by request count. A local model reports no AI units (`0.000`), and a provider without list prices has no `cost_estimated` (`-`). `list_cost_usd` is the request priced at the provider's published list prices whether or not `estimate_cost` is on (the figure monthly dollar budgets count,","m":"both"},{"p":"Query your usage","h":"Prompt-cache hit rate by tool","u":"querying.html#prompt-cache-hit-rate-by-tool","x":"`hit_rate` is the share of input tokens read from the provider's prompt cache; `lost` counts requests whose cache was lost. Why a cache missed: Why the cache missed .","m":"both"},{"p":"Query your usage","h":"Errors by provider in the last 24 hours","u":"querying.html#errors-by-provider-in-the-last-24-hours","x":"`--last 24h` is a rolling window; `--last 1d` would be today since midnight UTC. The Health tab and Provider health show the same over time.","m":"both"},{"p":"Query your usage","h":"Fallbacks, and why","u":"querying.html#fallbacks-and-why","x":"Each line is a request a fallback answered; `route_reason` names the class of error that sent it there. Add `model` to `--group-by` to see which model stepped in.","m":"both"},{"p":"Query your usage","h":"What auto chose","u":"querying.html#what-auto-chose","x":"`maple-proxy usage auto-report` and How auto did go further.","m":"both"},{"p":"Query your usage","h":"A roll-up per hub","u":"querying.html#a-roll-up-per-hub","x":"On a top hub with proxies below it , by the proxy each request started on: Use `client_requests` and `ai_units_spent` to count each request and its spend once; `requests` counts a line on every proxy a request passed through. Add `person` to `--group-by` for who asked.","m":"both"},{"p":"Query your usage","h":"With DuckDB","u":"querying.html#with-duckdb","x":"The same records, in plain SQL. Each source below gives you a `usage` table or view and the named views (`maple.requests`, `maple.daily_spend`, `maple.sessions`, `maple.people` and the rest), so a query written for one works on the others.","m":"both"},{"p":"Query your usage","h":"The daily files","u":"querying.html#the-daily-files","x":"Spend by provider, week by week (saved as `weekly-provider.sql`): The cache hit rate by tool, counted in requests rather than tokens (saved as `cache-by-tool.sql`): Over many months the daily files are many files; `maple-proxy usage compact` packs them into one Parquet file per month, and `--source parquet-monthly` mak","m":"both"},{"p":"Query your usage","h":"The DuckDB file","u":"querying.html#the-duckdb-file","x":"`maple-proxy usage duckdb` loads the daily files into one DuckDB file and, run again, adds only what they gained: The two biggest models of each month (saved as `monthly-model.sql`): July starts part way through: the DuckDB file follows the daily files, and the 90-day cleanup had deleted the days before July 11. `-read","m":"both"},{"p":"Query your usage","h":"The lake","u":"querying.html#the-lake","x":"Attach it read-only ( how ), and the same query reaches back past the cleanup: The lake in DuckDB: its snapshots, the lake as it was at one of them, and history from before the 90-day cleanup. Time travel: the same table as it was at an earlier snapshot (saved as `then-and-now.sql`; list the snapshots with `FROM maple_","m":"both"},{"p":"Query your usage","h":"PostgreSQL","u":"querying.html#postgresql","x":"In central mode, two ways to read the shared PostgreSQL from DuckDB, both read-only. Use a role that can only read ( the grants ). The named views over PostgreSQL. The connection string stays in an environment variable and is read when you query; it is never written into the SQL: Spend by person per week for one team (","m":"both"},{"p":"Query your usage","h":"Into a spreadsheet","u":"querying.html#into-a-spreadsheet","x":"A summary as CSV. Any `usage query` prints CSV with `--format csv`; the notes go to the terminal, not the file: Every request as CSV (or Parquet), with every column of the usage record : An Excel file from DuckDB , from any source above. DuckDB's `excel` extension writes `.xlsx` (`INSTALL excel` once). From the lake, a","m":"both"},{"p":"Query your usage","h":"Related","u":"querying.html#related","x":"Your data : where the data lives, and which store to use. The Maple data lake : set up the lake, on one machine or for a team. See and filter your usage and Your usage data : the filters, views, columns and Grafana.","m":"both"},{"p":"Control it from your phone","h":"Control it from your phone","u":"remote-control.html","x":"Share a session and drive it from your phone's browser or from another machine. You can approve tool calls (with a look at exactly what will change), open several sessions in one list, and start new ones in folders you have allowed.","m":"single"},{"p":"Control it from your phone","h":"In plain words","u":"remote-control.html#in-plain-words","x":"What it is. Follow a session from your phone, approve what the agent wants to do (with the exact change in front of you), and start new sessions in folders you allowed. Why you want it. Leave your desk while a long task runs, and still answer its questions. The clicks. On the setup page, Security & access , Remote acce","m":"single"},{"p":"Control it from your phone","h":"Recommended setup: Tailscale","u":"remote-control.html#recommended-setup-tailscale","x":"Put the computer and the phone on the same Tailscale network. Nothing is exposed to your local network or the internet, the traffic is already encrypted, and Tailscale can issue a real HTTPS certificate for your machine, which is what lets a phone show notifications with the page closed. Install Tailscale on the comput","m":"single"},{"p":"Control it from your phone","h":"Sharing a session","u":"remote-control.html#sharing-a-session","x":"Inside a running session, type: `/remote` starts the gateway if it is not running. The gateway is the one address on your machine that lists every shared session, so a phone sees them all. A session shared through it stays shared until you turn it off or close the session. To start without any session open, use `maple-","m":"single"},{"p":"Control it from your phone","h":"Pairing","u":"remote-control.html#pairing","x":"A pairing code (eight characters like `K7QM-4XPT`) works once and expires after five minutes. The phone trades it for a token of its own, which you can see and revoke in the device list on the setup page at `http://127.0.0.1:11539`. Scan the QR code, open the link, or type the code. Turning Remote control on in the set","m":"single"},{"p":"Control it from your phone","h":"Sharing one session","u":"remote-control.html#sharing-one-session","x":"You can give someone a link to one session, and nothing else. On the remote page, open the session and tap Share (the arrow button at the top; a watch-only device does not have it). Give the share a label, such as who it is for, and choose what they can do: Watch only (the default): they follow the session live and can","m":"single"},{"p":"Control it from your phone","h":"A device for one session","u":"remote-control.html#a-device-for-one-session","x":"When you want a device of your own (a tablet, a second phone, a work laptop's browser) to reach one session and nothing else, pair it for that session only. On the remote page, open the session, tap Share , give it a label (which device it is), and choose My device, this session only . If other sessions are running, yo","m":"single"},{"p":"Control it from your phone","h":"Starting sessions from the phone","u":"remote-control.html#starting-sessions-from-the-phone","x":"The New session sheet starts a session in a folder you have allowed: A phone can only start sessions in those folders, and never anywhere else. A folder inside one of them that does not exist yet, such as a new project, is created when the session starts. Resuming a saved session whose folder has been deleted is refuse","m":"single"},{"p":"Control it from your phone","h":"Starting in yolo mode","u":"remote-control.html#starting-in-yolo-mode","x":"Yolo mode allows every tool call that would otherwise ask, without asking. Your `deny` rules still apply. Because a stray tap on a phone is easy, starting a session as yolo from the phone is off until you turn it on for the machine: Restart the gateway after changing it. The sheet then offers Start in yolo mode , asks ","m":"single"},{"p":"Control it from your phone","h":"Notifications","u":"remote-control.html#notifications","x":"Turn on Notify me when this session needs me in the session sheet. When the page is in the background you get a notification, a vibration and a count in the tab title when a session needs approval, stops with an error or finishes. It says only that, plus the project's name, never your commands or paths. With the page c","m":"single"},{"p":"Control it from your phone","h":"Using the page","u":"remote-control.html#using-the-page","x":"Replies render as Markdown as they stream: headings, lists, tables and code blocks. Code blocks and tables have Copy buttons, and each reply has Copy Markdown . Send with in the session sheet chooses the key that sends: Ctrl/Cmd+Enter (the default), Enter, Shift+Enter or a combination you record. The Send button always","m":"single"},{"p":"Control it from your phone","h":"Attach from another computer","u":"remote-control.html#attach-from-another-computer","x":"","m":"single"},{"p":"Control it from your phone","h":"If it does not work","u":"remote-control.html#if-it-does-not-work","x":"The phone cannot reach the address. Check that both devices are on the same Tailscale network, or the same local network for the LAN address, and that a firewall is not blocking port 11541. Certificate warning on the Tailscale name. HTTPS certificates are not enabled for your tailnet yet; see step 2. `+ New session` is","m":"single"},{"p":"Drive it from another program","h":"Drive it from another program","u":"rpc.html","x":"`maple-harness --rpc` reads commands from another process instead of a person, and writes back everything that process needs to follow along: the model's own output, tool calls and results, questions that need an answer, and the running cost — as it happens, not only once the whole session ends. It is how a CI step, an","m":"single"},{"p":"Drive it from another program","h":"In plain words","u":"rpc.html#in-plain-words","x":"What it is. A way for another program (a CI job, an editor plugin, your own script) to drive a Maple session, line by line, as the terminal does. Why you want it. Automate the agent and still see every tool call, question and cost as it happens. What to do. Start `maple-harness --rpc` from your program and exchange one","m":"single"},{"p":"Drive it from another program","h":"Start it","u":"rpc.html#start-it","x":"It never starts on its own; it is meant to be the child of whatever is driving it. One JSON object per line comes in on stdin, one JSON object per line goes out on stdout. Nothing else is written to stdout — diagnostics and notes go to stderr, the same convention `--prompt` uses, so a plain `harness --rpc < commands.js","m":"single"},{"p":"Drive it from another program","h":"Commands","u":"rpc.html#commands","x":"Every line you send is one JSON object with a `cmd` field, and an optional `id` you choose — it comes back on the response so you can match one to the other. `cmd` What it does `prompt` Starts a new turn with `text`. Rejected while one is already running — say `steer` or `follow_up` instead, because they mean different","m":"single"},{"p":"Drive it from another program","h":"Events","u":"rpc.html#events","x":"What the turn itself does streams as further lines, `\"type\": \"event\"`, tagged by `event`: `event` Carries `assistant_delta` / `assistant_done` `text` (and `thinking`, when the model reasoned) `tool_call` / `tool_result` `tool`, `tool_arg` `steered` `text` — a `steer` command landed and is now part of the conversation `","m":"single"},{"p":"Drive it from another program","h":"A permission question","u":"rpc.html#a-permission-question","x":"A tool call that needs approval under your permission settings — the same settings `--yolo` and the interface's own mode control (ask/auto/yolo) govern — is not decided for you. It arrives as an event: Answer it the same way a person would: and a `permission_resolved` event confirms it, with `allow` and `always`. There","m":"single"},{"p":"Drive it from another program","h":"What it is not","u":"rpc.html#what-it-is-not","x":"This is a local pipe between a parent process and the child it started — there is no listening address and no token, because whoever can write to this process's stdin already has everything a shell here would give them. If you want a session another device can join over the network instead, see Control it from your pho","m":"single"},{"p":"The shell sandbox","h":"The shell sandbox","u":"sandbox.html","x":"The agent runs shell commands for you through its `bash` tool. Those commands run as you, with your files, your logins and your network. The sandbox contains them: when it is on, a command the model runs can change your project but not the rest of your computer, and cannot read the places where your credentials live. I","m":"single"},{"p":"The shell sandbox","h":"In plain words","u":"sandbox.html#in-plain-words","x":"What it is. A fence around the commands the agent runs: they can change your project, but not the rest of your computer, and cannot see where your keys and logins live. Why you want it. A mistaken or malicious command cannot read your SSH key or your cloud login. What to do. `/sandbox` in a session shows whether it is ","m":"single"},{"p":"The shell sandbox","h":"What it does","u":"sandbox.html#what-it-does","x":"While the sandbox is on, every `bash` command runs with these limits: What contained commands get Writable Your project folder, a private temporary folder, and the usual build caches under your home folder (`~/.cache`, `~/go`, `~/.npm`, `~/.cargo`, `~/.gradle`, `~/.m2` and similar), so builds and installs into the proj","m":"single"},{"p":"The shell sandbox","h":"What it breaks","u":"sandbox.html#what-it-breaks","x":"Anything that needs a hidden login, or writes outside the project, fails while the sandbox is on: Tool Why it fails What to do `git push`, `git pull` over SSH, `ssh`, `scp` `~/.ssh` is hidden, so there is no key and no known hosts Push yourself, allow `~/.ssh` for the session, or run that one command outside the sandbo","m":"single"},{"p":"The shell sandbox","h":"Turning it on","u":"sandbox.html#turning-it-on","x":"Setting What it does `/sandbox auto` Contain commands in this session. Without a backend (see Platforms ) commands still run, uncontained, and the first result says so `/sandbox strict` Contain commands in this session, and refuse to run any command when there is no backend `/sandbox off` No containment in this session","m":"single"},{"p":"The shell sandbox","h":"Contain commands whenever nobody approves them","u":"sandbox.html#contain-commands-whenever-nobody-approves-them","x":"In `ask` mode you see each command before it runs. In `auto` and `yolo` mode commands run without a person approving each one, and that is where containment matters most. This option turns the sandbox on exactly then: With it on, and `sandbox.mode` not set: In `auto` or `yolo` mode, commands are contained (as with `aut","m":"single"},{"p":"The shell sandbox","h":"When a command is blocked","u":"sandbox.html#when-a-command-is-blocked","x":"When a contained command fails because of the sandbox, the result says so in plain words, for you and for the model. For example: A `git push` over SSH blocked by the sandbox: the note says why, and the model stops and tells you what it needed. It says \"blocked\" only when the failure names a place the sandbox hides or ","m":"single"},{"p":"The shell sandbox","h":"Recommended setup","u":"sandbox.html#recommended-setup","x":"Install the backend (below) and run `/sandbox` or `maple-harness doctor` to check it works. If you use `auto` mode, turn on `sandbox.when_unattended`. Set a password for the proxy's setup page ( More > Protect this page , or `maple-proxy setup-password set`). The sandbox keeps contained commands away from the page; the","m":"single"},{"p":"The shell sandbox","h":"Platforms","u":"sandbox.html#platforms","x":"System Backend Status Linux bubblewrap (optional install, below) Verified. Blocking the setup page needs Linux 6.7 or later; on an older kernel commands can still reach it, and `/sandbox` says so macOS The built-in `sandbox-exec`, nothing to install Built, but not yet run on a Mac by us: treat it as unverified Windows ","m":"single"},{"p":"See and reduce what a task costs","h":"See and reduce what a task costs","u":"saving-tokens.html","x":"Long agent tasks spend most of their money re-reading a growing conversation on every turn. This page shows you where your spend goes, what the numbers mean, and a few habits that keep it down. It makes no promise about how much you will save: that depends on your tasks and your models, so measure your own.","m":"single"},{"p":"See and reduce what a task costs","h":"In plain words","u":"saving-tokens.html#in-plain-words","x":"What it is. Most of a long task's cost is the model re-reading the conversation on every turn. Maple shows you where that goes. Why you want it. You see which habits and settings help on your own work, instead of guessing. The clicks. `/cost` in a session; on the setup page, the Cache tab, which also says why the cache","m":"single"},{"p":"See and reduce what a task costs","h":"See where your spend goes","u":"saving-tokens.html#see-where-your-spend-goes","x":"In the agent: `/cost` shows the session's totals, the most recent requests with their cache hit rate, any retries, and roughly where the input went (instructions, tool descriptions, your messages, tool output). `/context` shows the window and what has been spent so far, including helper agents. In the proxy: the Cache ","m":"single"},{"p":"See and reduce what a task costs","h":"What the numbers mean","u":"saving-tokens.html#what-the-numbers-mean","x":"Hit rate. The share of a request's input that the provider served from its cache instead of processing again. A long task with a stable start should sit high; it drops when something early in the request changes. Tokens written and read. The first time the provider sees a stretch of your conversation it stores it, whic","m":"single"},{"p":"See and reduce what a task costs","h":"Habits that keep costs down","u":"saving-tokens.html#habits-that-keep-costs-down","x":"Keep one model for a long task. Switching models mid-task starts the provider's cache over, and the whole conversation is written again. `/model auto` keeps your model when it fits the job for this reason. Start a new task in a new session. A fresh session starts with a small conversation. Carrying an old, finished tas","m":"single"},{"p":"See and reduce what a task costs","h":"What we do not do","u":"saving-tokens.html#what-we-do-not-do","x":"Maple Harness does not put a filter between your commands and the model, and does not ask the model to talk in shorthand. Very large outputs are cut at a limit, with a note saying so.","m":"single"},{"p":"See and reduce what a task costs","h":"What others have measured","u":"saving-tokens.html#what-others-have-measured","x":"These are other people's measurements, of other tools, on other models and workloads. They are here for context, not as a claim about Maple. rtk , a filter for shell command output that advertises 60 to 90% fewer tokens. In JetBrains' benchmark (Denis Shiryaev, July 2026; 86 tasks, 425 billed trials, Claude Code with `","m":"single"},{"p":"See and reduce what a task costs","h":"Related","u":"saving-tokens.html#related","x":"What /cost tells you · Settings by goal · Your usage data · Check it yourself · Measure with bench · Proxy settings · Harness settings · The proxy and its setup page · Choose a model · Subagents · Troubleshooting","m":"single"},{"p":"Search by meaning","h":"Search by meaning","u":"search-by-meaning.html","x":"The agent looks things up with three tools: `history_search` (older parts of the conversation that were moved out of view to make room), `recall` (memory notes) and `graph_search` (the project index of files and symbols). On their own they match words, so a search for \"the login bug\" misses a message about \"authenticat","m":"single"},{"p":"Search by meaning","h":"In plain words","u":"search-by-meaning.html#in-plain-words","x":"What it is. The agent can find old parts of the conversation, its memory notes and places in your code by what they mean, not only by the words: \"the login bug\" also finds \"authentication failures\". Why you want it. Fewer misses when the agent looks something up, so less re-reading and fewer wrong guesses. The clicks. ","m":"single"},{"p":"Search by meaning","h":"The two models","u":"search-by-meaning.html#the-two-models","x":"bge-small-en-v1.5 (default) EmbeddingGemma 300M (optional) Runs in the proxy itself, on the CPU; nothing else to install the proxy itself, on the CPU, or your own embeddings server (Ollama, LM Studio and others) Download about 128 MB, once; about 33 MB is kept on disk about 1.2 GB, once; about 304 MB is kept on disk. N","m":"single"},{"p":"Search by meaning","h":"Choose the engine","u":"search-by-meaning.html#choose-the-engine","x":"The Search by meaning card has two engines: Built-in (Go): the proxy runs the model itself, on the CPU, with nothing else to install. Pick bge-small or EmbeddingGemma, download it, and press Use this model . Your own server (OpenAI-compatible): the proxy sends the text to an embeddings server you already run on this ma","m":"single"},{"p":"Search by meaning","h":"Set it up","u":"search-by-meaning.html#set-it-up","x":"From the setup page: open the proxy's setup page, go to Models & routing , find the Search by meaning card, keep Built-in (Go) selected and press Download on bge-small-en-v1.5. The card shows the download's progress (with Cancel ), this machine's memory and CPU cores, and a note if the machine is below what the model i","m":"single"},{"p":"Search by meaning","h":"Use EmbeddingGemma built in","u":"search-by-meaning.html#use-embeddinggemma-built-in","x":"EmbeddingGemma is gated on Hugging Face: Google asks everyone to accept its terms before downloading it. Sign in to Hugging Face , open the EmbeddingGemma 300M page and accept the terms there. Read the Gemma Terms of Use and the Gemma Prohibited Use Policy first. Create a token with read access at huggingface.co/settin","m":"single"},{"p":"Search by meaning","h":"Use your own server","u":"search-by-meaning.html#use-your-own-server","x":"Any server with an OpenAI-compatible `/v1/embeddings` works, for example: Server Base URL (default port) Model name, for example Ollama `http://127.0.0.1:11434/v1` `embeddinggemma` (after `ollama pull embeddinggemma`) LM Studio `http://127.0.0.1:1234/v1` the embedding model you loaded llama.cpp's `llama-server` (starte","m":"single"},{"p":"Search by meaning","h":"When something goes wrong","u":"search-by-meaning.html#when-something-goes-wrong","x":"The card's status line, its messages and the terminal commands use the same words. Each problem says what to do: What you see What to do The network is not reachable Check the connection (and any VPN), then try again The secure connection could not be verified Check the machine's clock and any network filter that inter","m":"single"},{"p":"Search by meaning","h":"Is my machine enough?","u":"search-by-meaning.html#is-my-machine-enough","x":"The proxy reads the machine's total and free memory, its CPU cores, its processor type and operating system, and shows them on the card. Before a download it warns, and never blocks, when the machine is below these: Model Comfortable from Basis bge-small-en-v1.5 4 GB of memory and 2 CPU cores measured: about 150 MB pea","m":"single"},{"p":"Search by meaning","h":"What changes for the agent","u":"search-by-meaning.html#what-changes-for-the-agent","x":"Tool With a model Without one `history_search` Results ranked by words and meaning combined. The filters (role, tool, path, turn range) apply first, as before Words only, unchanged `recall` Gains a `query` argument that returns the eight closest notes by words and meaning. Without `query` it lists notes as before No `q","m":"single"},{"p":"Search by meaning","h":"Measured results","u":"search-by-meaning.html#measured-results","x":"A retrieval test, measured on 28 September 2026: queries worded differently from what they look for, over a 43-message coding conversation (24 queries), 24 project memory notes (16 queries), and this project's own index of about 7,100 files and symbols (16 queries). Each query was run twice through the real tools, word","m":"single"},{"p":"Search by meaning","h":"The proxy's cache","u":"search-by-meaning.html#the-proxys-cache","x":"The proxy keeps every vector it computes, so a text it has already seen is answered at once instead of computed again: the harness re-reading the same messages, notes or files costs almost nothing the second time. In a request with several texts, only the new ones are computed, and the answer keeps the order you sent. ","m":"single"},{"p":"Search by meaning","h":"Where things are kept, and removing them","u":"search-by-meaning.html#where-things-are-kept-and-removing-them","x":"What Where Removed by A built-in model `embeddings/bge-small-en-v1.5/` or `embeddings/embeddinggemma-300m/` in your Maple Harness config folder (folder private to you, files readable only by you) Remove on the card, or `maple-proxy embeddings remove <name>` The Hugging Face token, and your server's API key the proxy's ","m":"single"},{"p":"Search by meaning","h":"Privacy","u":"search-by-meaning.html#privacy","x":"Text goes from the harness to the proxy it already uses, and no further than a model on this machine or your own network. The built-in models run inside the proxy. With your own server, the proxy forwards only to an address it can tell is local, and refuses one that is not. When it forwards, the proxy does not follow a","m":"single"},{"p":"Security","h":"Security","u":"security.html","x":"An agent runs commands and writes files on your machine; that is its job. This page says what Maple Harness does to keep that safe, and what you should do.","m":"both"},{"p":"Security","h":"In plain words","u":"security.html#in-plain-words","x":"What it is. What Maple does to keep an agent that runs commands safe, and what you should do. The short version. It asks before it writes or runs anything; your keys are in your system keyring; the setup page answers only on this machine; and you can put a password on it and contain the agent's commands. The clicks. On","m":"both"},{"p":"Security","h":"Reporting a problem","u":"security.html#reporting-a-problem","x":"Email Kinn Coelho Juliao at <kinncj@gmail.com> with what you found, how to reproduce it, and the version (`maple-harness version`). Please allow a reasonable time to respond before disclosing it publicly. Reports are handled in confidence.","m":"both"},{"p":"Security","h":"What it does for you","u":"security.html#what-it-does-for-you","x":"It asks first. Every tool call passes your rules. By default it asks before writing a file or running a command, and a `deny` rule is absolute. `--yolo` skips the asking, never the denying, and it must be given on the command line: a configuration file can never turn it on by itself. The shell can be contained. With bu","m":"both"},{"p":"Security","h":"What you should do","u":"security.html#what-you-should-do","x":"Treat a device token like an SSH key. It is a shell on your computer. Prefer pairing codes, keep tokens out of chats and screenshots, and revoke a lost phone straight away. Use Tailscale, or another private network, for remote control. Do not forward the port through your router to the internet. See Control it from you","m":"both"},{"p":"Sign in","h":"Sign in","u":"sign-in.html","x":"You can sign in from the terminal or from the proxy's setup page at `http://127.0.0.1:11539`. Either way, the proxy holds the credential and the agent only asks it for models. Signing in again refreshes the same account — the models it can use, its plan, its allowance — rather than adding another. This page is about si","m":"both"},{"p":"Sign in","h":"In plain words","u":"sign-in.html#in-plain-words","x":"What it is. Signing in tells the proxy which accounts it may use for you: a GitHub Copilot seat, an Anthropic API key, or another provider. The proxy keeps the credential; the agent never sees it. Why you want it. Without an account (or a model server of your own) there is no model to talk to. With two, the proxy can m","m":"both"},{"p":"Sign in","h":"GitHub Copilot","u":"sign-in.html#github-copilot","x":"The command prints a code and a link. Open the link, enter the code, and approve. The agent then asks Copilot which models your plan allows and configures exactly those. Free plan. It works, including Copilot's auto mode. The setup page shows how many chats you have left and when they reset, and the status bar in the a","m":"both"},{"p":"Sign in","h":"Claude (Anthropic API key)","u":"sign-in.html#claude-anthropic-api-key","x":"It asks for an API key (typed hidden), checks it with Anthropic, and configures the models that key can call. You can also paste it into the Anthropic (Claude) card on the setup page. Create a key in the Claude Console . An account with no credit will sign in but its requests will fail with a \"credit balance too low\" m","m":"both"},{"p":"Sign in","h":"OpenRouter","u":"sign-in.html#openrouter","x":"OpenRouter can make a key for you when you sign in with your OpenRouter account. Nothing about OpenRouter appears until you choose it. On the setup page, click Add provider , choose OpenRouter as the type, and click Sign in with OpenRouter . Approve in the OpenRouter tab that opens. Back on the setup page, the provider","m":"both"},{"p":"Sign in","h":"Grok (xAI API key)","u":"sign-in.html#grok-xai-api-key","x":"It asks for an API key (typed hidden), checks it with xAI, and configures the chat models that key can call, with their context windows. Create a key in the xAI Console . Nothing about Grok appears on the setup page until you choose it: to add it there, open Providers , click + Add provider , choose Grok (xAI) as the t","m":"both"},{"p":"Sign in","h":"Gemini API (Google AI Studio key)","u":"sign-in.html#gemini-api-google-ai-studio-key","x":"Read this first. On Google's free (unpaid) tier, Google uses what you send and the responses to improve its products and machine learning, and human reviewers may read, annotate and process them. Do not send sensitive, confidential or personal information there, which for a coding agent includes private code and secret","m":"both"},{"p":"Sign in","h":"Azure and Google Cloud","u":"sign-in.html#azure-and-google-cloud","x":"Azure OpenAI, Microsoft Foundry and Google's Vertex AI sign in with your cloud identity (`az login`, `gcloud`, or a pod's workload identity) or an Azure API key: see Azure and Google Cloud .","m":"both"},{"p":"Sign in","h":"Amazon Bedrock","u":"sign-in.html#amazon-bedrock","x":"Bedrock signs in with your AWS credentials (a profile, `aws sso login`, or an IAM role on EC2, ECS or EKS); Maple stores no AWS key: see Amazon Bedrock .","m":"both"},{"p":"Sign in","h":"More than one account","u":"sign-in.html#more-than-one-account","x":"Add a second (or third) account of the same provider — another GitHub Copilot seat, another Anthropic API key — and give it a name, so you can tell it apart from the others. A name can be used by one account only, across all providers, and cannot be a provider's id: On the setup page, each provider's card shows \"+ Add ","m":"both"},{"p":"Sign in","h":"Where the credentials are kept","u":"sign-in.html#where-the-credentials-are-kept","x":"Keys and sign-ins go in your operating system's keyring (Keychain on macOS, the Secret Service on Linux). The configuration file holds only a reference such as `${secret:anthropic-key}`, never the key, so it is safe to paste into an issue. Without a keyring they fall back to a private file, and the proxy tells you. `ma","m":"both"},{"p":"Single sign-on for the setup page","h":"Single sign-on for the setup page","u":"single-sign-on.html","x":"Team admins can sign in to a shared proxy's setup page with their company account (Google, Microsoft Entra ID, Okta, any OpenID Connect provider, or GitHub) instead of sharing one password, and developers can sign in to get their own access tokens. Off unless you turn it on. A proxy for one person on one machine does n","m":"teams"},{"p":"Single sign-on for the setup page","h":"In plain words","u":"single-sign-on.html#in-plain-words","x":"What it is. Your company's sign-in page (the one you use for email) guards the proxy's setup page. Admins open an address like `https://maple-admin.example.com`, sign in with their work account, and see the dashboards and manage people and budgets. Nobody shares a password. Developers too, if you want. With developer s","m":"teams"},{"p":"Single sign-on for the setup page","h":"Set it up with the wizard","u":"single-sign-on.html#set-it-up-with-the-wizard","x":"The Single sign-on tab on the setup page on the proxy's own machine. The wizard is not available through single sign-on itself: who may sign in is only ever changed on the proxy's machine (or in `config.yaml`). Identity provider. Google, Microsoft Entra ID (your tenant id), Okta (your org or authorization server), GitH","m":"teams"},{"p":"Single sign-on for the setup page","h":"How it works","u":"single-sign-on.html#how-it-works","x":"The setup page on the proxy's machine does not change. It still listens on this machine only, and its password (if you set one) still works there. Maple's own commands keep using it. Single sign-on serves the page on a second address you choose (`setup_page.sso.listen`), which people reach through your load balancer or","m":"teams"},{"p":"Single sign-on for the setup page","h":"Developer sign-in","u":"single-sign-on.html#developer-sign-in","x":"Off by default (`developers.onboarding: off`). Turn it on in the wizard's step 6, or in `config.yaml`: Ask an admin (`approve`): a developer's first sign-in files a request; an admin accepts it on People & teams (as a new person, or as someone already there) or refuses it. Automatic (`auto`): someone whose verified ema","m":"teams"},{"p":"Single sign-on for the setup page","h":"Recent sign-ins","u":"single-sign-on.html#recent-sign-ins","x":"The Single sign-on tab lists the sign-ins of the last seven days from the proxy's audit log: who signed in (and as an admin or a developer), who was added, and who was refused and why: not an admin or a developer here, no verified email address, a Google account outside your Workspace domain, not in an approved GitHub ","m":"teams"},{"p":"Single sign-on for the setup page","h":"The settings","u":"single-sign-on.html#the-settings","x":"All under `setup_page.sso` in `config.yaml`. The wizard writes them for you. Read at start, except `admins`, `groups_claim`, `password`, `session_lifetime`, `self_view`, `people` and `developers`, which apply on the next request after the file is saved. Key Default What it does `enabled` `false` Turns single sign-on on","m":"teams"},{"p":"Single sign-on for the setup page","h":"Google","u":"single-sign-on.html#google","x":"In the Google Cloud console, APIs & Services > Credentials > Create credentials > OAuth client ID , type Web application . Add your redirect address under Authorized redirect URIs . In the wizard choose Google (`issuer: https://accounts.google.com`), and the client id and secret it shows. Admins: `emails` or `domains`.","m":"teams"},{"p":"Single sign-on for the setup page","h":"Microsoft Entra ID","u":"single-sign-on.html#microsoft-entra-id","x":"App registrations > New registration : supported account types this organizational directory only , redirect URI of type Web = your redirect address. Then Certificates & secrets > New client secret . In the wizard choose Microsoft Entra ID and type your directory (tenant) id: the issuer is `https://login.microsoftonlin","m":"teams"},{"p":"Single sign-on for the setup page","h":"Okta","u":"single-sign-on.html#okta","x":"Applications > Create App Integration > OIDC - OpenID Connect > Web Application . Sign-in redirect URI = your redirect address. In the wizard choose Okta : the issuer is `https://<your-org>.okta.com` (the org authorization server) or `https://<your-org>.okta.com/oauth2/default` (a custom one). Admins by group: on the o","m":"teams"},{"p":"Single sign-on for the setup page","h":"GitHub","u":"single-sign-on.html#github","x":"GitHub has no OpenID Connect sign-in for people, so Maple uses a GitHub OAuth app with PKCE and then asks GitHub for the account's verified email addresses and, when groups are used, its organisations and teams. Settings > Developer settings > OAuth Apps > New OAuth App ; Authorization callback URL = your redirect addr","m":"teams"},{"p":"Single sign-on for the setup page","h":"Other OpenID Connect providers","u":"single-sign-on.html#other-openid-connect-providers","x":"Keycloak, Auth0, Authentik, Dex and others work the same way: register a confidential web client, type the issuer whose `/.well-known/openid-configuration` names that same issuer, and use emails, domains, groups or subjects. The provider must support the authorization code flow with PKCE (S256). Only an `email_verified","m":"teams"},{"p":"Single sign-on for the setup page","h":"On Kubernetes","u":"single-sign-on.html#on-kubernetes","x":"When `config.yaml` comes from a ConfigMap (`storage.settings: file`) or a settings repository, the wizard cannot save: use Show the YAML and put the block there, and the client secret in your Secret under `sso.client_secret`. For example: Expose port 11547 with a Service, and an Ingress (or a Gateway route) for `maple-","m":"teams"},{"p":"Single sign-on for the setup page","h":"People see their own usage","u":"single-sign-on.html#people-see-their-own-usage","x":"With `self_view: on`, someone who is not an admin but whose verified email is one of your people's sees that person's own usage (the last 30 days, by day and by model) at `/sso/me`, and nothing else. Map emails to person ids under `people` (`ana@example.com: ana`), or give a person their email as their id. Off by defau","m":"teams"},{"p":"Single sign-on for the setup page","h":"What is not verified","u":"single-sign-on.html#what-is-not-verified","x":"Tested against a stand-in OpenID Connect provider and a stand-in GitHub, and in Chromium through a TLS front like a load balancer. Not yet run against real Google, Microsoft Entra ID, Okta or GitHub accounts, real ingress controllers, or Firefox and Safari.","m":"teams"},{"p":"Subagents","h":"Subagents","u":"subagents.html","x":"A subagent is a second session that the main one hands a small, self-contained job to: \"find where the retry logic lives\", \"review this diff\", \"plan the change\". It has its own instructions, its own list of tools and optionally its own model. It does not see your conversation, and only its final report comes back, so a","m":"single"},{"p":"Subagents","h":"In plain words","u":"subagents.html#in-plain-words","x":"What it is. A helper the agent hands a small job to (\"find where retries happen\", \"review this diff\"). It works on its own and only its report comes back. Why you want it. A long search stays out of your conversation, so the main session stays short and cheap. What to do. Nothing: the agent uses them when a job fits. T","m":"single"},{"p":"Subagents","h":"The ones you already have","u":"subagents.html#the-ones-you-already-have","x":"Name What it does `general-purpose` Takes any self-contained task. Has the same tools as the main session `explore` Reads and searches the project and answers questions about it. Cannot change anything `plan` Studies the code and returns a step-by-step plan. Cannot change anything The model uses them by itself when a j","m":"single"},{"p":"Subagents","h":"Watching them work","u":"subagents.html#watching-them-work","x":"While a subagent runs, a small list appears just above the input box (which does not move): `◯` is running, `!` is waiting for you, `✓` finished, `✗` failed or stopped. Press the down arrow from an empty input box (or `ctrl+t` from anywhere) to move into the list, then: the arrow keys choose between `main` and a subage","m":"single"},{"p":"Subagents","h":"Make your own","u":"subagents.html#make-your-own","x":"Type `/agents`. You see every agent with where it lives, and can view, create, edit or delete them. Creating one asks where it should live (this project, or all your projects), a name, a description (this is what tells the model when to use it), which tools it may use (all, read-only, or a list you tick), which model, ","m":"single"},{"p":"Subagents","h":"Settings","u":"subagents.html#settings","x":"Setting Default Meaning `harness.subagents.max_parallel` `3` How many run at the same time when the model hands out several jobs at once `harness.subagents.max_turns` `50` The most turns any one subagent may take When the model gives out several jobs in one message they run together, and their reports come back in the ","m":"single"},{"p":"Subagents","h":"Delegate to your local Claude","u":"subagents.html#delegate-to-your-local-claude","x":"If you have Claude Code installed and signed in on your machine, the harness can hand it a whole task: \"ask my local Claude to review the error handling in this package\". The harness runs your own `claude` program in this project folder (`claude -p`, its non-interactive mode), shows its progress in the agents list, and","m":"single"},{"p":"Subagents","h":"Turn it on","u":"subagents.html#turn-it-on","x":"It is off until you turn it on in your own `config.yaml` (a project's `.mapleharness/config.yaml` cannot): The first time it runs in a project, and again whenever that project's Claude settings files change, the harness shows you what it is about to run and asks. The question lists what the project's `.claude/settings.","m":"single"},{"p":"Subagents","h":"Choose which `claude` runs","u":"subagents.html#choose-which-claude-runs","x":"By default the harness runs the `claude` it finds on your `PATH`. To run a particular one, name it in your own `config.yaml`: The path must be absolute (`~/` for your home folder is fine). Before it runs, the harness checks that it exists, is a regular executable file and is not inside the project, and that nobody else","m":"single"},{"p":"Subagents","h":"Extra options for Claude Code","u":"subagents.html#extra-options-for-claude-code","x":"`claude_code_options` adds options to the `claude -p` command, one per entry, written as `--flag value` (or `--flag=value`): Only these are allowed, each with a check on its value: Option What it does Accepted values `--model` The model it uses An alias such as `sonnet` or `opus`, or a full model name (letters, digits ","m":"single"},{"p":"Subagents","h":"Checks before it runs","u":"subagents.html#checks-before-it-runs","x":"Before its first run in a session, and again when the program or the options change, the harness checks that the program answers `claude --version` within a few seconds (asked in an empty folder of its own, or inside the sandbox when the sandbox applies), that the options pass the checks above, and that the sandbox wou","m":"single"},{"p":"Subagents","h":"How it is kept safe","u":"subagents.html#how-it-is-kept-safe","x":"Only in a project the harness trusts. If the project has files the harness itself would run and you have not trusted them (`maple-harness trust approve`), it refuses. It also refuses in your home folder itself, and refuses a `claude` inside the project, whether found on `PATH` or named in `claude_code_path`. The projec","m":"single"},{"p":"Share Maple with a team: hubs, people and fallbacks","h":"Share Maple with a team: hubs, people and fallbacks","u":"teams-and-hubs.html","x":"This guide is for running Maple for more than one person: a small team sharing one proxy, a company with a hub per team, or one person whose laptop should fall back to a shared hub when their own account runs out. It walks through each setup with the configuration to type, what you see afterwards, and where to look whe","m":"teams"},{"p":"Share Maple with a team: hubs, people and fallbacks","h":"In plain words","u":"teams-and-hubs.html#in-plain-words","x":"One proxy, several people: open the proxy to your network, give each person a token, and see who used what. The setup page's People & teams tab walks you through it. Your laptop and a team hub: your own proxy joins the hub, by signing in with your company account or with a token from the hub's admin. Maple Harness keep","m":"teams"},{"p":"Share Maple with a team: hubs, people and fallbacks","h":"Who this is for, and who can skip it","u":"teams-and-hubs.html#who-this-is-for-and-who-can-skip-it","x":"If you use Maple on your own machine, you need none of this. Install it, sign in and work, as before. Nothing on this page is on until you add it to `config.yaml`: a proxy with no `people:`, no `teams:` and no other Maple proxy as a provider behaves exactly as it always has, its setup page shows no People or Chained pr","m":"teams"},{"p":"Share Maple with a team: hubs, people and fallbacks","h":"The building blocks","u":"teams-and-hubs.html#the-building-blocks","x":"Word What it is Proxy `maple-proxy`: the program that holds sign-ins and keys and talks to the models. Every Maple install has one, usually listening only on `127.0.0.1` Hub A proxy that other machines or other proxies use. Nothing extra is installed: a hub is a proxy that listens on a network address and asks every ca","m":"teams"},{"p":"Share Maple with a team: hubs, people and fallbacks","h":"A. One shared proxy for a small team","u":"teams-and-hubs.html#a-one-shared-proxy-for-a-small-team","x":"One machine runs the proxy with the team's accounts; everyone else connects to it with their own token. One shared proxy: a token per person, the accounts on one machine. The hub can run as a program on a machine, as below, in a container ( Docker ), or on a cluster, with several replicas when a restart must not interr","m":"teams"},{"p":"Share Maple with a team: hubs, people and fallbacks","h":"B. Who uses which account","u":"teams-and-hubs.html#b-who-uses-which-account","x":"A hub can hold several accounts for the same models, and give them out: one for everyone, one per team, or one for a single person. The same with the commands: The order the accounts are tried in. An account is only used for models of its own kind. An account that does not serve the model, or that the person's model li","m":"teams"},{"p":"Share Maple with a team: hubs, people and fallbacks","h":"C. Chained hubs","u":"teams-and-hubs.html#c-chained-hubs","x":"When there is more than one proxy, chain them: each proxy uses the one above it as a provider of `type: maple`. A laptop goes to a team hub, and the team hub goes to the top hub that holds the company's accounts. Requests go up; people and teams come down. On the setup page (the quickest way): on the hub, Hubs & chaini","m":"teams"},{"p":"Share Maple with a team: hubs, people and fallbacks","h":"People defined once, at the top","u":"teams-and-hubs.html#people-defined-once-at-the-top","x":"Define the people and teams on the top hub. Every proxy below fetches them at start and again as soon as they change (it waits on the hub for news), with their budgets, model lists and accounts, and a check value for each of their tokens, never the tokens. So a person's token made at the top also works at the team hub ","m":"teams"},{"p":"Share Maple with a team: hubs, people and fallbacks","h":"Usage rolls up","u":"teams-and-hubs.html#usage-rolls-up","x":"Each proxy sends its usage records (counts and labels only, never prompts, answers or files) up to its parent, with the records its own children sent it, so the top hub can report on the whole chain. Each request is counted once: every hop shares one request id. If the parent is down, the child keeps its records and se","m":"teams"},{"p":"Share Maple with a team: hubs, people and fallbacks","h":"D. Fallback across the chain","u":"teams-and-hubs.html#d-fallback-across-the-chain","x":"The pattern most people want from a chain is a fallback that crosses machines, either way round. Both are ordinary fallbacks on the proxy where you set them, so they work on any proxy in the chain. Your account first, or the hub first.","m":"teams"},{"p":"Share Maple with a team: hubs, people and fallbacks","h":"Your own account first, the team hub when you run out","u":"teams-and-hubs.html#your-own-account-first-the-team-hub-when-you-run-out","x":"On your laptop: your own Copilot account serves your requests, and when it is out of credits or rate limited they go to the team hub, for the same model. While your account is marked as out of quota, later requests go straight to the hub (the route reason is `exhausted-order`), and back to your account once its wait is","m":"teams"},{"p":"Share Maple with a team: hubs, people and fallbacks","h":"The hub first, your local model when the hub says no","u":"teams-and-hubs.html#the-hub-first-your-local-model-when-the-hub-says-no","x":"Everything goes to the hub, and a model on your own machine answers when the hub refuses (a budget there is spent, its accounts are out of quota) or cannot be reached: With the team hub stopped, the answer came from the local model: and with the top hub's account out of quota, two hubs up, the same: Both can be set on ","m":"teams"},{"p":"Share Maple with a team: hubs, people and fallbacks","h":"E. See who used what","u":"teams-and-hubs.html#e-see-who-used-what","x":"Every proxy in the chain records, on each request, who asked (`person`, `team`), which proxy they asked (`origin_proxy`), the path (`via`, `hops`), the account that served it and why (`provider`, `account_source`, `route_reason`). Filter by any of them on the setup page, with `maple-proxy usage query`, or in DuckDB. On","m":"teams"},{"p":"Share Maple with a team: hubs, people and fallbacks","h":"F. Day to day: revoking, outages and security","u":"teams-and-hubs.html#f-day-to-day-revoking-outages-and-security","x":"Revoking someone. Revoke their token on the proxy that made it (the setup page's token list). It stops working there at once, and on every proxy below within seconds while the hubs answer, one hop after another (a proxy below that cannot reach its hub keeps the last copy for up to `chain.directory_grace`, 15 minutes by","m":"teams"},{"p":"Share Maple with a team: hubs, people and fallbacks","h":"What is not covered, and what is not verified","u":"teams-and-hubs.html#what-is-not-covered-and-what-is-not-verified","x":"Downgrading. A proxy older than 2.0 ignores `people:`, `teams:` and `default_accounts:` and keeps working, but no budget, model list or account rule applies and requests are no longer attributed (see Going back to an older version ). A chain was not tested with an older proxy in it. Older usage. Requests from before 2.","m":"teams"},{"p":"Share Maple with a team: hubs, people and fallbacks","h":"Related","u":"teams-and-hubs.html#related","x":"Teams : every key for people, teams, tokens, budgets and accounts on one proxy. Chain proxies into hubs : every key for one proxy using another. The proxy and its setup page : the setup page's cards, including People. See and filter your usage and Your usage data : queries, exports and DuckDB. Your data : PostgreSQL fo","m":"teams"},{"p":"Teams","h":"Teams","u":"teams.html","x":"Several people can share one proxy, each with their own access token. The proxy then knows who sent every request, can give each person or team their own provider accounts, model list and budget, and lets each person see their own usage. If you use Maple on your own machine, you need none of this. Nothing on this page ","m":"teams"},{"p":"Teams","h":"In plain words","u":"teams.html#in-plain-words","x":"What it is. Several people share one proxy, each with a token of their own, so the proxy knows who sent each request. Why you want it. Each person or team can get their own accounts, models and budget, and you see who spent what. The clicks. On the setup page, Set up more , Share this proxy with a team : the People & t","m":"teams"},{"p":"Teams","h":"How each person signs in","u":"teams.html#how-each-person-signs-in","x":"A person has no password of their own on the proxy. What identifies a person is an access token tied to them. Every request that carries one of their tokens is theirs: their budget, their model list, their accounts, their line on the People card. A person with no token cannot connect from another machine at all. (With ","m":"teams"},{"p":"Teams","h":"Make a token and tie it to the person","u":"teams.html#make-a-token-and-tie-it-to-the-person","x":"On the proxy's machine: Open the setup page (`http://127.0.0.1:11539`), then People & teams , and find the person on the People card. Open Access and tokens under their name. Give the token a name you will recognise later, such as `carol-laptop`, and choose when it expires. Select Make a token . It has the proxy scope ","m":"teams"},{"p":"Teams","h":"Hand the token over safely","u":"teams.html#hand-the-token-over-safely","x":"Send it over a private channel: in person, a password manager's shared item, or a direct message that is not kept in a shared channel. Never in a group chat, an issue, an email list or a commit. The person keeps it in their secret store or an environment variable, never in a file that goes into a repository. If it may ","m":"teams"},{"p":"Teams","h":"How each tool uses the token","u":"teams.html#how-each-tool-uses-the-token","x":"Each person's tools send the token as their API key. How they connect , under the person's Access and tokens , shows these lines with this proxy's addresses. Replace `proxy.example` with the address of the proxy's machine, and use the ports it listens on (`11535` for OpenAI-compatible tools and `11537` for Anthropic-co","m":"teams"},{"p":"Teams","h":"`local` and `unattributed`","u":"teams.html#local-and-unattributed","x":"`local` is a request from the proxy's own machine that carries no token. It needs no token, so the People card shows no note for it. Add a person with the id `local` to give it a team, a budget or rules. `unattributed` is a request no person was recorded for: one made with a token tied to nobody, or sent before people ","m":"teams"},{"p":"Teams","h":"Several tokens per person","u":"teams.html#several-tokens-per-person","x":"A person can have as many tokens as they like, one per device (`carol-laptop`, `carol-desktop`). Tie each one to them; all their requests count as theirs, and revoking one leaves the others working. The People card says what each person signs in with (\"signs in with: carol-laptop, carol-desktop\"), and `maple-proxy peop","m":"teams"},{"p":"Teams","h":"Rotate or revoke a token","u":"teams.html#rotate-or-revoke-a-token","x":"Revoke: under the person's Access and tokens , select Revoke next to the token (or Revoke all ), or run `maple-proxy token revoke <token-id>`. It stops working at once and is untied from the person. Rotate: make a new token for the same person, hand it over, let them connect with it (pasted again on their proxy's Hubs ","m":"teams"},{"p":"Teams","h":"When a request is refused","u":"teams.html#when-a-request-is-refused","x":"Answer What it means What to do `401`, \"a valid access token with the proxy scope is required\" No token, a mistyped, revoked or expired one, or one without the proxy scope Check the key the tool sends; make a new token with the proxy scope if needed `403` from the proxy's address The address the tool uses is not in `se","m":"teams"},{"p":"Teams","h":"Set up a team on the People & teams tab","u":"teams.html#set-up-a-team-on-the-people--teams-tab","x":"The setup page's People & teams tab does all of this step by step, on the proxy's own machine. On a proxy used by one person it is not in the menu: open Set up more and press Set up on Share this proxy with a team (the tab then stays in the menu on that browser). Used alone, it says there is nothing to do, with one but","m":"teams"},{"p":"Teams","h":"Set up a team in four steps","u":"teams.html#set-up-a-team-in-four-steps","x":"Make a token for each person. On the proxy's setup page, open Security & access → Remote access . It asks what the token is for: choose A person's tools to send model requests , tick proxy (the only scope it offers), choose Token , and give each person their token. (The other choice, A phone or browser to follow and dr","m":"teams"},{"p":"Teams","h":"Who a request belongs to","u":"teams.html#who-a-request-belongs-to","x":"A request with a token belongs to the person that token is tied to. A proxy token tied to nobody still works; its requests are counted as unattributed and no person's rules apply to them. `maple-proxy people list` lists such tokens, so you can tie or revoke them. On a proxy that listens only on this machine, a token is","m":"teams"},{"p":"Teams","h":"Budgets","u":"teams.html#budgets","x":"A budget is in AI units, as the providers reported them: the same units as the provider budgets (`budgets:`). Set a daily limit, a monthly limit or both, per person and per team. Days and months are counted in UTC, so a daily budget resets at 00:00 UTC and a monthly one on the 1st. AI units are what GitHub Copilot repo","m":"teams"},{"p":"Teams","h":"Models","u":"teams.html#models","x":"`models.allow` lists the models a person or a team may use: names as asked for (an alias such as `coder-default`, or `auto`), model ids, or `account/model`, with `*` as a wildcard (`copilot/*`; a name ending in `/*` also covers everything a hub passes on under it, such as `hub/copilot-work/gpt-5`). An empty list allows","m":"teams"},{"p":"Teams","h":"Accounts","u":"teams.html#accounts","x":"Each account (a provider entry, such as one GitHub Copilot sign-in) has an id and may have a nickname; either works in these lists. `accounts` on a person, then on their team, then `default_accounts` for everyone, are tried first, in that order, for a model of their kind. A person on two accounts falls back from the fi","m":"teams"},{"p":"Teams","h":"The self view","u":"teams.html#the-self-view","x":"With `people_self_view: \"on\"` (off by default; the switch is on the People card, or `maple-proxy people self-view on`), each person can ask the proxy for their own figures with their own token: It answers with the person, the label of the token that asked, their own budget used and left, and their requests, tokens, AI ","m":"teams"},{"p":"Teams","h":"Share one proxy with your team","u":"teams.html#share-one-proxy-with-your-team","x":"","m":"teams"},{"p":"Teams","h":"On the proxy's machine","u":"teams.html#on-the-proxys-machine","x":"Set up the people as above. Let the proxy listen where your teammates can reach it, for example `server.openai_compat: 0.0.0.0:11535` and `server.anthropic_compat: 0.0.0.0:11537`. Off this machine every request needs a token with the proxy scope; the proxy will not start that way until one exists. The setup page stays ","m":"teams"},{"p":"Teams","h":"On each teammate's machine","u":"teams.html#on-each-teammates-machine","x":"Each person uses their own token, with the `proxy` scope. Do not share tokens: every request made with one is counted as its person's. Maple Harness. With Maple installed on your machine, join your own proxy to this one and keep using Maple Harness as before: paste the token on your proxy's setup page ( Hubs & chaining","m":"teams"},{"p":"Teams","h":"Teams across chained hubs","u":"teams.html#teams-across-chained-hubs","x":"When a proxy uses another Maple proxy as its parent, it inherits the parent's people, teams, their accounts, budgets and model rules, and the parent's tokens (as hashes, never the tokens), and passes them on to its own children. What the proxy uses is the inherited people and teams plus its own. Inherited entries are r","m":"teams"},{"p":"Teams","h":"Limits","u":"teams.html#limits","x":"Budgets and spend are in AI units as the providers reported them; `cost_estimated` is an estimate from list prices. Neither is a bill. Only requests with a token can be told apart. On a proxy that listens only on this machine, anyone on the machine can leave the token out and be counted as `local`; listen off this mach","m":"teams"},{"p":"Teams","h":"Going back to an older version","u":"teams.html#going-back-to-an-older-version","x":"A proxy older than 2.0 ignores `people:`, `teams:`, `default_accounts:` and `people_self_view:` and keeps working: every token still works, but no person's budget, model list or accounts apply, and requests are no longer attributed. The keys stay in `config.yaml` and apply again after an upgrade. The usage log stays re","m":"teams"},{"p":"Settings by goal","h":"Settings by goal","u":"three-es.html","x":"Maple Harness aims at three things at once: to be efficient (fewer tokens for the same work), efficacious (the work stays as good, and defaults are safe) and effective (whole tasks succeed and waste stops early). This page maps a goal to the settings and commands that serve it. It makes no promise about your bill: resu","m":"single"},{"p":"Settings by goal","h":"In plain words","u":"three-es.html#in-plain-words","x":"What it is. A map from what you want (spend less, keep quality, stop runaways, get reliable results) to the setting that serves it. What to do. Find your goal below, change the setting where it says (most are on the setup page's Models & routing tab, or on Advanced , which Find a setting reaches by name), and measure t","m":"single"},{"p":"Settings by goal","h":"I want to spend less on long runs","u":"three-es.html#i-want-to-spend-less-on-long-runs","x":"Do this Where Trade-off Keep one model for a long task instead of switching `/model <name>`, or `/model auto` (it keeps your model when it fits) A fixed model may be more than a small job needs See where the money goes `/cost`, the setup page's Cache card, `maple-proxy cache` None Keep the provider's cache working `pro","m":"single"},{"p":"Settings by goal","h":"I want the work to stay as good","u":"three-es.html#i-want-the-work-to-stay-as-good","x":"Do this Where Trade-off Let the compiler check the model's edits `lsp` (on by default), `/lsp` to see what is running The first edit starts the server in the background Keep the safe defaults `permission.mode: ask`, `sandbox.mode`, `file_policy.*` More questions, fewer surprises Prefer the tools that show a diff `bash_","m":"single"},{"p":"Settings by goal","h":"I want to stop runaways","u":"three-es.html#i-want-to-stop-runaways","x":"Do this Where Trade-off End a turn that keeps failing the same way, with a summary `loop_guard.mode: stop`, `loop_guard.stop_after` See above Cut a reply that repeats itself `degenerate_guard` (the repeat guard) on the proxy's Guards card A repetitive answer could be cut short Refuse or trim a request bigger than the m","m":"single"},{"p":"Settings by goal","h":"I want results I can rely on","u":"three-es.html#i-want-results-i-can-rely-on","x":"Do this Where Trade-off Get told when a session needs you `notifications.*`, `maple-harness notify test` None Resume where you left off `--continue`, `--resume`, `/sessions` Old sessions take disk; `cleanup` removes them Keep a checklist for a big request `ledger: auto`, `/ledger` One extra model call Make the model ca","m":"single"},{"p":"Troubleshooting","h":"Troubleshooting","u":"troubleshooting.html","x":"Start with the checker. It looks at your configuration, the proxy, sign-ins, ports, remote control and the sandbox, and says what to do about each problem it finds: Two commands inside a session answer most of the rest: `maple-proxy serve --logs=true` shows every request and the reason behind every failed one.","m":"both"},{"p":"Troubleshooting","h":"In plain words","u":"troubleshooting.html#in-plain-words","x":"Start with the checker. `maple-harness doctor` looks at everything and says what to fix, in plain words. Then look here. Find the symptom below; each has the fix. On the setup page, What's configured on the Overview marks anything that needs attention, with a link to where it is set.","m":"both"},{"p":"Troubleshooting","h":"The proxy","u":"troubleshooting.html#the-proxy","x":"The agent says the proxy is unreachable. Nothing answers at the configured address. `/proxy` shows where the agent is looking, and `/proxy start` starts one. This usually means `server.openai_compat` in `config.yaml` differs from where a proxy was started. The setup page at `127.0.0.1:11539` does not load. Check the pr","m":"both"},{"p":"Troubleshooting","h":"Signing in","u":"troubleshooting.html#signing-in","x":"Copilot sign-in never finishes. Open the link it printed and enter the code within ten minutes, then run `maple-harness auth login github-copilot` again. A `403` means the account has no Copilot seat, or the grant was revoked; signing in again is the first thing to try. \"This account has no chat models enabled for Copi","m":"both"},{"p":"Troubleshooting","h":"Models","u":"troubleshooting.html#models","x":"The context window shows 0, or history is trimmed too early. A window of 0 means unknown. Leave `context_window` out of `config.yaml` so the proxy asks the server, and check `/context`. If the server reports nothing, set it by hand. A local model repeats itself or stops without finishing. Small models do this. Use them","m":"both"},{"p":"Troubleshooting","h":"Cost","u":"troubleshooting.html#cost","x":"","m":"both"},{"p":"Troubleshooting","h":"A run cost more than I expected","u":"troubleshooting.html#a-run-cost-more-than-i-expected","x":"Type `/cost`. It shows what the session has spent, the last few requests with how much of each was served from the provider's cache, and where the input goes. A request marked \"low cache hit\" or \"model changed\" is where the cache was lost: a new model starts with a cold cache. To keep it, use `/model fixed` (or pick a ","m":"both"},{"p":"Troubleshooting","h":"The agent keeps retrying the same failing command","u":"troubleshooting.html#the-agent-keeps-retrying-the-same-failing-command","x":"When the same problem fails again and again, Maple Harness steps in. After three similar failures it adds a note to the result telling the model to stop trying variations, say what it is assuming, check that with a cheap read-only command, and choose a different approach. After six, it ends the turn with a summary of w","m":"both"},{"p":"Troubleshooting","h":"Installing and updating","u":"troubleshooting.html#installing-and-updating","x":"`checksum mismatch`. The installer stops and leaves the existing files untouched. A truncated download is the usual cause; run it again. `permission denied` writing to `/usr/local/bin`. Use `--install-location ~/.local/bin`, or run the installer with `sudo`. macOS says a downloaded binary cannot be opened. Binaries dow","m":"both"},{"p":"Troubleshooting","h":"Remote control","u":"troubleshooting.html#remote-control","x":"The phone cannot reach the address. Check both devices are on the same Tailscale network (or the same local network for a LAN address) and that a firewall is not blocking port 11541. Pairing shows a link that does not load. The gateway may have stopped. `/remote pair` starts it again; or run `maple-harness gateway star","m":"both"},{"p":"Troubleshooting","h":"The shell sandbox","u":"troubleshooting.html#the-shell-sandbox","x":"`/sandbox` says bubblewrap is missing, or installed but not working. It is optional; the install command for your system is in Install . \"Installed but does not work\" usually means unprivileged user namespaces are blocked, for example in a container. Until it works, commands run uncontained. A command fails inside a sh","m":"both"},{"p":"Troubleshooting","h":"Disk space","u":"troubleshooting.html#disk-space","x":"The configuration directory (`~/.config/maple-harness`) holds saved sessions, memory notes and logs. To see how much it uses and clear old sessions, run `maple-harness cleanup --help`.","m":"both"},{"p":"Troubleshooting","h":"More help on other pages","u":"troubleshooting.html#more-help-on-other-pages","x":"Some pages have their own troubleshooting section for the feature they cover: Language servers: Troubleshooting : a server is not found, or its errors do not show up. Search by meaning: When something goes wrong . The shell sandbox: When a command is blocked . Control it from your phone: If it does not work . Keep it r","m":"both"},{"p":"Upgrading to 2.0","h":"Upgrading to 2.0","u":"upgrading-to-2.0.html","x":"If you use Maple Harness on your own machine, upgrading to 2.0 asks nothing of you: update, restart the proxy, and carry on. The team, hub and container features are new, but they stay out of the way until you set them up. This page says what keeps working, what is new and on, what is new and off, what changed, how to ","m":"both"},{"p":"Upgrading to 2.0","h":"In plain words","u":"upgrading-to-2.0.html#in-plain-words","x":"On one computer, nothing to do. Update, restart the proxy, carry on. Your settings, sign-ins, tokens, sessions and usage history keep working. New things stay out of the way. Teams, hubs and containers only appear once you set them up. The setup page looks different. Its tabs now sit in two groups, Watch and Configure ","m":"both"},{"p":"Upgrading to 2.0","h":"Nothing to do","u":"upgrading-to-2.0.html#nothing-to-do","x":"Everything a 1.x release left on your disk keeps working on 2.0: your `config.yaml` and settings, sign-ins and provider keys; access tokens, and the phones and computers you paired; saved sessions, which resume where you left them; the usage log, the events log, your exports, and the DuckDB queries you already wrote. W","m":"both"},{"p":"Upgrading to 2.0","h":"New, and on by default","u":"upgrading-to-2.0.html#new-and-on-by-default","x":"These need no setup and change nothing about how your requests are routed. The setup page in tabs. Overview, Usage, Costs & alerts, Cache, Health, Auto, Data and Settings in 2.0 (2.2 groups them into Watch and Configure, and Settings is split up: see The setup page ), with charts of your usage and a live view of reques","m":"both"},{"p":"Upgrading to 2.0","h":"New, and off until you turn it on","u":"upgrading-to-2.0.html#new-and-off-until-you-turn-it-on","x":"None of these appear in your setup, ask you anything, or show up on the setup page until you configure them. People and teams on one proxy, each with their own budgets, models and accounts. See Teams . The self view , where a person's token can read their own budget and spend (`people_self_view`). See The self view . H","m":"both"},{"p":"Upgrading to 2.0","h":"What changed","u":"upgrading-to-2.0.html#what-changed","x":"The setup page's sections moved into tabs. Nothing was removed; see the table in The setup page for where each card went. `maple-proxy usage status` explains how long the usage log is kept. It used to say \"everything (usage_retention_days: 0)\" when the setting was not set. It now shows the retention the daily cleanup r","m":"both"},{"p":"Upgrading to 2.0","h":"Upgrade","u":"upgrading-to-2.0.html#upgrade","x":"On your own machine: Or run the installer one-liner again. Then restart the proxy so the new version runs (`/proxy restart` in the harness does it); if it runs as a background service, see Update . Check the versions: In a container: no image is published, so build a new one from the 2.0 release with the sample `Docker","m":"both"},{"p":"Upgrading to 2.0","h":"Going back to 1.18 or 1.17","u":"upgrading-to-2.0.html#going-back-to-118-or-117","x":"If you need to, install the older release over 2.0. On macOS and Linux: On Windows, in PowerShell: We tested going back to 1.18 and to 1.17 on the files 2.0 left, after using 2.0 with people, a team, budgets, alerts, health, a chain token and an MCP hub token. What we saw: It starts and works, with no crash. Routing, a","m":"both"},{"p":"Upgrading to 2.0","h":"Known limitations in 2.0","u":"upgrading-to-2.0.html#known-limitations-in-20","x":"The setup page's live view is limited to eight open views per proxy; past that, a tab refreshes every few seconds instead. With several replicas, \"in flight\" counts the replica serving the page, and the others' requests reach the live view within about ten seconds. The setup page's charts read the usage log through the","m":"both"},{"p":"Upgrading to 2.0","h":"Related","u":"upgrading-to-2.0.html#related","x":"Install : the installer, updating and choosing a release. Keep it running in the background : restarting the service after an update. Share Maple with a team : the features that stay off until you set them up. Release notes , with every change in each release.","m":"both"},{"p":"Your usage data","h":"Your usage data","u":"usage-export.html","x":"The usage log is on by default: one JSON object per request, in your own files — not locked inside Maple. It holds counts and labels only (model, tokens, cache figures, AI units, timing), never prompts or answers. This page is about where it lives, how long it is kept, and actually using that data: querying it directly","m":"both"},{"p":"Your usage data","h":"In plain words","u":"usage-export.html#in-plain-words","x":"What it is. The reference for the usage log: where the files are, how long they are kept, every column, and how to export them or wire them into Grafana. What to do. On the setup page, Data & storage , the Usage data card moves, keeps, exports or prunes the log without a terminal. The guide for first questions is See a","m":"both"},{"p":"Your usage data","h":"Where the files are","u":"usage-export.html#where-the-files-are","x":"By default the log is in the `usage` folder of your Maple Harness config folder (`~/.config/maple-harness/usage/` on Linux and macOS), one file per UTC day with a name nothing else uses: `maple_harness_proxy_usage_ledger-2026-09-28.jsonl`, `maple_harness_proxy_usage_ledger-2026-09-29.jsonl`, and so on. The folder is cr","m":"both"},{"p":"Your usage data","h":"How long it is kept","u":"usage-export.html#how-long-it-is-kept","x":"`server.usage_retention_days` ( Keep on the Usage data card) is how many days of daily files to keep. Older daily files are deleted when the proxy starts and then once a day, the proxy logs one line saying what it removed, and each cleanup that deletes files is recorded in the audit log (how many, and the folder). `0` ","m":"both"},{"p":"Your usage data","h":"Turn it off","u":"usage-export.html#turn-it-off","x":"Press Turn off on the Usage data card (or on the one-time notice the setup page shows after upgrading), or set `usage_ledger: off` in `config.yaml`. For one run of the proxy, `CCH_USAGE_LEDGER=off` in its environment does the same (and `CCH_USAGE_LEDGER=/some/path` sends that run's log elsewhere). Nothing already writt","m":"both"},{"p":"Your usage data","h":"From the setup page","u":"usage-export.html#from-the-setup-page","x":"The proxy's setup page has, on its Data & storage tab, a Usage data card with everything below without a terminal: whether the log is on and where; how much disk the daily files take, how many there are and the days they cover, with an estimate at the retention limit once there is a whole day of data; Location (Move, U","m":"both"},{"p":"Your usage data","h":"Query it directly with DuckDB","u":"usage-export.html#query-it-directly-with-duckdb","x":"This is the fastest way to ask a real question of your own data — no export step, no code. DuckDB reads JSON Lines natively. Start by creating a typed view named `usage`: `usage schema --format sql` prints a `CREATE OR REPLACE VIEW usage AS ...` statement over every daily file (it finds them the same way `usage export`","m":"both"},{"p":"Your usage data","h":"Named views","u":"usage-export.html#named-views","x":"For the questions most people ask, `--views` adds ready-made views to the `usage` view: The output starts with exactly what `usage schema --format sql` prints without `--views`, then makes a schema named `maple` (`CREATE SCHEMA IF NOT EXISTS maple`) and the views inside it. Nothing outside that schema is created or rep","m":"both"},{"p":"Your usage data","h":"Lots of history? Use the DuckDB file or monthly Parquet","u":"usage-export.html#lots-of-history-use-the-duckdb-file-or-monthly-parquet","x":"A view over the daily files reads one file for every day. That is quick for weeks of data; over months or years, and on a hub that also keeps what the proxies below it send up, it gets slower. Two ways to read far fewer files: Monthly Parquet files. `maple-proxy usage compact` packs each month's closed UTC days into on","m":"both"},{"p":"Your usage data","h":"Several pods? Query Postgres","u":"usage-export.html#several-pods-query-postgres","x":"In central mode ( Kubernetes ) every replica writes to one PostgreSQL, so there are no daily files on any one machine. `--source postgres` prints the same named views over the shared tables, through DuckDB's `postgres` extension: The connection string is read from the variable each time a view is queried; it is never w","m":"both"},{"p":"Your usage data","h":"Cost and savings","u":"usage-export.html#cost-and-savings","x":"Each line carries what the request cost and, as estimates, what the proxy's techniques saved and cost on it: the same figures the setup page's Savings card and `maple-proxy savings` show, per request. The card adds them up; the log keeps them one request at a time, so you can add them up your own way. A sum over a day ","m":"both"},{"p":"Your usage data","h":"The usage record schema","u":"usage-export.html#the-usage-record-schema","x":"Every line of the log, and every row of an export, is one usage record. The schema is versioned; this is version 6 . Later versions may add columns at the end, and never rename, remove, reorder or retype one. Version 2 added the columns from `schema_version` on: the session, the cache-warming flag, a list-price estimat","m":"both"},{"p":"Your usage data","h":"The events log","u":"usage-export.html#the-events-log","x":"Next to the daily usage files the proxy keeps an events log: things that happen that are not requests, one JSON object per line in `events-YYYY-MM-DD.jsonl` (one file per UTC day, in the same folder). It is separate so that counting the usage files' lines still counts requests, and so every existing query and export is","m":"both"},{"p":"Your usage data","h":"Export to CSV or Parquet","u":"usage-export.html#export-to-csv-or-parquet","x":"For handing the data to a tool that cannot read JSON Lines directly: `--since`/`--until` are UTC-day boundaries (`YYYY-MM-DD`); `--until` is exclusive. Without `--path`, it finds the log the same way the running proxy does (`usage_ledger` from your `config.yaml`, the default place when that is unset, and `CCH_USAGE_LED","m":"both"},{"p":"Your usage data","h":"Filter your data","u":"usage-export.html#filter-your-data","x":"`maple-proxy usage query` answers questions about the usage log without DuckDB or an export: totals, or a table grouped by what you choose, for any mix of filters. The filters: Filter Flags Matches Time `--since`, `--until` A UTC day (`YYYY-MM-DD`) or a time (RFC 3339); `--until` is exclusive. Recent `--last` `7d` is t","m":"both"},{"p":"Your usage data","h":"Requests with no person","u":"usage-export.html#requests-with-no-person","x":"Two names stand for a request without a person of its own: `unattributed` (stored `person`: empty, NULL): no person was recorded. The request came before people were set up (or before schema version 4), or with an access token tied to no person. `local` (stored `person`: `local`): a request from the proxy's own machine","m":"both"},{"p":"Your usage data","h":"Keep it tidy: prune","u":"usage-export.html#keep-it-tidy-prune","x":"The proxy already deletes daily files older than `server.usage_retention_days` (see How long it is kept ). To remove more now, by hand: Only rotated, dated files are ever candidates for deletion. A plain, undated file left over from before rotation existed is never touched by `prune` — if you want that gone, remove it ","m":"both"},{"p":"Your usage data","h":"Wire it into Grafana","u":"usage-export.html#wire-it-into-grafana","x":"Point an existing Grafana plugin at your files rather than a bespoke Maple Harness dashboard, so it stays current with however you actually run Grafana: Infinity (maintained by Grafana Labs, in the official plugin catalog) reads CSV and JSON directly, by path or URL, and is the simpler route: Add it as a data source, p","m":"both"},{"p":"What's inside Maple","h":"What's inside Maple","u":"whats-inside.html","x":"Maple Harness is more than a chat window on a model. This page walks through what it does for you behind the scenes: what each part does, why it helps, and where you can see it or change it. Each one is described in plain words first, then with the settings that control it. How they work inside is not described here. N","m":"both"},{"p":"What's inside Maple","h":"In plain words","u":"whats-inside.html#in-plain-words","x":"The agent remembers your project. It keeps notes, builds a map of your code, and can look things up by what they mean. It doesn't have to rediscover your project every session. Long sessions stay affordable. Requests are shaped so your provider can reuse what it has already read, old bulky tool output can be shortened,","m":"both"},{"p":"What's inside Maple","h":"The agent","u":"whats-inside.html#the-agent","x":"","m":"both"},{"p":"What's inside Maple","h":"Project memory and the code map","u":"whats-inside.html#project-memory-and-the-code-map","x":"In plain words. The agent keeps short notes about your project and about you (how you like things done, what was decided), and a map of your project's files and the names defined in them. When you mention a function, it can find it in the map instead of searching the whole folder again. A new session starts already kno","m":"both"},{"p":"What's inside Maple","h":"Search by meaning","u":"whats-inside.html#search-by-meaning","x":"In plain words. Looking something up by its words misses what was said differently: \"the login bug\" is not \"authentication failures after the upgrade\". With search by meaning, the agent's look-ups in the conversation's history, its notes and the code map also find things by what they mean. Words still count: an exact m","m":"both"},{"p":"What's inside Maple","h":"Long conversations","u":"whats-inside.html#long-conversations","x":"In plain words. A long task outgrows what a model can see at once. What leaves the model's view is kept and searchable: Maple keeps every message, puts a record of what happened in its place (requests, files changed, commands and whether they passed, decisions), and the agent can search the old part whenever it needs i","m":"both"},{"p":"What's inside Maple","h":"Stopping when it is stuck","u":"whats-inside.html#stopping-when-it-is-stuck","x":"In plain words. Sometimes an agent keeps trying different fixes for the same failing problem. Maple notices, tells the model to step back, and if it still fails, ends the turn with a summary for you instead of spending more. The technical view. `loop_guard.mode` is `stop` by default (`warn` only notes it; `off` turns i","m":"both"},{"p":"What's inside Maple","h":"Language servers","u":"whats-inside.html#language-servers","x":"In plain words. After each edit, the errors your editor would underline come straight back to the agent, so it fixes its own mistake on the next step instead of finding out when the build fails. The technical view. The agent uses the language servers already on your `PATH` for your project's languages (`harness.lsp: au","m":"both"},{"p":"What's inside Maple","h":"The sandbox and file rules","u":"whats-inside.html#the-sandbox-and-file-rules","x":"In plain words. The agent asks before it writes a file or runs a command, and `deny` rules always win. Its file tools refuse your keys and shell history. With the sandbox on, the commands it runs can change your project but not the rest of your computer, and cannot see where your logins live. The technical view. `/sand","m":"both"},{"p":"What's inside Maple","h":"Remote control from your phone","u":"whats-inside.html#remote-control-from-your-phone","x":"In plain words. Share a session with your phone. You see what the agent is doing, read the exact change before you approve it, answer its questions, and start new sessions in folders you allowed. Notifications say when it needs you, without commands, paths or error text in them. The technical view. `/remote` in a sessi","m":"both"},{"p":"What's inside Maple","h":"The proxy","u":"whats-inside.html#the-proxy","x":"","m":"both"},{"p":"What's inside Maple","h":"Auto routing","u":"whats-inside.html#auto-routing","x":"In plain words. Ask for the model `auto` and the proxy picks one for each request: a stronger model for planning and deciding, an everyday one for work already under way, a light one for short side jobs. You choose which model each of those is, or keep the defaults from your provider's own ranking. The technical view. ","m":"both"},{"p":"What's inside Maple","h":"Requests shaped for your provider's cache","u":"whats-inside.html#requests-shaped-for-your-providers-cache","x":"In plain words. A long conversation sends the same beginning on every turn. Providers that cache charge less for the part they have already seen, but only if that beginning stays exactly the same. Maple keeps it the same from turn to turn, and shows you how often the cache was used and why it missed. The technical view","m":"both"},{"p":"What's inside Maple","h":"The response cache","u":"whats-inside.html#the-response-cache","x":"In plain words. Some tools send exactly the same request again and want the same answer. With the response cache on, the proxy answers such a repeat itself, without asking the provider again. Off by default: a reused answer is the same text as before, not a fresh one. The technical view. `response_cache`, `response_cac","m":"both"},{"p":"What's inside Maple","h":"Fallbacks","u":"whats-inside.html#fallbacks","x":"In plain words. When an account is out of quota, rate limited or down, the request goes to the next account or model you chose, mid-task, with nothing for you to do. Nothing falls back unless you add a rule. The technical view. `model_fallbacks` and `provider_fallbacks`, on Models & routing , Proxy settings , Fallbacks","m":"both"},{"p":"What's inside Maple","h":"Provider health","u":"whats-inside.html#provider-health","x":"In plain words. The proxy watches how each provider and model answers your real requests, and marks one that is failing far more than usual. It sends no requests of its own to check. If you choose, a struggling model is tried after your other fallbacks. The technical view. The Health tab and `maple-proxy health`; `heal","m":"both"},{"p":"What's inside Maple","h":"Budgets and alerts","u":"whats-inside.html#budgets-and-alerts","x":"In plain words. Cap what an account may use a day (requests or AI units), or give an Anthropic account a monthly allowance in dollars. You are warned at 80% and 100%; at the limit, requests move to a fallback or stop, as you choose. Every dollar figure Maple counts itself is labelled estimated. The technical view. `bud","m":"both"},{"p":"What's inside Maple","h":"Teams and hubs","u":"whats-inside.html#teams-and-hubs","x":"In plain words. One proxy can serve several people, each with a token of their own, their own accounts, models and budget. Proxies can be chained: a laptop's proxy uses a team hub, which uses a company hub; people are set up once at the top and everyone's usage arrives there. The technical view. People & teams and Hubs","m":"both"},{"p":"What's inside Maple","h":"Seeing everything","u":"whats-inside.html#seeing-everything","x":"In plain words. Every request leaves one line of counts and labels in a log on your own disk: never a prompt or an answer. The setup page draws it; you can ask it questions in a terminal or in DuckDB, and feed Prometheus, OpenTelemetry or a security tool. The technical view. Usage , Costs & budgets , Cache and Health o","m":"both"},{"p":"What's inside Maple","h":"One page to set it all up","u":"whats-inside.html#one-page-to-set-it-all-up","x":"In plain words. The proxy's setup page is the control panel for all of the above. What's configured on its first tab says what is on, what is off and what needs a look; Find a setting takes you to any setting by a word; Set up more starts the team features only when you want them. The technical view. See The setup page","m":"both"},{"p":"Which setup do I need?","h":"Which setup do I need?","u":"which-setup.html","x":"Maple runs the same way on one laptop and in a company: a proxy holds the accounts and talks to the models, and your tools talk to a proxy. The setups below differ only in how many proxies there are and how they are joined. Start with the first one that fits, and move to another later: nothing you set up is lost (see H","m":"both"},{"p":"Which setup do I need?","h":"In plain words","u":"which-setup.html#in-plain-words","x":"Answer three questions: Is it just you? Then use one laptop . Install it, sign in, work. Nothing on this page is needed, and nothing here shows up until you set it up. Does your company or team already run a Maple hub? Then join your laptop's proxy to it : sign in with your company account (about a minute), or paste a ","m":"both"},{"p":"Which setup do I need?","h":"At a glance","u":"which-setup.html#at-a-glance","x":"Setup Pick this if What you run Step by step 1. One laptop You use Maple on your own `maple-harness` (it starts `maple-proxy` for you) Your first session 2. Laptop joined to a hub Your team runs a hub and you want its models, budget and accounts Your own proxy, signed in to the hub Join your laptop to a team hub 3. A t","m":"both"},{"p":"Which setup do I need?","h":"Words used on these pages","u":"which-setup.html#words-used-on-these-pages","x":"Word What it means Proxy `maple-proxy`, the program that holds sign-ins and keys and talks to the models. Every Maple install has one Hub A proxy that other machines or other proxies use. Nothing extra is installed: a hub is a proxy that listens on the network and asks every caller for a token Personal token An access ","m":"both"},{"p":"Which setup do I need?","h":"1. One laptop","u":"which-setup.html#1-one-laptop","x":"One laptop: your tools, your proxy, your accounts. Pick this if you use Maple on your own. This is the setup everyone starts with, and most people stay with. What you run. `maple-harness`. It starts `maple-proxy` on the same machine when nothing is running, and you sign in to your accounts on the proxy's setup page (`h","m":"both"},{"p":"Which setup do I need?","h":"2. Your laptop's proxy joined to a team hub","u":"which-setup.html#2-your-laptops-proxy-joined-to-a-team-hub","x":"Your tools keep using your own proxy; your proxy uses the hub, as you. Pick this if your team or company runs a Maple hub and you want its models, under your own name, budget and model list. What you run. Nothing new. Your own proxy joins the hub, and Maple Harness keeps talking to your own proxy, as on one laptop. Eve","m":"both"},{"p":"Which setup do I need?","h":"3. A team hub for many people","u":"which-setup.html#3-a-team-hub-for-many-people","x":"One hub: a token per person, the accounts on one machine. Pick this if several people should share accounts, and you want to see who used what, give each person or team their own accounts, or cap what each can spend. What you run. One `maple-proxy` on a machine your team can reach (a server, a container , or one replic","m":"both"},{"p":"Which setup do I need?","h":"4. Hubs chained to a company hub","u":"which-setup.html#4-hubs-chained-to-a-company-hub","x":"A company hub, a team hub joined with a chain token, and a laptop signed in to the team hub. Pick this if there is more than one hub: a hub per team or per office, with people defined once and every hub's usage in one place. What you run. A proxy per team, each joined to the hub above it with a chain token made by that","m":"both"},{"p":"Which setup do I need?","h":"5. Several replicas behind a load balancer","u":"which-setup.html#5-several-replicas-behind-a-load-balancer","x":"Several replicas: PostgreSQL for what lasts, Redis for what is shared and short-lived. Pick this if a hub (setup 3 or 4) must keep answering through a restart, an upgrade or a lost machine. One replica is enough for most teams: the proxy is light and restarts in seconds. What you run. Copies of one proxy behind a load ","m":"both"},{"p":"Which setup do I need?","h":"6. CI jobs and containers","u":"which-setup.html#6-ci-jobs-and-containers","x":"Pick this if Maple Harness runs where no proxy of your own can run, or where the proxy runs as another user. There are two ways, and they are different things: Use a hub directly with a token (advanced) Trust a proxy on the same machine that cannot prove it is yours When A CI job or container that should use a team hub","m":"both"},{"p":"Which setup do I need?","h":"How to move between setups","u":"which-setup.html#how-to-move-between-setups","x":"From To What to do What is kept One laptop Joined to a hub Sign in (setup 2) Everything: your accounts, settings and usage stay on your proxy Joined to a hub One laptop Disconnect on Hubs & chaining , or `maple-proxy chain disconnect` (the token is revoked at the hub too); for a pasted token, delete the hub's provider ","m":"both"},{"p":"Which setup do I need?","h":"What is not verified","u":"which-setup.html#what-is-not-verified","x":"The setups on this page were run on one Linux machine: several proxies on loopback with stand-in model services, a stand-in identity provider through a TLS front, and the replicas with a local PostgreSQL and a stand-in Redis. Not yet run: real Google, Microsoft Entra ID, Okta or GitHub accounts, chains over HTTPS betwe","m":"both"},{"p":"Which setup do I need?","h":"Related","u":"which-setup.html#related","x":"Share Maple with a team : the worked team and chain examples, with screenshots. Teams , Chain proxies into hubs , Single sign-on and How developers sign in : every key and every step. Run the proxy in Docker and on Kubernetes : one container, or several replicas. Security : what each setup protects, in short.","m":"both"},{"p":"Your data: where it lives and how to query it","h":"Your data: where it lives and how to query it","u":"your-data.html","x":"Every request that goes through the proxy leaves one usage record: counts and labels, never content. This page is the map: what is recorded, the three places that data can live, which one to use, how it moves between them, and what that costs to run. The details are on their own pages: Query your usage is the cookbook,","m":"both"},{"p":"Your data: where it lives and how to query it","h":"In plain words","u":"your-data.html#in-plain-words","x":"What it is. Where your usage history lives: daily files on this machine (the default), PostgreSQL for a team with several replicas, and an optional lake for years of history. What to do alone on one machine. Nothing: the files are already there. The setup page's Data & storage tab says where, how much and for how long.","m":"both"},{"p":"Your data: where it lives and how to query it","h":"What is recorded, and what never is","u":"your-data.html#what-is-recorded-and-what-never-is","x":"One record per request, with: what was asked for and what answered: the model the client asked for, the provider and model that answered, and whether a fallback stepped in; how big it was: input, output and cached tokens, and the AI units the provider reported; how long it took, and how it ended: fine, failed (with a f","m":"both"},{"p":"Your data: where it lives and how to query it","h":"The three places your data can live","u":"your-data.html#the-three-places-your-data-can-live","x":"","m":"both"},{"p":"Your data: where it lives and how to query it","h":"The daily files: the default","u":"your-data.html#the-daily-files-the-default","x":"The proxy writes one file per UTC day into the `usage` folder of its config folder (`~/.config/maple-harness/usage/` on Linux and macOS), and keeps 90 days unless you change `server.usage_retention_days`. This is how a single user, or a single container, keeps its data, and it is all most people need. `maple-proxy usag","m":"both"},{"p":"Your data: where it lives and how to query it","h":"PostgreSQL: teams and several pods","u":"your-data.html#postgresql-teams-and-several-pods","x":"When several copies of one proxy run behind a load balancer ( central mode ), each copy writes its usage into a shared PostgreSQL instead, so every copy, the setup page and `usage query` see the whole team's traffic. PostgreSQL is the live database: it holds the recent days (`storage.retention_days`, by default the sam","m":"both"},{"p":"Your data: where it lives and how to query it","h":"DuckLake: history and analysis, for both","u":"your-data.html#ducklake-history-and-analysis-for-both","x":"A DuckLake keeps your usage as Parquet data files plus a small catalog that lists them, and it only ever adds: rows the daily files or PostgreSQL delete later stay in the lake. It is where history longer than your retention lives, and the fastest thing to query over months or years. On one machine its catalog is a file","m":"both"},{"p":"Your data: where it lives and how to query it","h":"On the setup page","u":"your-data.html#on-the-setup-page","x":"The Data & storage tab says where this proxy keeps its data, how much there is and how long it is kept, with queries to copy into DuckDB, and holds the Usage data card: the location, Keep , Analytics files , Export and Remove old days . Its charts and the other tabs read the daily files (in central mode, PostgreSQL); t","m":"both"},{"p":"Your data: where it lives and how to query it","h":"Which one should I use?","u":"your-data.html#which-one-should-i-use","x":"You are Live data History beyond retention Query it with One person, one machine The daily files (nothing to do) Raise `usage_retention_days`, or add a local lake `usage query`, the setup page, DuckDB over the files One person who keeps a lot of history The daily files A local lake (`usage_analytics: lake`), or the Duc","m":"both"},{"p":"Your data: where it lives and how to query it","h":"How data moves","u":"your-data.html#how-data-moves","x":"Usage goes up a chain of hubs, and from the live store into the lake. In batches, not per request. A request is written to the daily file or to PostgreSQL as it ends. The lake is filled by a job: hourly for a team (the Kubernetes sample's schedule), and once a day on one machine when Analytics files is `lake` (or whene","m":"both"},{"p":"Your data: where it lives and how to query it","h":"What it costs to run","u":"your-data.html#what-it-costs-to-run","x":"There are no new services to pay for on one machine: the daily files, the monthly Parquet files, the DuckDB file and a local lake are files on your disk, made by the proxy or by your own `duckdb` program. What they take: Disk. The daily files take about 0.25 to 0.65 KB a request (an estimate from sample lines; `usage s","m":"both"},{"p":"Your data: where it lives and how to query it","h":"Your proxy's identity key","u":"your-data.html#your-proxys-identity-key","x":"Since 2.2 your proxy also keeps `local-identity.key` in your settings folder (`~/.config/maple-harness/` on Linux and macOS, `%APPDATA%\\maple-harness\\` on Windows). It is a random key, made by the proxy on its first start, readable and writable only by you (mode `0600`); the proxy replaces it if anyone else could read ","m":"both"},{"p":"Your data: where it lives and how to query it","h":"Where to go next","u":"your-data.html#where-to-go-next","x":"Query your usage : tested recipes for `usage query`, DuckDB over the files, the DuckDB file, the lake and PostgreSQL, and exports to spreadsheets. The Maple data lake : set up a lake on one machine or for a team, maintenance, time travel, backups. See and filter your usage : the filters, the ready-made views, the event","m":"both"}];
